= Quantum field theory
{wiki}

Quantum field theory combines <special relativity> with <quantum theory> by treating fields as operator-valued distributions and particles as field excitations.

= Integrable quantum field theory
{parent=Quantum field theory}

A <quantum field theory> with enough mutually compatible conserved quantities to constrain its dynamics exactly. In massive relativistic theories in one spatial dimension, suitable higher-spin conserved charges imply elastic <factorized scattering>: the set of incoming <rapidities> is preserved and multiparticle amplitudes are assembled from two-particle <S-matrices>.

= Factorized scattering
{parent=Integrable quantum field theory}

Multiparticle <quantum scattering> is factorized when it decomposes into two-body scatterings without particle production or a new irreducible many-body amplitude. The independence of the answer from the order of exchanges imposes the <Yang-Baxter equation>. Classical <pairwise additivity of soliton shifts> is a corresponding property of integrable <soliton> collisions.

= O(N)-invariant S-matrix
{parent=Factorized scattering}
{c}
{title2=$S=S_2I+S_3P+S_1K$}

For particles in the <vector representation> of the <orthogonal group>, the two-body <S-matrix> is a linear combination of the identity $I$, permutation $P$ and trace contraction $K$: $S=S_2I+S_3P+S_1K$. Here $K(e_i\otimes e_j)=\delta_{ij}\sum_a e_a\otimes e_a$, $P^2=I$, $PK=KP=K$ and $K^2=NK$. The <orthogonal-invariant scattering channels> diagonalize these three operators simultaneously.

= Orthogonal-invariant scattering channels
{parent=O(N)-invariant S-matrix}
{title2=$s_0=NS_1+S_2+S_3,\quad s_\pm=S_2\pm S_3$}

The two-vector <tensor product> splits into a trace singlet, symmetric traceless tensors and antisymmetric tensors. For an <O(N)-invariant S-matrix>, the corresponding <eigenvalues> are $s_0=NS_1+S_2+S_3$, $s_+=S_2+S_3$ and $s_-=S_2-S_3$. For $N\ge2$, analytic <unitarity> requires $s_a(\theta)s_a(-\theta)=1$. With <Hermitian analyticity of a two-particle S-matrix>, each channel has unit modulus on the real <rapidity> axis.

= Yang-Baxter equation
{parent=Factorized scattering}
{c}
{wiki=Yang–Baxter_equation}
{title2=$R_{12}(u)R_{13}(u+v)R_{23}(v)=R_{23}(v)R_{13}(u+v)R_{12}(u)$}

For a two-body operator $R(u)$ acting on a <tensor product> of particle species spaces, the spectral equation is $R_{12}(u)R_{13}(u+v)R_{23}(v)=R_{23}(v)R_{13}(u+v)R_{12}(u)$. It guarantees agreement between the two ways to reorder three particles in <factorized scattering>. A <Yang–Baxter operator> is a related braid-form operator; multiplying by the permutation operator converts between the braided and unbraided conventions.

= Faddeev-Zamolodchikov algebra
{parent=Factorized scattering}
{c}
{title2=$A_i(\theta_1)A_j(\theta_2)=S_{ij}^{kl}(\theta_{12})A_l(\theta_2)A_k(\theta_1)$}

= Zamolodchikov-Faddeev algebra
{c}
{synonym}

Particle operators obey a rapidity-dependent exchange rule $A_i(\theta_1)A_j(\theta_2)=S_{ij}^{kl}(\theta_1-\theta_2)A_l(\theta_2)A_k(\theta_1)$, with repeated species indices summed. A consistent <associative algebra> must give the same coefficients when a triple product is brought to decreasing or increasing <rapidity> order by either sequence of adjacent exchanges. This is the <Faddeev-Zamolodchikov associativity constraint>. Rapidity ordering here is distinct from the creation/annihilation convention of ordinary <normal ordering>.

= Faddeev-Zamolodchikov associativity constraint
{parent=Faddeev-Zamolodchikov algebra}
{c}

Starting with three ordered <rapidities>, the adjacent-exchange sequences $121$ and $212$ must give identical coefficients for every final species word in the <Faddeev-Zamolodchikov algebra>. The exchange parameters are $\theta$, $\theta+\theta'$ and $\theta'$ because rapidity differences add. These coefficient identities express the spectral <Yang-Baxter equation>. One scalar identity is necessary but generally does not exhaust all the constraints.

= Local quantum field operator
{parent=Quantum field theory}
{title2=$\mathcal O(x)$}

A local quantum field operator is supported at a spacetime point, with operator-valued distributions understood by smearing. Products at the same point generally require <renormalization>; these give <composite operators>. This is distinct from the topos-theoretic meaning of <local operator>.

= Composite operator
{parent=Local quantum field operator}
{title2=$\mathcal O_R(x)$}

= Composite local operator
{synonym}

A composite operator is a renormalized local product of quantum fields and their derivatives. Operators with compatible quantum numbers can undergo <composite operator mixing>, and products at coincident insertion points can need <contact terms in a correlation function>.

= Contact term in a correlation function
{parent=Composite operator}

= Contact terms in a correlation function
{synonym}

A contact term is a distribution supported where insertion points coincide. Its <Fourier transform> is local in momentum, typically polynomial. Renormalizing an operator product can require additive contact counterterms even if each operator has zero <anomalous dimension>.

= Composite operator mixing
{parent=Composite operator}
{title2=$\mathcal O_B=Z\mathcal O_R$}

Renormalization can relate bare operators to a matrix of renormalized operators, $\mathcal O_{i,B}=Z_{ij}\mathcal O_{j,R}$. The corresponding <anomalous dimension> matrix acts on each insertion slot in the <Callan-Symanzik equation>.

= Soft graviton theorem
{c}
{parent=Quantum field theory}
{wiki}

The soft graviton theorem factorizes an amplitude with one low-energy graviton into the hard amplitude times a universal sum over external momenta. Gauge invariance of the soft factor requires energy-momentum conservation and universal gravitational coupling.

= Weinberg soft theorem
{c}
{parent=Soft graviton theorem}

The Weinberg soft theorem constrains interactions of a massless particle by requiring its soft-emission amplitude to be invariant under shifts of an unphysical polarization. For helicity one it gives charge conservation, for helicity two it gives universal gravitational coupling, and for higher helicity it obstructs generic long-range interactions.

= Effective field theory
{title2=EFT}
{parent=Quantum field theory}
{wiki}

An effective field theory describes phenomena below a chosen energy scale by retaining the relevant light fields and organizing local interactions by increasing suppression at that scale.

= Wilson coefficient
{c}
{parent=Effective field theory}

A Wilson coefficient weights a local operator in an <effective field theory>. For an operator of <mass dimension> $d$ in four spacetime dimensions, a dimensionless coefficient is conventionally accompanied by $\Lambda^{4-d}$. The coefficient encodes short-distance dynamics and can be a matrix in flavour space.

= Ultraviolet completion
{parent=Effective field theory}

An ultraviolet completion specifies a theory valid at higher energies whose low-energy limit gives an <effective field theory>. For large-field <cosmic inflation>, it may also need to control the potential over a large field range, which is distinct from the energy scale of a process.

= Planck-suppressed corrections to inflaton potentials
{c}
{parent=Effective field theory}
{title2=$\Delta V=\sum_{p>4}c_p\phi^p/M_{\rm Pl}^{p-4}$}

In an <effective field theory>, higher-power terms in an <inflaton> <scalar potential> can be important when $\phi/M_{\rm Pl}$ is large, even if $V/M_{\rm Pl}^4$ is small. An assumed simple <monomial inflation potential> over such a field range needs a <symmetry> or <ultraviolet completion> controlling these operators and <radiative corrections>. A large field value alone does not establish Planckian <energy density> or invalidate every possible completion.

= Local operator
{disambiguate=physics}
{parent=Effective field theory}

= Local field operator
{synonym}

A local field operator is built from fields and finitely many of their derivatives at one spacetime point. Its integral can deform an action, with the operator's scaling dimension determining the engineering dimension of the associated coupling.

= Heavy-field decoupling
{parent=Effective field theory}

Heavy-field decoupling says that a field of mass $M$ affects processes with external momenta $p\ll M$ through renormalizations of existing low-energy couplings and local higher-dimensional operators suppressed by powers of $p/M$.

= Ultraviolet cutoff
{title2=$\Lambda$}
{parent=Effective field theory}
{wiki}

An ultraviolet cutoff removes modes above a chosen energy or momentum scale. An <effective field theory> encodes the influence of those omitted short-distance modes in its couplings.

= Infrared divergence
{parent=Effective field theory}
{wiki}

An infrared divergence is a divergence caused by contributions from arbitrarily low energies or long wavelengths.

= Quadratic Lagrangian
{parent=Effective field theory}

A quadratic Lagrangian contains terms of second order in field fluctuations and determines their linearized propagators and dispersion relations.

= Higgs mode
{c}
{parent=Quadratic Lagrangian}
{wiki}

= Amplitude mode
{synonym}

A Higgs mode is a fluctuation in the magnitude of a complex or multicomponent <order parameter>, as distinct from a phase or orientational fluctuation.

= Integrating out a field
{parent=Effective field theory}

Integrating out a field means performing its path integral while retaining the other fields as backgrounds. The result is generally a nonlocal <quantum effective action>; for a field much heavier than all external momenta, a derivative expansion turns it into local effective interactions.

= Derivative expansion
{parent=Effective field theory}
{wiki}

A derivative expansion expresses a nonlocal effective interaction as a series of local operators with successively more derivatives. For a field of mass $M$, each additional pair of derivatives is typically suppressed by $M^{-2}$.

= Four-fermion interaction
{parent=Effective field theory}
{wiki}

A four-fermion interaction is a local operator containing four fermion fields. In four spacetime dimensions it has mass dimension six, so its coefficient has mass dimension minus two; writing it as the square of a current multiplied by a coefficient inside the square gives that coefficient mass dimension minus one.

= Naive factorization of a nonleptonic meson decay
{parent=Four-fermion interaction}

Approximate a hadronic <matrix element> of a product of two currents by a product of separate current <matrix elements>. For a decay to two <mesons>, one factor can create a <pion> from the vacuum while the other gives a <pseudoscalar-to-pseudoscalar form factor>. Nonfactorizable <quantum chromodynamics> interactions are omitted; tree-level weak vertices do not alone justify this approximation.

= Fermi interaction
{c}
{parent=Four-fermion interaction}
{wiki}

The Fermi interaction is the low-energy four-fermion approximation to the <weak charged current>. Integrating out a <W boson> gives its coefficient $G_F/\sqrt2=g^2/(8M_W^2)$ at tree level.

= Massless neutrino-electron contact cross-section
{title2=$\sigma_\nu=G_F^2s(c_V^2+c_Vc_A+c_A^2)/(3\pi)$}
{parent=Fermi interaction}

For a left-handed incoming neutrino, unpolarized electrons, and electron vector/axial couplings $c_V,c_A$, the <four-fermion interaction> gives $\overline{|\mathcal M|^2}=4G_F^2[(c_V+c_A)^2s^2+(c_V-c_A)^2u^2]$. Integrating massless <two-body Lorentz-invariant phase space> yields $\sigma=G_F^2s(c_V^2+c_Vc_A+c_A^2)/(3\pi)$. The electron spin average is included, while no sterile neutrino helicity is averaged in.

= Neutrino-antineutrino interchange of chiral scattering weights
{parent=Massless neutrino-electron contact cross-section}

Crossing a massless neutrino to an antineutrino reverses the antisymmetric part of its spin tensor. The $s^2$ and $u^2$ weights exchange, giving $\sigma_{\bar\nu}=G_F^2s[(c_V-c_A)^2+(c_V+c_A)^2/3]/(4\pi)$ for the same electron couplings. This is a low-energy contact statement rather than a replacement for a full weak-boson propagator.

= Charged-current shift in neutrino-electron scattering
{title2=$(c_V,c_A)\mapsto(c_V+1,c_A+1)$}
{parent=Massless neutrino-electron contact cross-section}

Electron-neutrino scattering has a charged-current contribution absent for muon neutrinos. <Fierz rearrangement> combined with the external <fermionic sign> adds a left-electron current: $c_V\to c_V+1$, $c_A\to c_A+1$. The right-electron coupling stays unchanged; the left-electron coupling increases by two.

= Fierz rearrangement
{c}
{parent=Four-fermion interaction}
{wiki=Fierz_identity}

A Fierz rearrangement rewrites a product of fermion bilinears with one spinor pairing as a sum of products with another pairing. For left-handed vector currents, it lets a charged-current four-fermion operator be compared directly with a neutral-current operator.

= Fermi constant
{title2=$G_F$}
{c}
{parent=Four-fermion interaction}
{wiki}

The Fermi constant is the coefficient conventionally used for the low-energy charged-current weak interaction. Tree-level exchange of a $W$ boson gives $G_F/\sqrt2=g^2/(8M_W^2)$.

= Mass gap
{title2=$\Delta$}
{parent=Quantum field theory}
{wiki}

A quantum field theory has a mass gap when the vacuum energy is separated from the rest of the spectrum by a strictly positive energy. Correlation functions then typically decay exponentially at long distance.

= Spontaneous symmetry breaking
{title2=SSB}
{parent=Quantum field theory}
{wiki}

Spontaneous symmetry breaking occurs when the action and equations have a symmetry but a chosen <ground state> is invariant only under a proper subgroup. Acting with the broken symmetry generates a degenerate <vacuum manifold>.

= Vacuum manifold
{parent=Spontaneous symmetry breaking}

The vacuum manifold is the space of field configurations that minimize the energy. If a group $G$ acts transitively and a chosen vacuum has stabilizer $H$, its connected component is the homogeneous space $G/H$.

= Classical vacuum
{parent=Vacuum manifold}
{title2=$V(\phi_v)=\min V$}

= Classical vacua
{synonym}

A spatially homogeneous field value minimizing the potential energy. For a <finite-energy field configuration> on an infinite domain, the potential is normally shifted so the relevant vacuum energy density is zero. Disconnected components of the <vacuum manifold> allow a <scalar-field kink> to connect distinct vacuum values.

= Scalar-field vacuum
{parent=Vacuum manifold}

= Scalar-field vacua
{synonym}

A scalar-field vacuum is a constant field at a minimum of its <potential energy>, with the minimum shifted to zero when discussing finite-energy <classical field-theory solitons>. Distinct isolated vacua allow a <scalar-field kink> to approach different values at the two ends of space.

= Vacuum expectation value
{title2=$\langle\phi\rangle$}
{parent=Spontaneous symmetry breaking}
{wiki}

A vacuum expectation value is the expectation of a field or operator in the vacuum state. A nonzero value that is not invariant under the full symmetry group can select a point of the <vacuum manifold> and spontaneously break the symmetry.

= Scalar mass matrix
{title2=$M^2_{ij}=\partial_i\partial_jV$}
{parent=Spontaneous symmetry breaking}

= Scalar squared-mass matrix
{synonym}

The scalar mass matrix is the <Hessian matrix> of the <scalar potential> at a vacuum. Its eigenvalues are the tree-level squared masses; broken symmetry directions are zero eigenvectors before a <Higgs mechanism> or explicit symmetry breaking removes the corresponding massless modes.

= Goldstone theorem
{c}
{parent=Spontaneous symmetry breaking}
{wiki}

= Goldstone's theorem
{c}
{synonym}

In a relativistic quantum field theory, each spontaneously broken generator of a continuous internal symmetry produces a massless scalar excitation. For $G\to H$, the standard setting therefore has $\dim G-\dim H$ Goldstone bosons.

= Radial and angular modes of an orthogonally invariant scalar potential
{parent=Goldstone theorem}
{title2=$m_\eta^2=2\lambda v^2$}

For $V=m^2\phi^2/2+\lambda(\phi^2)^2/4$, $m^2<0$, the <vacuum manifold> is a sphere of radius $v=\sqrt{-m^2/\lambda}$. At a chosen vacuum the <Hessian matrix> is $2\lambda v^2nn^T$, where $n$ is its unit direction. The radial scalar has mass squared $2\lambda v^2$; the $n-1$ angular modes are physical <Goldstone bosons> when the symmetry is global.

= Pseudo-Goldstone boson
{parent=Goldstone theorem}
{wiki=Pseudo-Goldstone_boson}

A pseudo-Goldstone boson is a light mode produced when a spontaneously broken continuous symmetry is also weakly broken explicitly. Its mass vanishes with the explicit symmetry-breaking parameters.

= Nonlinear sigma model
{parent=Spontaneous symmetry breaking}
{wiki}

A nonlinear sigma model has fields valued in a curved target space such as a <vacuum manifold> $G/H$. Its leading action is quadratic in spacetime derivatives but nonlinear in any unconstrained coordinates used for the target.

= O(N) nonlinear sigma model
{c}
{parent=Nonlinear sigma model}

The $O(N)$ nonlinear sigma model has an $N$-component field constrained to a sphere, commonly $\mathbf n^2=N$ or $1$. An auxiliary Lagrange-multiplier field turns the constraint into a self-consistent mass gap in the <large-N expansion>.

= O3 nonlinear sigma model
{c}
{parent=O(N) nonlinear sigma model}
{title2=$O(3)$ nonlinear sigma model}

= CP1 nonlinear sigma model
{c}
{synonym}

In two spatial dimensions, a unit-vector field $\mathbf n:\mathbb R^2\to S^2$ has static energy $E=\int|\nabla\mathbf n|^2d^2x/2$. The sphere is also the <complex projective line>, so its <stereographic projection> gives the equivalent complex field description. A constant value at spatial infinity compactifies a regular configuration to a map $S^2\to S^2$, whose <degree of a map between oriented manifolds> is its <topological charge>.

= Sigma-model lump
{parent=O3 nonlinear sigma model}
{title2=$E=4\pi|N|$}

A sigma-model lump is a finite-energy <classical field-theory soliton> of the two-dimensional <O3 nonlinear sigma model>. With an orientation chosen so holomorphic maps have positive charge, the <Bogomolny bound> $E\geq4\pi|N|$ is saturated by <rational maps> of degree $N>0$ in a <stereographic projection> coordinate, and by antiholomorphic maps for negative charge. The field remains smooth at coordinate poles, which are points at the other pole of the target sphere. Scale invariance permits lumps of arbitrary size; topology alone does not prevent concentration to a singular limiting configuration.

= Degree charge of an O3 sigma-model lump
{parent=Sigma-model lump}
{title2=$Q=\frac1{4\pi}\int\mathbf n\cdot(\partial_1\mathbf n\times\partial_2\mathbf n)\,d^2x$}

A unit-vector field $\mathbf n:\mathbb R^2\to S^2$ approaching a fixed value at infinity extends to $S^2\to S^2$. Its <topological charge> is the <topological degree> $Q=(4\pi)^{-1}\int\mathbf n\cdot(\partial_1\mathbf n\times\partial_2\mathbf n)\,d^2x$. This normalized target area pullback counts the number of oriented coverings.

= Bogomolny degree bound for the O3 sigma model
{c}
{parent=Degree charge of an O3 sigma-model lump}
{title2=$E\geq4\pi|Q|$}

For $E=\tfrac12\int(|\partial_1\mathbf n|^2+|\partial_2\mathbf n|^2)$, completing the square gives $E=\tfrac12\int|\partial_1\mathbf n\pm\mathbf n\times\partial_2\mathbf n|^2\pm4\pi Q$, hence $E\geq4\pi|Q|$. Saturation yields the <Bogomolny equations> of the <sigma-model lump>. With the oriented <stereographic projection> $\mathbf n=(2\operatorname{Re}w,2\operatorname{Im}w,1-|w|^2)/(1+|w|^2)$, holomorphic rational maps have positive degree and saturate the bound; antiholomorphic maps have negative degree.

= Discrete symmetry in quantum field theory
{parent=Quantum field theory}

= CP symmetry
{c}
{parent=Discrete symmetry in quantum field theory}
{title2=$CP$}

The combined operation of <charge conjugation> and <parity symmetry in quantum field theory>. A <weak interaction> can preserve this symmetry while violating each constituent separately.

= Z2 symmetry
{title2=$\mathbb Z_2$ symmetry}
{c}
{parent=Discrete symmetry in quantum field theory}

A Z2 symmetry is generated by an operation whose square is the identity. For a real scalar field, the sign symmetry $\phi\mapsto-\phi$ forbids every odd power of $\phi$.

= Parity symmetry in quantum field theory
{parent=Discrete symmetry in quantum field theory}

Parity symmetry means invariance under <spatial reflection>, together with the intrinsic transformation rules of the fields.

= Parity-invariant vacuum
{parent=Parity symmetry in quantum field theory}

A parity-invariant vacuum satisfies $P|0\rangle=|0\rangle$ up to an irrelevant phase. Vacuum correlation functions then transform according to the product of the intrinsic parities of their insertions.

= Parity-odd scalar interaction
{parent=Parity symmetry in quantum field theory}

A parity-odd scalar interaction is rotationally invariant but changes sign under <spatial reflection>. It must contain a <pseudoscalar> contraction, such as a <Levi-Civita symbol> contracted with three spatial gradients.

= Charge conjugation
{title2=$C$}
{parent=Discrete symmetry in quantum field theory}
{wiki}

Charge conjugation exchanges particles with antiparticles. For a matrix-valued gauge potential it acts as $A_\mu\mapsto-A_\mu^T$.

= Charge conjugation of a non-Abelian gauge field
{parent=Charge conjugation}
{title2=$\mathcal A_\mu\mapsto-\mathcal A_\mu^T$}

Write a matrix-valued <gauge field> $\mathcal A_\mu=A_\mu^aT^a$ in the <fundamental representation>. <Charge conjugation> sends it to $-\mathcal A_\mu^T$ because reordering the fermions transposes both the spinor and internal matrices. The identity $[A^T,B^T]=-[A,B]^T$ then sends the <gauge field strength> to $-\mathcal F_{\mu\nu}^T$. This distinction prevents treating all color components as individually C-odd.

= Violation of charge conjugation
{parent=Charge conjugation}

= C violation
{synonym}
{c}

A theory violates <charge conjugation> when conjugating particles to antiparticles changes a physical interaction or its transition probabilities. This is different from <CP violation>: an interaction can violate <charge conjugation> and <parity symmetry in quantum field theory> separately while preserving their combination.

= Charge-conjugation matrix
{parent=Charge conjugation}
{title2=$C^{-1}\gamma^\mu C=-(\gamma^\mu)^T$}

For a <Dirac field>, the charge-conjugation matrix implements $\psi^c=C\bar\psi^T$. In the standard <gamma matrix> representation one can take $C=i\gamma^2\gamma^0$. An overall phase is conventional. The transpose intertwining relation is different from the entrywise <complex conjugation> involved in an <antiunitary operator>.

= Intrinsic charge-conjugation phase
{parent=Charge conjugation}
{title2=$\eta_C$}

The phase in a field transformation $\hat C V^\mu\hat C^{-1}=\eta_C V^{\mu\dagger}$. For a real vector <relativistic quantum field>, <Hermitian conjugation> restricts this phase to $\pm1$.

= Charge conjugation of fermion bilinears
{parent=Charge conjugation}

For <normal ordering> of a <fermion bilinear>, <charge conjugation> sends $\bar\psi\Gamma\psi$ to $\bar\psi(C^{-1}\Gamma C)^T\psi$. In particular the <Dirac current> is odd and the <axial current> is even under <charge conjugation>.

= Charge conjugation of a Dirac field
{parent=Charge conjugation}
{title2=$\psi^c=C\bar\psi^T$}

= Operator conjugation of a chiral field component
{title2=$\hat C\psi_L\hat C^{-1}=P_L\psi^c=(\psi_R)^c$}
{parent=Charge conjugation of a Dirac field}

The unitary charge-conjugation operator acts on field operators and commutes with numerical <chiral projectors>, so $\hat C(P_L\psi)\hat C^{-1}=P_L\psi^c$. This component is left-chiral. The algebraic conjugate of an already projected spinor instead satisfies $(P_L\psi)^c=P_R\psi^c$, because its adjoint carries the opposite projector. Keeping the two operations separate avoids a false chirality conclusion.

= Transpose symmetry of a charge-conjugation matrix
{title2=$C^T=\pm C$}
{parent=Charge conjugation of a Dirac field}

If $C\gamma^{\mu T}C^{-1}=-\gamma^\mu$, transposition shows that $C^TC^{-1}$ commutes with every <gamma matrix>. Irreducibility of the complex Dirac representation and the <Schur lemma> make it scalar. Transposing $C^T=zC$ again yields $z^2=1$, so $C^T=\pm C$.

= Unitarity of the charge-conjugation matrix
{parent=Charge conjugation of a Dirac field}
{title2=$C^\dagger C=I$}

For $\psi^C=\eta_C C\bar\psi^T$ and $\gamma^{0T}=\gamma^0=-C\gamma^0C^{-1}$, the <Dirac adjoint> transforms as $\bar\psi^C=-\eta_C^*\psi^TC^\dagger$. Anticommuting the components of the <fermion bilinear> gives $(\bar\psi\psi)^C=\bar\psi(C^\dagger C)^T\psi$. Invariance of the scalar bilinear therefore fixes $C^\dagger C=I$.

= Furry's theorem
{c}
{parent=Charge conjugation}
{wiki}

= Furry theorem
{c}
{synonym}

Furry's theorem states that a charge-conjugation-invariant vacuum has vanishing correlation functions containing an odd number of electromagnetic currents or photon fields. Consequently amplitudes with an odd number of external photons and no charged external state vanish.

= Time-reversal symmetry
{title2=$T$}
{parent=Discrete symmetry in quantum field theory}
{wiki=T-symmetry}

Time-reversal symmetry reverses the time coordinate and is represented antiunitarily in quantum theory.

= Time reversal of an electromagnetic gauge field
{parent=Time-reversal symmetry}
{title2=$A_0(x)\mapsto A_0(x_T),\quad A_i(x)\mapsto-A_i(x_T)$}

The vector <Dirac current> has an even temporal component and odd spatial components under <time-reversal symmetry>. Invariance of its real minimal coupling therefore gives the displayed transformation of the <gauge field>, up to a <gauge transformation>. Differentiation at $x_T=(-x^0,\boldsymbol x)$ yields $F_{\mu\nu}(x)\mapsto-t_\mu t_\nu F_{\mu\nu}(x_T)$, with $t=(1,-1,-1,-1)$. Thus the <electric field> is even and the <magnetic field> is odd.

= Time reversal of a Dirac field
{parent=Time-reversal symmetry}
{title2=$\hat T\psi(x)\hat T^{-1}=B\psi(-x^0,\boldsymbol x)$}

The spinor matrix $B$ obeys $B^{-1}\gamma^{\mu*}B=t_\mu\gamma^\mu$, with $t_0=1$ and $t_i=-1$. Conjugating the explicit $i$ and three spatial matrices also gives $B^{-1}\gamma^{5*}B=\gamma^5$. With spin phases $f_s=(-1)^{1/2-s}$ and identities $f_su^{-s*}(p_T)=\gamma^5Cu^s(p)$, relabeling spin and momentum in the <mode expansion of a Dirac field> gives $B=-\gamma^5C$, because $f_{-s}=-f_s$. That last relation is phase-convention dependent; the <quantum time-reversal operator> itself is not just a spinor matrix.

= CP violation
{c}
{parent=Discrete symmetry in quantum field theory}
{wiki}

CP violation is the failure of a theory to be invariant under the combination of charge conjugation and parity. Under CPT invariance it is equivalent to time-reversal violation.

= Fermion electric dipole moment operator
{title2=$\bar\psi\sigma^{\mu\nu}\gamma^5\psi F_{\mu\nu}$}
{parent=CP violation}

The fermion electric dipole moment operator couples a fermion pseudotensor bilinear to the <electromagnetic field tensor>. Its nonrelativistic interaction is proportional to $\mathbf S\cdot\mathbf E$, so it is odd under parity and time reversal and signals CP violation.

= Time-reversal parity of a fermion electric dipole operator
{parent=Fermion electric dipole moment operator}

For real $a$, the Hermitian effective interaction $ia\bar\psi\sigma^{\mu\nu}\gamma^5\psi F_{\mu\nu}$ is odd under <time-reversal symmetry>. The spinor tensor and <gauge field strength> both acquire $-t_\mu t_\nu$, while the <chirality matrix> is even. Their signs cancel, leaving the minus sign from the explicit $i$ under <antiunitarity>. By the <CPT theorem> this is also <CP violation>. It is not a renormalizable tree-level <Standard Model> term, but a radiatively generated low-energy dipole is allowed because of the <CP-violating phase> in the <CKM matrix>.

= CP-violating phase
{c}
{parent=CP violation}

A CP-violating phase is a complex phase in couplings or mixing matrices that cannot be removed by field redefinitions and changes sign under CP conjugation.

= Quantum field rephasing
{parent=CP-violating phase}
{title2=$\psi_i\mapsto e^{i\alpha_i}\psi_i$}

Multiplying individual fields by constant phases changes the apparent phases of interaction coefficients. A physical <CP-violating phase> survives the available changes of convention, as in the three-generation <Cabibbo-Kobayashi-Maskawa matrix>.

= CPT theorem
{c}
{parent=Discrete symmetry in quantum field theory}
{wiki}

The CPT theorem states that every local Lorentz-invariant quantum field theory with a Hermitian Hamiltonian is invariant under the combined action of charge conjugation, parity and time reversal. An operator that is odd under CP must therefore also be odd under time reversal if CPT is preserved.

= CPT symmetry
{c}
{parent=CPT theorem}
{title2=$CPT$}

The combined <charge conjugation>, <parity symmetry in quantum field theory> and <time-reversal symmetry> guaranteed under the hypotheses of the <CPT theorem>.

= Standard Model
{c}
{title2=SM}
{parent=Quantum field theory}
{wiki}

The Standard Model is a gauge theory with gauge group $SU(3)_c\times SU(2)_L\times U(1)_Y$. Its Higgs field breaks the electroweak subgroup to the electromagnetic $U(1)_Q$.

= Strong interaction
{parent=Standard Model}
{wiki}

The strong interaction is the $SU(3)_c$ gauge interaction described by <quantum chromodynamics>. It couples quarks and gluons through color charge.

= Meson-baryon scattering
{parent=Strong interaction}

Scattering with one <meson> and one <baryon> in the initial and final states. The <strong interaction> conserves <electric charge>, <baryon number>, net <strangeness>, four-momentum, total <angular momentum>, and parity. Approximate <isospin> symmetry imposes additional relations among charge-channel amplitudes.

= Pion-nucleon octet channels
{parent=Meson-baryon scattering}
{title2=$\pi N\to\pi N,\eta_8N,K\Lambda,K\Sigma$}

For an initial <pion>-<nucleon> state and outgoing <meson octet>-<baryon octet> pair, conservation of <baryon number> and <strangeness> allows $\pi N$, $\eta_8N$, $K\Lambda$, and $K\Sigma$, subject to <electric charge> and production thresholds. Total <isospin> is $1/2$ or $3/2$; the $\eta_8N$ and $K\Lambda$ channels contain only $1/2$.

= Weak interaction
{parent=Standard Model}
{wiki}

The weak interaction is the short-range chiral interaction mediated by the $W$ and $Z$ bosons. Above the electroweak scale it is part of the unbroken $SU(2)_L\times U(1)_Y$ electroweak gauge theory.

= Charged-lepton flavor violation
{parent=Weak interaction}

= Charged-lepton flavour violation
{synonym}

Processes that change individual charged-lepton family numbers are absent perturbatively in the minimal massless-neutrino <Standard Model>. A flavour-misaligned Higgs coupling can permit decays such as $h\to\mu\tau$. Neutrino masses and mixing permit extremely suppressed loop effects, while additional flavour structures can give larger rates.

= Dimension-six Higgs Yukawa misalignment
{title2=$Y_h=M_{\mathrm{diag}}/v+v^2\widehat\lambda^\prime/\Lambda^2$}
{parent=Charged-lepton flavor violation}

An operator $(\bar L_i\phi e_{Rj})(\phi^\dagger\phi)/\Lambda^2$ contains three factors of $v+h$. Its contribution to the mass matrix is proportional to $v^3$, while its single-Higgs coupling is three times the corresponding mass-over-v contribution. With the normalization $-\sqrt2\lambda^\prime/\Lambda^2$, the mass-basis coupling is $Y_h=M_{\mathrm{diag}}/v+v^2\widehat\lambda^\prime/\Lambda^2$. Nonzero off-diagonal coefficients permit charged-lepton flavour-violating Higgs decays.

= Weak charged current
{parent=Weak interaction}
{wiki=Charged_current}

The weak charged current couples a <W boson> to two members of a left-handed electroweak doublet. In the quark mass basis its flavor coefficients are entries of the <Cabibbo-Kobayashi-Maskawa matrix>.

= Weak flavour selection in neutrino-electron scattering
{parent=Weak charged current}

The <weak charged current> pairs each neutrino with its corresponding charged lepton. Thus elastic muon-neutrino/electron scattering has only tree-level neutral-current exchange, while electron-neutrino and electron-antineutrino scattering also have crossed and annihilation charged-current diagrams. A contact term pairing a muon neutrino directly with an electron would violate this Standard Model flavour assignment.

= Helicity suppression
{parent=Weak charged current}

A spin-zero parent decaying through a chiral <weak charged current> to a charged lepton and a massless <neutrino> cannot supply the helicities selected by a massless interaction. A charged-lepton mass insertion makes the amplitude proportional to $m_\ell$ and the <decay width> proportional to $m_\ell^2$. A spin-one <W boson> does not suffer this suppression.

= Weak charged-current quark scattering
{parent=Weak charged current}

For the massless contact interaction $-(G_F/\sqrt2)[\bar d\gamma^\alpha(P+Q\gamma^5)u][\bar c\gamma_\alpha(P+Q\gamma^5)s]$ with real $P,Q$, spin and colour averaging gives
$$
\frac{d\sigma}{d\Omega}=\frac{G_F^2s}{128\pi^2}\left[(P^2+Q^2)^2(1+\cos^2\theta)+8P^2Q^2\cos\theta\right].
$$
The <gamma matrix trace identities> give the averaged amplitude $4G_F^2[(C^2+4P^2Q^2)(p_2\cdot k_1)(p_1\cdot k_2)+(C^2-4P^2Q^2)(p_2\cdot k_2)(p_1\cdot k_1)]$, where $C=P^2+Q^2$. Substitute the massless <Mandelstam variables> and divide by $64\pi^2s$. The two singlet colour contractions give factor one after final summation and initial averaging. This is a low-energy <Fermi interaction> result, not its high-energy extrapolation.

= Neutrino scattering on an antiquark
{parent=Weak charged-current quark scattering}
{title2=$d\sigma_{\bar u}/dy=G_F^2s(1-y)^2/\pi$}

For a massless <neutrino> scattering on an unpolarized up <antiquark> through the <Fermi interaction>, $d\sigma/dy=G_F^2s(1-y)^2/\pi$. The corresponding down-quark channel has $d\sigma/dy=G_F^2s/\pi$. Their difference follows from the <chiral weak-current trace contraction>. The <parton distribution functions> weight these rates in a hadron.

= Chiral weak-current trace contraction
{parent=Weak charged-current quark scattering}
{title2=$T^{\alpha\beta}(a,b)T_{\alpha\beta}(c,d)$}

For massless momenta, define $T^{\alpha\beta}(a,b)=\operatorname{Tr}[\not a\gamma^\alpha(1-\gamma^5)\not b\gamma^\beta(1-\gamma^5)]$. The <gamma matrix trace identities> and the antisymmetric tensor contraction give $T^{\alpha\beta}(a,b)T_{\alpha\beta}(c,d)=256(a\cdot c)(b\cdot d)$. Reversing an antiquark's spinor order changes the momentum pairing and creates the $(1-y)^2$ angular factor in a <weak charged-current quark scattering> rate.

= Angular asymmetry of a chiral charged-current interaction
{parent=Weak charged-current quark scattering}
{title2=$A(\theta)$}

For the massless common vector-axial coupling at both vertices in <weak charged-current quark scattering>, subtracting opposite directions gives
$$
\boxed{A(\theta)=\frac{8P^2Q^2}{(P^2+Q^2)^2}\frac{\cos\theta}{1+\cos^2\theta}.}
$$
It is undefined for $P=Q=0$. Overall rate normalization cancels. Pure vector or axial interactions have zero asymmetry, while $P=\pm Q$ gives its maximal coefficient. The inequality $4P^2Q^2\leq(P^2+Q^2)^2$ implies $|A|\leq1$. This unpolarized observable does not distinguish purely left- from purely right-handed couplings applied at both vertices.

= Weak neutral current
{parent=Weak interaction}
{wiki=Neutral_current}

The weak neutral current couples the <Z boson> to a fermion without changing its electric charge. Its vector and axial-vector coefficients depend on weak isospin, electric charge, and the weak mixing angle.

= Flavor-changing neutral current
{parent=Weak neutral current}
{wiki}

A flavor-changing neutral current changes fermion flavor without changing electric charge. Such currents are absent at tree level in the Standard Model and are strongly suppressed at loop level by the <Glashow-Iliopoulos-Maiani mechanism>.

= GIM mechanism
{c}
{wiki}
{parent=Flavor-changing neutral current}

Unitarity of the <CKM matrix> cancels flavour-independent contributions to weak flavour-changing neutral amplitudes. In a quark loop, equal internal masses would give a common contribution whose off-diagonal CKM sum vanishes; unequal masses leave a suppressed nonzero amplitude. This explains why <neutral kaon mixing> occurs through loops rather than a tree neutral-current vertex.

= Glashow-Iliopoulos-Maiani mechanism
{c}
{title2=GIM mechanism}
{parent=Flavor-changing neutral current}
{wiki}

The Glashow-Iliopoulos-Maiani mechanism uses the unitarity of the quark mixing matrix to cancel flavor-changing neutral-current amplitudes when equal-charge quark masses are degenerate. The remaining loop amplitudes are suppressed by quark-mass differences.

= Accidental symmetry
{parent=Standard Model}
{wiki}

An accidental symmetry is respected by every operator of the chosen field content and dimension even though it was not imposed when constructing the theory. Higher-dimensional operators can violate it.

= Hierarchy problem
{parent=Standard Model}
{wiki}

The hierarchy problem asks why the Higgs mass remains far below a much larger ultraviolet scale when scalar mass parameters receive additive radiative corrections proportional to that scale squared.

= Naturalness
{disambiguate=physics}
{parent=Hierarchy problem}
{wiki}

= Naturalness
{synonym}

Naturalness is the expectation that a dimensionless observable should not be extremely small because unrelated large contributions cancel with high precision. It is a heuristic guide to model building rather than a mathematical consistency condition.

= Quantum chromodynamics
{title2=QCD}
{c}
{parent=Standard Model}
{wiki}

= QCD
{c}
{synonym}

Quantum chromodynamics is the $SU(3)_c$ gauge theory of quarks and gluons. At low energies it confines coloured fields into gauge-singlet hadrons.

= Large-Nc baryon scaling
{parent=Quantum chromodynamics}
{title2=$M_B,\Lambda,F_\pi^2,e^{-2}=O(N_c)$}

In the large-color limit of <QCD> with $g_s^2N_c$ fixed, baryon masses scale as $N_c$ while meson masses remain of order one. A <Skyrme model> action with coefficients of order $N_c$ has soliton mass and rotational moment of inertia of order $N_c$, and rotor splittings of order $1/N_c$. This motivates a semiclassical mesonic description of <baryons>; it does not uniquely determine every term in a finite-color effective theory.

= Strong coupling constant
{parent=Quantum chromodynamics}
{title2=$\alpha_s=g_s^2/(4\pi)$}

= QCD coupling
{synonym}
{c}

The strong coupling constant parametrizes the strength of <Quantum chromodynamics> interactions. Its dependence on the <renormalization scale> is governed by the <renormalization-group beta function>. It is dimensionless in four dimensions.

= Color charge
{parent=Quantum chromodynamics}
{wiki}

Color charge is the <gauge group> charge of <Quantum chromodynamics>, associated with its $SU(3)$ symmetry. A <quark> transforms in the fundamental three-dimensional representation, an <antiquark> in its conjugate, and a <lepton> is a singlet. The word color is a label for this charge, unrelated to visible light.

= Color multiplicity in a decay width
{parent=Color charge}
{title2=$N_c=3$}

For a colorless parent decaying through a color-diagonal interaction into a <quark> and <antiquark>, the three orthogonal matching-color states add incoherently. The inclusive flavor <decay width> is $N_c=3$ times the one-color width. In a parton scattering rate, an initial color average instead cancels the matching final color sum.

= Parton
{parent=Quantum chromodynamics}
{wiki=Parton_(particle_physics)}

= Parton model
{parent=Parton}

A high-energy <hadron> is treated as constituents scattering incoherently. In electromagnetic <deep inelastic scattering>, the charged constituents are <quarks> and <antiquarks>.

= Bjorken scaling
{c}
{parent=Parton model}

At leading order in the naive <parton model>, dimensionless <deep-inelastic structure functions> depend only on <Bjorken x>. <Quantum chromodynamics> evolution introduces scale dependence.

= Callan-Gross relation
{c}
{parent=Parton model}
{title2=$F_2=2xF_1$}

For massless spin-one-half <partons> at leading order, the unpolarized electromagnetic <deep-inelastic structure functions> obey this relation. Radiative <quantum chromodynamics> corrections and target-mass effects can modify it.

= Massless collinear parton approximation
{parent=Parton model}
{title2=$k=\xi P,\quad P^2=k^2=0$}

The leading high-energy approximation neglects <quark> and target masses and transverse constituent momentum. A lightlike reference momentum is used for the <hadron>; this does not describe a massless particle at rest.

= Parton distribution function
{parent=Parton model}
{title2=$q_f(x),\ \bar q_f(x)$}

A density of <partons> with a given flavour and momentum fraction at a specified <parton factorization scale>. It is a number density, not generally a distribution normalized to one.

= Parton factorization scale
{parent=Parton distribution function}
{title2=$\mu_F$}

The scale at which collinear contributions are assigned to <parton distribution functions> rather than the hard scattering coefficient. The separate factors depend on this scale, while a consistently calculated physical <differential scattering cross-section> does not.

= Deep inelastic scattering
{parent=Quantum chromodynamics}
{wiki}

Scattering that resolves the constituents of a <hadron> at large spacelike <momentum transfer>, with an inclusive sum over final <hadron> states.

= Inelasticity
{parent=Deep inelastic scattering}
{title2=$y=P\cdot q/(P\cdot p)$}

The inelasticity in <deep inelastic scattering> is $y=P\cdot q/(P\cdot p)$, where $p$ is the incoming lepton momentum, $P$ the target momentum and $q$ the transfer. In the target rest frame it is the fraction of the incoming lepton energy transferred to the target. It is unchanged by replacing $P$ by a collinear parton momentum $\xi P$, and $Q^2=xyS$ in massless kinematics.

= Bjorken scaling variable
{c}
{parent=Deep inelastic scattering}
{title2=$x=Q^2/(2P\cdot q)$}

In <deep inelastic scattering>, the scaling variable is $x=Q^2/(2P\cdot q)$ with $Q^2=-q^2$. In the massless collinear <parton model>, the outgoing on-shell condition $(xP+q)^2=0$ identifies $x$ with the struck parton's incoming longitudinal momentum fraction.

= Bjorken x
{c}
{parent=Deep inelastic scattering}
{title2=$x=\frac{Q^2}{2P\cdot q}$}

Here $Q^2=-q^2>0$. In the leading <massless collinear parton approximation>, this kinematic variable equals the momentum fraction of the struck <parton>.

= Hadronic tensor
{parent=Deep inelastic scattering}
{title2=$W^{\mu\nu}$}

The inclusive product of hadronic-current <matrix elements>, summed over final states with a <Lorentz-invariant phase-space measure>. Electromagnetic <current conservation> makes both indices transverse to the exchanged <four-momentum>.

= Deep-inelastic structure function
{parent=Hadronic tensor}
{title2=$F_1,\ F_2$}

The two scalar coefficients in the unpolarized electromagnetic <hadronic tensor>. Extra polarization or parity-violating currents can require further coefficients.

= Longitudinal deep-inelastic structure function
{parent=Deep-inelastic structure function}
{title2=$F_L=F_2-2xF_1$}

In the massless-target convention, the <Callan-Gross relation> sets this coefficient to zero at leading order in the <parton model>.

= Leptonic tensor
{parent=Deep inelastic scattering}
{title2=$L_{\mu\nu}$}

The product of lepton-current <matrix elements> with a specified <spin sum> or <spin average>. Its normalization must be stated when quoting a <differential scattering cross-section>.

= Gluon
{parent=Quantum chromodynamics}
{wiki}

A gluon is a massless spin-one gauge boson of quantum chromodynamics. The eight gluons transform in the adjoint representation of $SU(3)_c$ and carry color charge.

= Confinement
{parent=Quantum chromodynamics}
{wiki}

Confinement is the absence of isolated color-charged asymptotic states. Quarks and gluons instead occur inside color-singlet hadrons.

= QCD scale
{title2=$\Lambda_{\rm QCD}$}
{c}
{parent=Quantum chromodynamics}
{wiki=Lambda_QCD}

The QCD scale is the renormalization-group invariant energy at which the perturbative running coupling becomes strong. It sets the characteristic scale of confinement and most hadron masses.

= Flavor symmetry
{parent=Quantum chromodynamics}
{wiki}

Flavor symmetry rotates quark species whose strong interactions are identical. Quark masses break it explicitly, so it is a useful approximate symmetry only among quarks light compared with the hadronic scale.

= U-spin
{parent=Flavor symmetry}
{c}
{title2=$SU(2)_U$}
{wiki}

The $SU(2)$ <flavor symmetry> rotating down and strange <quarks>, with the up <quark> a singlet. Since down and strange <quarks> have equal <electric charge>, the charge operator commutes with all U-spin generators, and every irreducible U-spin multiplet has a common charge. Unequal down- and strange-quark masses break this symmetry.

= Flavor SU(3) Cartan generators
{parent=Flavor symmetry}
{title2=$\operatorname{tr}(h_ih_j)=\delta_{ij}$}

For <flavor symmetry> acting on $(u,d,s)$, trace-orthonormal Hermitian diagonal generators are $h_1=\operatorname{diag}(1,-1,0)/\sqrt2$ and $h_2=\operatorname{diag}(1,1,-2)/\sqrt6$. The <isospin> and <flavor hypercharge> operators are $I_3=h_1/\sqrt2$ and $Y=\sqrt{2/3}\,h_2$. Their multiples by $i$ lie in the compact <SU(3) Lie algebra>.

= Strangeness
{parent=Flavor symmetry}
{title2=$S$}
{wiki}

An additive <flavor symmetry> quantum number: a strange <quark> has $S=-1$, its <antiquark> has $S=+1$, and up and down <quarks> have $S=0$. The <strong interaction> conserves net strangeness. Together with <baryon number>, it determines <flavor hypercharge> $Y=B+S$.

= Flavor hypercharge
{parent=Flavor symmetry}
{title2=$Y=B+S$}

= Strong hypercharge
{synonym}

= Flavour hypercharge
{synonym}

The additive <flavor symmetry> quantum number $Y=B+S$ combines <baryon number> with <strangeness>. For the $(u,d,s)$ <quark> triplet its values are $(1/3,1/3,-2/3)$. The <Gell-Mann--Nishijima formula> is $Q=I_3+Y/2$. This is distinct from the normalization of <electroweak hypercharge>.

= Gell-Mann--Nishijima formula
{parent=Flavor hypercharge}
{c}
{title2=$Q=I_3+\frac Y2$}

For the light <quark> flavors, <electric charge> is $Q=I_3+Y/2$, with <flavor hypercharge> $Y=B+S$. On $(u,d,s)$ it gives $(2/3,-1/3,-1/3)$, and additivity extends it to the <baryon octet> and <meson octet>.

= Chiral symmetry
{parent=Flavor symmetry}
{title2=$SU(2)_L\times SU(2)_R$}

<Chiral symmetry> allows independent flavor rotations of left-handed and right-handed fields. For two massless quark flavors, the non-Abelian chiral transformations form $SU(2)_L\times SU(2)_R$. The additional vector $U(1)$ symmetry of <baryon number> acts trivially on the <pion> field $U$. The low-energy <pion> field $U\in SU(2)$ transforms as $U\mapsto LUR^\dagger$. Its derivative <Skyrme model> terms are invariant under constant $L,R$. A chosen vacuum $U=1$ preserves the diagonal subgroup $L=R$, which acts by conjugation and is the <isospin> symmetry. An ordinary common nonzero <pion> mass explicitly breaks the independent left/right symmetry to that vector subgroup; vacuum selection in the massless theory is instead <spontaneous symmetry breaking>. The singlet axial anomaly is a separate issue and is not part of this two-flavor non-anomalous group.

= Isospin
{parent=Flavor symmetry}
{wiki}

Isospin is the approximate $SU(2)$ flavor symmetry rotating the up and down quarks. It is accurate because their masses are close and both are small compared with the QCD scale.

= Isorotation
{parent=Isospin}
{title2=$U\mapsto AUA^\dagger$}

= Isospin rotation
{synonym}

An <isorotation> is a global transformation generated by <isospin>. In the <pion> <Skyrme model> it acts as $U\mapsto AUA^\dagger$, with constant $A\in SU(2)$, rotating the three <pion> components while preserving the scalar component. The matrices $A$ and $-A$ induce the same classical rotation, so this action factors through $SO(3)$; quantum states may carry half-integer <isospin> on its $SU(2)$ cover. This global internal rotation is distinct from a spatial rotation or a local <gauge transformation>.

= Eightfold way
{parent=Flavor symmetry}
{wiki=Eightfold_way_(physics)}

The eightfold way organizes light hadrons into approximate $SU(3)$ flavor multiplets built from the up, down, and strange quarks.

= Meson octet
{parent=Eightfold way}
{title2=$\mathbf8_M$}

= Octet mesons
{synonym}

The pseudoscalar octet of <flavor symmetry> contains three <pions>, four <kaons> and the <eta octet state>. In its <weight diagram>, the neutral <pion> and $\eta_8$ are separate states at the origin.

= Eta octet state
{parent=Meson octet}
{c}
{title2=$\eta_8$}

The zero-<isospin>, zero-<flavor hypercharge> state $\eta_8=(u\bar u+d\bar d-2s\bar s)/\sqrt6$ in the pseudoscalar <meson octet>. It is a pure octet basis state; the physical eta may mix it with the flavor-singlet state.

= Baryon octet
{parent=Eightfold way}
{title2=$\mathbf8_B$}

= Octet baryons
{synonym}

The spin-one-half octet of <flavor symmetry> contains the <nucleons>, <Sigma baryons>, <Lambda baryon>, and <Xi baryons>. Its <weight diagram> has six peripheral states and two states at $(I_3,Y)=(0,0)$: $\Sigma^0$ and $\Lambda^0$, with different total <isospin>.

= Chiral symmetry breaking
{parent=Quantum chromodynamics}
{wiki}

In massless QCD, a quark condensate breaks $SU(N_f)_L\times SU(N_f)_R$ to the diagonal vector subgroup. The associated Goldstone modes are the light pseudoscalar mesons.

= Linear sigma model
{parent=Chiral symmetry breaking}
{wiki}

The linear sigma model packages scalar and pseudoscalar fields into a linearly transforming multiplet. A symmetry-breaking potential gives a massive radial sigma mode and massless pions before explicit breaking is added.

= Chiral perturbation theory
{parent=Chiral symmetry breaking}
{wiki}

Chiral perturbation theory is the low-energy effective field theory of pseudo-Goldstone bosons from chiral symmetry breaking. It expands amplitudes in derivatives and light-quark masses over the chiral scale.

= Pion decay constant
{title2=$f_\pi$}
{parent=Chiral symmetry breaking}
{wiki}

The pion decay constant normalizes the axial current matrix element and the leading pion nonlinear-sigma-model action. It has mass dimension one in four spacetime dimensions.

= Pion
{title2=$\pi$}
{parent=Quantum chromodynamics}
{wiki}

A pion is one of the light pseudoscalar mesons produced by chiral symmetry breaking in quantum chromodynamics. The charge states are $\pi^+$, $\pi^0$ and $\pi^-$.

= Leptonic pion decay
{parent=Pion}
{title2=$\pi^+\to\ell^+\nu_\ell$}

The <pion decay constant> gives $\langle0|J_A^\mu|\pi(p)\rangle\propto f_\pi p^\mu$. Contracting the momentum with the leptonic <weak charged current> and applying the external Dirac equations makes the amplitude proportional to the charged-lepton mass. This is an explicit example of <helicity suppression>.

= Electroweak interaction
{parent=Standard Model}
{wiki}

The electroweak interaction is the $SU(2)_L\times U(1)_Y$ gauge theory that unifies the weak and electromagnetic interactions.

= Weinberg angle
{c}
{parent=Electroweak interaction}
{title2=$\theta_W$}
{wiki}

= Weak mixing angle
{synonym}

= Electroweak gauge invariance
{parent=Electroweak interaction}

Electroweak gauge invariance is invariance under local $SU(2)_L\times U(1)_Y$ transformations. It fixes the gauge couplings and organizes left-handed fermions into weak doublets while right-handed charged fermions are weak singlets.

= Sphaleron
{parent=Electroweak interaction}
{wiki}

= Electroweak sphaleron
{synonym}

A sphaleron is an unstable static electroweak field configuration at the top of the barrier between vacua of different topological number. Thermal transitions across this barrier violate $B+L$ while preserving $B-L$.

= Hypercharge
{title2=$Y$}
{parent=Electroweak interaction}
{wiki}

= Weak hypercharge
{synonym}

= Electroweak hypercharge
{synonym}

Hypercharge is the generator of the electroweak $U(1)_Y$ factor. With the convention $D_\mu=\partial_\mu-igW_\mu^aT^a-ig'YB_\mu$, electric charge is $Q=T^3+Y$.

= Charge quantization
{parent=Hypercharge}
{wiki}

Charge quantization is the restriction of electric charges to discrete multiples of a basic unit. In the Standard Model, Yukawa gauge invariance and gauge-anomaly cancellation relate the quark and lepton hypercharges and imply that the proton and electron have opposite charges.

= Higgs field
{c}
{parent=Electroweak interaction}
{wiki}

= Higgs doublet
{c}
{synonym}

The Standard Model Higgs field is a complex $SU(2)_L$ doublet of hypercharge $1/2$. Its nonzero vacuum expectation value triggers the <Higgs mechanism>.

= Higgs field potential
{parent=Higgs field}
{c}
{title2=$V(\phi)=-\mu_h^2\phi^\dagger\phi+\lambda(\phi^\dagger\phi)^2$}

= Higgs potential
{synonym}
{c}

For $\mu_h^2>0$ and $\lambda>0$, the minimum has $\phi^\dagger\phi=v^2/2$ with $v^2=\mu_h^2/\lambda$. In <unitary gauge>, $\phi=(0,v+H)^T/\sqrt2$ gives $V=\text{constant}+\lambda v^2H^2+\lambda vH^3+\lambda H^4/4$. The canonically normalized <Higgs boson> therefore has $m_H^2=2\lambda v^2$. The angular modes produce the <Goldstone bosons> absorbed in the <Higgs mechanism>.

= Higgs mechanism
{c}
{parent=Higgs field}
{wiki}

In the Higgs mechanism, gauge fields associated with broken generators acquire longitudinal polarizations and masses by absorbing Goldstone modes. Gauge fields of the unbroken subgroup remain massless.

= SO(3) vector Higgs model
{parent=Higgs mechanism}
{c}
{title2=$SO(3)\to SO(2)$}

A <Yang-Mills theory> with <gauge group> $SO(3)$ and a real three-component <scalar field> in its defining vector representation. With a symmetry-breaking <scalar potential>, a nonzero vector vacuum leaves its $SO(2)$ <stabilizer subgroup> unbroken. The two transverse internal directions produce massive <gauge bosons>.

= SO(3) invariant quartic scalar potential
{parent=SO(3) vector Higgs model}
{c}
{title2=$V=\frac\lambda4(\Phi^T\Phi-v^2)^2$}

The <scalar potential> $V(\Phi)=\lambda(\Phi^T\Phi-v^2)^2/4$, with $\lambda>0$, is invariant under the <SO(3) group>. For $v>0$, its vacuum manifold is a sphere. Canonical scalar normalization gives radial mass squared $2\lambda v^2$.

= Vector Higgs field
{parent=SO(3) vector Higgs model}
{title2=$\Phi\in\mathbb R^3$}

= Real triplet Higgs field
{synonym}

In the <SO(3) vector Higgs model>, a real <scalar field> $\Phi\in\mathbb R^3$ transforms as $\Phi\mapsto R\Phi$. Its <gauge covariant derivative> is $D_\mu\Phi=\partial_\mu\Phi+g\mathbf A_\mu\times\Phi$ for generators acting by the <cross product>.

= Covariantly constant vector Higgs field
{parent=Vector Higgs field}
{title2=$D_\mu\Phi=0$}

If $D_\mu\Phi=0$ in the <SO(3) vector Higgs model>, antisymmetry of the <gauge potential> implies $\partial_\mu|\Phi|^2=0$. On a local patch with nonzero constant norm, <unitary gauge> aligns the field with a fixed axis. The connection and <gauge field strength> then take values in the axis stabilizer; curvature can remain nonzero. Global alignment can require several bundle patches.

= SU2 adjoint Higgs model
{c}
{parent=Higgs mechanism}
{title2=$SU(2)\to U(1)$}

An $SU(2)$ <gauge theory> with a real adjoint scalar and a vacuum $\phi=ve_3$ breaks to $U(1)$. The <Higgs mechanism> gives two charged vectors mass $gv$, leaves one massless neutral vector, and gives the radial scalar mass $\sqrt{2\lambda}\,v$ for the quartic potential. Unlike the <Standard Model> <electroweak interaction>, this model has no additional hypercharge field and no massive neutral <Z boson>.

= Adjoint-Higgs breaking of SU(3) to SU(2) and U(1)
{parent=Higgs mechanism}
{title2=$SU(3)\to S(U(2)\times U(1))$}

For an adjoint <vacuum expectation value> proportional to $\operatorname{diag}(1,1,-2)$, the <centralizer> is $(SU(2)\times U(1))/\mathbb Z_2$. Four <gauge bosons> remain massless and four acquire a common mass. This differs from <fundamental-Higgs breaking of SU(3) to SU(2)>.

= Gauge-boson mass matrix from an adjoint scalar
{parent=Higgs mechanism}
{title2=$(M^2)_{ab}=-2g^2\operatorname{Tr}([t^a,\Phi_0][t^b,\Phi_0])$}

For generators normalized by $\operatorname{Tr}(t^at^b)=\delta^{ab}/2$, a constant adjoint <vacuum expectation value> $\Phi_0$ gives this <matrix>. A generator commuting with $\Phi_0$ corresponds to a massless <gauge boson>.

= Electroweak symmetry breaking
{parent=Higgs mechanism}
{wiki}

Electroweak symmetry breaking is the Higgs-field breaking $SU(2)_L\times U(1)_Y\to U(1)_Q$. It gives masses to the $W$ and $Z$ bosons while leaving the photon massless.

= Tree-level electroweak gauge-boson masses
{parent=Electroweak symmetry breaking}
{title2=$m_W=gv/2,\quad m_Z=v\sqrt{g^2+g'^2}/2,\quad m_A=0$}

For the <Higgs doublet> vacuum $(0,v)^T/\sqrt2$, the neutral <gauge-boson mass matrix> in $(W^3,B)$ is $(v^2/4)\begin{pmatrix}g^2&-gg'\\-gg'&g'^2\end{pmatrix}$. Its zero <eigenvector> is proportional to $(g',g)$, the <photon>; the perpendicular vector is the <Z boson>. The charged fields $W^\pm=(W^1\mp iW^2)/\sqrt2$ have the stated <W boson> mass. Thus $m_W=m_Z\cos\theta_W$, where $\theta_W$ is the <Weinberg angle>. The unbroken charge generator annihilates the vacuum and explains the zero eigenvalue.

= Unitary gauge
{parent=Higgs mechanism}
{wiki}

Unitary gauge uses a gauge transformation to remove the Goldstone fields from the Higgs multiplet, leaving the physical radial Higgs excitation and massive vector fields.

= Higgs boson
{c}
{parent=Higgs field}
{wiki}

The Higgs boson is the neutral radial excitation of the Higgs field about its vacuum expectation value.

= Higgs boson coupling to Z bosons
{parent=Higgs boson}
{c}
{title2=$\mathcal L_{HZ}=m_Z^2HZ_\mu Z^\mu/v+m_Z^2H^2Z_\mu Z^\mu/(2v^2)$}

= Higgs boson couplings to Z bosons
{synonym}
{c}

The neutral vector mass term is $\frac12m_Z^2Z_\mu Z^\mu(1+H/v)^2$. Expanding it gives the cubic $HZZ$ and quartic $HHZZ$ interactions in <unitary gauge>. Differentiating the action with respect to identical fields gives <Feynman rules> $2im_Z^2g_{\mu\nu}/v$ and $2im_Z^2g_{\mu\nu}/v^2$. A single $Z$ does not couple to two copies of the real neutral radial <Higgs boson> through an elementary vertex.

= Higgs decay to two Z bosons
{parent=Higgs boson coupling to Z bosons}
{c}
{title2=$\Gamma(H\to ZZ)$}

For $m_H>2m_Z$, the <Higgs boson coupling to Z bosons> gives $\mathcal M=(2m_Z^2/v)\epsilon_1^*\cdot\epsilon_2^*$. The <massive vector polarization sum> gives $\sum|\mathcal M|^2=(m_H^4/v^2)(1-4x+12x^2)$, with $x=m_Z^2/m_H^2$. The <two-body decay phase space> and <identical final-state symmetry factor> $1/2$ give
$$
\boxed{\Gamma(H\to ZZ)=\frac{m_H^3}{32\pi v^2}\sqrt{1-4x}(1-4x+12x^2).}
$$
The contraction uses $k_1\cdot k_2=(m_H^2-2m_Z^2)/2$. The on-shell rate vanishes at threshold; below threshold an off-shell calculation is required.

= Electroweak scalar triplet
{parent=Higgs field}

An electroweak scalar triplet transforms in the three-dimensional adjoint representation of $SU(2)_L$. A complex triplet of <hypercharge> one has components of electric charge two, one and zero, and its neutral component can couple to two left-handed lepton doublets.

= Electroweak gauge boson
{parent=Electroweak interaction}

= W boson
{c}
{title2=$W^\pm$}
{parent=Electroweak gauge boson}
{wiki}

The charged electroweak gauge fields are $W^\pm_\mu=(W^1_\mu\mp iW^2_\mu)/\sqrt2$. After electroweak symmetry breaking they have electric charges $\pm1$ and mass $gv/2$.

= W boson decay to a quark pair
{c}
{parent=W boson}
{title2=$\Gamma(W^+\to q\bar q\prime)$}

Ignoring accessible quark masses and strong corrections, the <W boson> flavor width is $\Gamma(W^+\to q\bar q')=N_cG_FM_W^3|V_{qq'}|^2/(6\pi\sqrt2)$. The <CKM matrix> fixes the flavor factor, while the <color multiplicity in a decay width> supplies $N_c=3$. Only the up and charm rows are accessible for an on-shell <W boson>, giving a total hadronic width $G_FM_W^3/(\pi\sqrt2)$ by row unitarity.

= Z boson
{c}
{title2=$Z^0$}
{parent=Electroweak gauge boson}
{wiki}

The neutral massive electroweak gauge field is proportional to $gW^3_\mu-g'B_\mu$ and has mass $v\sqrt{g^2+g'^2}/2$.

= Standard Model fermion
{parent=Standard Model}

A Standard Model generation contains left-handed quark and lepton doublets and right-handed up-quark, down-quark and charged-lepton singlets. A right-handed neutrino may be added as a gauge singlet.

= Fermion generation
{parent=Standard Model fermion}

A generation groups corresponding <quarks> and <leptons> with the same gauge quantum numbers as their counterparts in other generations. The <Standard Model> has three such families. <Yukawa matrices> assign their different masses, and the mismatch of up-type and down-type mass bases produces the <CKM matrix>.

= Quark generation
{parent=Fermion generation}

The three <quark> generations pair $(u,d)$, $(c,s)$ and $(t,b)$, each containing one charge-$2/3$ and one charge-$-1/3$ quark. The number of generations controls the number of physical mixing angles and <CP-violating phases> in the <CKM matrix>.

= Lepton
{parent=Standard Model fermion}
{wiki}

A lepton is a <Standard Model fermion> without <color charge>. The charged leptons have <electric charge> $-1$ and their corresponding <neutrinos> have charge zero. There are three generations; their <antiparticles> carry opposite <electric charge> and <lepton number>. Charged leptons and neutrinos have <lepton number> $+1$, with the corresponding antiparticles carrying $-1$.

= Muon
{parent=Lepton}
{title2=$\mu^-$}
{wiki}

The muon is the negatively charged second-generation <lepton>, with <electric charge> $-1$ and <lepton number> $+1$. Its <antiparticle> is the <antimuon>.

= Antimuon
{parent=Muon}
{title2=$\mu^+$}

= Positive muon
{synonym}

The antimuon is the <antiparticle> of the <muon>. It has <electric charge> $+1$ and <lepton number> $-1$.

= Quark
{parent=Standard Model fermion}
{wiki}

= Top quark
{parent=Quark}
{title2=$t$}
{wiki}

The top quark is the up-type member of the third <quark generation>. Its mass exceeds the <W boson> mass, so a real <W boson> cannot decay into an on-shell top quark. A massless approximation for accessible decay products must not silently remove this channel threshold.

= Spectator quark
{parent=Quark}

A constituent <quark> that does not participate in the elementary weak vertex of a <meson> decay. It combines with a produced <quark> or <antiquark> in the final <hadron>.

= Bottom quark
{parent=Quark}
{title2=$b$}
{wiki}

= Charm quark
{parent=Quark}
{title2=$c$}
{wiki}

= Charm antiquark
{parent=Charm quark}
{title2=$\bar c$}

= Strange quark
{parent=Quark}
{title2=$s$}
{wiki}

= Strange antiquark
{parent=Strange quark}
{title2=$\bar s$}

The <antiparticle> of the <strange quark> has <electric charge> $+1/3$ and <baryon number> $-1/3$.

= Down quark
{parent=Quark}
{title2=$d$}
{wiki}

= Down antiquark
{parent=Down quark}
{title2=$\bar d$}

The <antiparticle> of the <down quark> has <electric charge> $+1/3$ and <baryon number> $-1/3$.

= Up quark
{parent=Quark}
{title2=$u$}
{wiki}

= Antiquark
{parent=Quark}

The <antiparticle> of a <quark>.

= Standard Model representation
{c}
{parent=Standard Model fermion}

The Standard Model representations under $SU(3)_C\times SU(2)_L\times U(1)_Y$ are $Q_L=(\mathbf3,\mathbf2)_{1/6}$, $u_R=(\mathbf3,\mathbf1)_{2/3}$, $d_R=(\mathbf3,\mathbf1)_{-1/3}$, $L_L=(\mathbf1,\mathbf2)_{-1/2}$, $e_R=(\mathbf1,\mathbf1)_{-1}$, and $H=(\mathbf1,\mathbf2)_{1/2}$.

= Electroweak representation and hypercharge table
{title2=$Q=T_3+Y$}
{parent=Standard Model representation}

In normalization $Q=T_3+Y$, the left quark doublet has $Y=1/6$, right up/down singlets have $2/3,-1/3$, the left lepton doublet has $-1/2$, the right charged lepton has $-1$, and the Higgs doublet has $1/2$. The alternative convention $Q=T_3+\widehat Y/2$ uses $\widehat Y=2Y$. Up-type <Yukawa interactions> use the conjugate doublet of hypercharge $-1/2$.

= Baryon number
{title2=$B$}
{parent=Standard Model fermion}
{wiki}

Baryon number assigns $B=1/3$ to each quark, $B=-1/3$ to each antiquark and zero to leptons. A proton has baryon number one.

= Baryon plus lepton number
{title2=$B+L$}
{parent=Baryon number}

Baryon plus lepton number is the sum of <baryon number> and <lepton number>. It is an accidental classical symmetry of the renormalizable Standard Model but has an $SU(2)_L$ chiral anomaly, so electroweak instantons and sphalerons violate it.

= Proton decay
{parent=Baryon number}
{wiki}

Proton decay is a hypothetical baryon-number-violating transition of the proton into lighter particles. Its nonobservation places strong lower bounds on the scale, or strong upper bounds on the couplings, of baryon-number-violating interactions.

= Proton-decay operator
{parent=Proton decay}

A proton-decay operator is a local effective interaction that violates baryon number. Standard Model gauge invariance first permits such operators at dimension six, including $QQQL$ and $u^cu^cd^ce^c$.

= Lepton number
{title2=$L$}
{parent=Standard Model fermion}
{wiki}

Lepton number assigns $L=1$ to leptons, $L=-1$ to antileptons and zero to quarks. Neutrino masses show that separate family lepton numbers need not be exact.

= Neutrino
{parent=Lepton number}
{wiki}

A neutrino is an electrically neutral spin-one-half lepton. The Standard Model has three active neutrino flavours associated with the electron, muon and tau.

= Muon neutrino
{parent=Neutrino}
{title2=$\nu_\mu$}
{wiki}

The muon neutrino is the neutral <lepton> coupled to a <muon> by the <weak charged current>. It carries <lepton number> $+1$; its antineutrino has the opposite <lepton number>.

= Antineutrino
{parent=Neutrino}
{wiki}

An antineutrino is the antiparticle of a neutrino and carries the opposite additive lepton number.

= Neutrino oscillation
{parent=Neutrino}
{wiki}

Neutrino oscillation is the coherent conversion between neutrino flavors caused by different propagation phases of mixed mass eigenstates. It measures mass-squared differences and mixing parameters.

= Right-handed neutrino
{parent=Standard Model fermion}
{wiki}

A right-handed neutrino transforms as $(\mathbf1,\mathbf1)_0$ under $SU(3)_c\times SU(2)_L\times U(1)_Y$, so it is neutral under every Standard Model gauge interaction.

= Seesaw mechanism
{parent=Right-handed neutrino}
{wiki}

The type-I seesaw mechanism combines a Dirac neutrino mass $m_D$ with a much larger right-handed-neutrino <Majorana mass term> $M$. Diagonalizing the mass matrix gives a light eigenvalue of order $m_D^2/M$ and a heavy eigenvalue of order $M$.

= Weinberg operator
{c}
{parent=Seesaw mechanism}
{wiki}

The Weinberg operator is the dimension-five Standard Model operator $(LH)(LH)/\Lambda$. After the <Higgs field> condenses, it gives a left-handed neutrino Majorana mass of order $v^2/\Lambda$.

= Type-II seesaw mechanism
{parent=Seesaw mechanism}
{wiki}

The type-II seesaw mechanism couples two lepton doublets to an <electroweak scalar triplet>. A small neutral-triplet vacuum expectation value directly generates a left-handed neutrino <Majorana mass term>.

= Yukawa interaction
{c}
{parent=Standard Model fermion}
{wiki}

= Yukawa coupling
{c}
{synonym}

A Yukawa interaction couples left- and right-handed fermions through a scalar field. Replacing the Higgs field by its vacuum expectation value turns a gauge-invariant Standard Model Yukawa interaction into a fermion mass term.

= Fermion mass matrix
{title2=$-\bar f_LM_ff_R+\mathrm{h.c.}$}
{parent=Yukawa interaction}

After a scalar vacuum expectation value, a <Yukawa matrix> produces a bilinear $-\bar f_LM_ff_R+\mathrm{h.c.}$. Independent unitary left/right rotations diagonalize $M_f$ with nonnegative masses. Extra scalar-dependent operators can make the physical scalar coupling matrix fail to diagonalize with the mass matrix.

= Yukawa matrix
{title2=$Y_f$}
{c}
{parent=Yukawa interaction}

A Yukawa matrix contains the flavor-space couplings between left-handed fermions, right-handed fermions, and a scalar field. Its singular values determine fermion masses after symmetry breaking, while mismatches between its diagonalizing transformations produce flavor mixing.

= Cabibbo-Kobayashi-Maskawa matrix
{title2=$V_{\mathrm{CKM}}$}
{c}
{parent=Standard Model fermion}
{wiki=Cabibbo–Kobayashi–Maskawa_matrix}

= CKM matrix
{synonym}
{c}

The Cabibbo-Kobayashi-Maskawa matrix is the unitary mismatch between the left-handed transformations that diagonalize the up- and down-quark Yukawa matrices. It appears in charged weak currents.

= CKM parameter counting
{c}
{parent=Cabibbo-Kobayashi-Maskawa matrix}
{title2=$(N-1)^2$}

An $N\times N$ <CKM matrix> starts with $N^2$ parameters of a <unitary matrix>. Independent quark rephasings remove $2N-1$ phases because the common phase has no effect. The remaining $(N-1)^2$ parameters consist of $N(N-1)/2$ angles and $(N-1)(N-2)/2$ <CP-violating phases>. Extra mass degeneracies may reduce this generic count.

= Cabibbo angle
{c}
{parent=Cabibbo-Kobayashi-Maskawa matrix}
{title2=$\theta_C$}

The angle of the real two-generation <quark> mixing rotation, with $V_{ud}=V_{cs}=\cos\theta_C$, $V_{us}=\sin\theta_C$, and $V_{cd}=-\sin\theta_C$.

= Cabibbo suppression
{c}
{parent=Cabibbo angle}

= Cabibbo suppressed
{c}
{synonym}

A small <weak charged current> amplitude factor associated with $\sin\theta_C$. A doubly suppressed amplitude contains two such factors, giving a rate suppression of order $\tan^4\theta_C$ relative to a favoured process when other matrix elements are comparable.

= Jarlskog invariant
{c}
{title2=$J$}
{parent=Cabibbo-Kobayashi-Maskawa matrix}
{wiki}

The Jarlskog invariant is a rephasing-invariant measure of CP violation in a three-generation quark mixing matrix. It is the common magnitude of imaginary quartic products such as $\operatorname{Im}(V_{ij}V_{k\ell}V_{i\ell}^*V_{kj}^*)$ for distinct rows and columns.

= Classical field theory
{parent=Quantum field theory}
{wiki}

= Hamiltonian density
{parent=Classical field theory}
{c}
{title2=$\mathcal H=\pi_a\dot\phi_a-\mathcal L$}

A <Hamiltonian density> is a local integrand for the field-theory <Hamiltonian>. For canonical fields with invertible velocity-momentum relation it is the <Legendre transform in mechanics> of the <Lagrangian density>, summing over all conjugate field components. <Gauge theories> require constraints and multiplier terms as well.

= Canonical Hamiltonian density of a real scalar field
{parent=Hamiltonian density}
{title2=$\mathcal H=\tfrac12\pi^2+\tfrac12|\nabla\phi|^2+V(\phi)$}

For a <real scalar field> with <Lagrangian density> $\mathcal L=\dot\phi^2/2-|\nabla\phi|^2/2-V(\phi)$, the <canonical momentum> is $\pi=\dot\phi$. Its <Hamiltonian density> is the sum of momentum energy, gradient energy and potential energy. Hamilton's equations recover the <Euler-Lagrange equation>.

= Gauge theory
{parent=Classical field theory}
{wiki}

A <classical field theory> is a gauge theory when its description has a local redundancy represented by <gauge transformations>. A <gauge field> defines the <covariant derivative> that compares matter fields at different points, and its <gauge field strength> measures the curvature of that comparison. <Electromagnetism> is the familiar Abelian example; <Yang-Mills theory> generalizes it to a non-Abelian internal group. Both classical equations and their <quantum field theory> can have this structure. Physically equivalent gauge descriptions must give the same observables, even though the <gauge potential> changes.

= Internal symmetry of a classical field theory
{parent=Classical field theory}

An internal symmetry changes field values while leaving spacetime coordinates fixed, preserving the <action>. A constant phase rotation of a <complex scalar field> is an example. This distinguishes internal charges from the spacetime translation charges in the <canonical stress-energy tensor>. Localizing the parameter generally requires gauge fields; a global internal symmetry is not automatically a gauge symmetry.

= Variational symmetry of a Lagrangian density
{parent=Classical field theory}

A differentiable infinitesimal transformation is a variational symmetry when its change of the <Lagrangian density> is a divergence, $\delta\mathcal L=\varepsilon\partial_\mu K^\mu$. Under suitable <boundary conditions> the <action> is invariant, so exact pointwise invariance is unnecessary. The off-shell variation identity gives the <Noether current for a first-derivative scalar field>. This definition is for a constant transformation parameter; a spacetime-dependent parameter is a different, gauge-symmetry problem.

= Scalar field
{parent=Classical field theory}
{wiki}

A scalar field assigns one scalar to each point of <spacetime>. Under a change of coordinates, its value at a physical point is unchanged.

= Minimally coupled scalar field
{parent=Scalar field}
{title2=$L=-\tfrac12g^{ab}\nabla_a\phi\nabla_b\phi-V(\phi)$}

A real <scalar field> is minimally coupled to a <metric tensor> when its kinetic term uses the metric contraction of first derivatives and its <scalar potential> depends only on the field, without an explicit <scalar curvature> coupling. For <metric signature> $(-,+,+,+)$ the displayed <Lagrangian> and volume density $\sqrt{-g}$ give $\Box\phi-V'(\phi)=0$. Its <stress-energy tensor> is $T_{ab}=\nabla_a\phi\nabla_b\phi-g_{ab}[\tfrac12(\nabla\phi)^2+V]$. The <scalar stress-energy divergence identity> explains its conservation on the field equation.

= Scalar stress-energy divergence identity
{parent=Minimally coupled scalar field}
{title2=$\nabla^aT_{ab}=(\Box\phi-V^{\prime}(\phi))\nabla_b\phi$}

For a <minimally coupled scalar field> with a <Levi-Civita connection>, <metric compatibility> and the <symmetric Hessian of a scalar field> give the displayed off-shell identity. It is a special case of the <diffeomorphism Noether identity for a scalar field>, and gives <stress-energy conservation> when the scalar equation is satisfied. No gravitational field equation is needed.

= Scalar mass
{parent=Scalar field}
{title2=$m$}

The mass of a canonically normalized <scalar field> is determined by its quadratic fluctuation potential at a stationary vacuum. With several real fields, physical squared masses are the <eigenvalues> of the <scalar mass matrix>. A negative squared mass signals an unstable direction rather than a stable massive particle.

= Adjoint scalar field
{parent=Scalar field}

A <scalar field> transforming in the <adjoint representation of a Lie algebra> transforms by a <Lie bracket> with the infinitesimal gauge parameter.

= Sextic scalar field theory
{parent=Scalar field}
{title2=$\phi^6$}

= Zero-dimensional scalar field theory
{parent=Scalar field}

A <scalar field> with no spacetime coordinates reduces its <path integral> to an ordinary integral. It preserves <Wick contractions> and <Feynman-diagram symmetry factors>, making it a useful model of <perturbative quantum field theory>.

= Charged scalar field
{parent=Scalar field}

A charged scalar field is a complex <scalar field> transforming nontrivially under a gauge symmetry. For an electromagnetic potential $A_a$, its gauge-covariant derivative is $\mathcal D_a=\nabla_a-iqA_a$.

= Gauge-covariant derivative of a charged scalar field
{title2=$\mathcal D_a$}
{parent=Charged scalar field}

For electric charge $q$ and <electromagnetic four-potential> $A_a$, the derivative $\mathcal D_a=\nabla_a-iqA_a$ transforms covariantly under an electromagnetic <gauge transformation>.

= Gauge-covariant wave equation for a charged scalar field
{parent=Charged scalar field}

The massless gauge-covariant wave equation is $\mathcal D_a\mathcal D^a\Phi=0$. In a stationary charged background, the electrostatic potential shifts the local frequency from $\omega$ to $\omega+qA_t$.

= Scalar potential
{title2=$V(\phi)$}
{parent=Scalar field}

A scalar potential is the derivative-free energy density $V(\phi)$ of a <scalar field>. Its derivative supplies the force term in the field equation, and its local minima describe candidate vacuum field values.

= Flat direction of a scalar potential
{parent=Scalar potential}

= Flat directions of a scalar potential
{synonym}

In vacuum analysis, an exact <flat direction of a scalar potential> is a nonconstant continuous family of degenerate stationary vacua. The <scalar potential> stays constant along this family and its <gradient> vanishes there. An ordinary level set does not suffice: $V(x,y)=x^2+y^2$ is constant on every circle, but its vacuum at the origin has no flat direction. A vanishing <eigenvalue> of the quadratic <Hessian matrix> also does not suffice: $V(x)=x^4$ has zero quadratic mass at zero but no family of nearby vacua. Exact flat directions can be lifted by corrections absent from the classical <scalar potential>.

= Scalar-field shift symmetry
{parent=Scalar field}

A scalar-field shift symmetry acts by $\phi(x)\mapsto\phi(x)+c$ for constant $c$. Its <Noether charge> is the spatial integral of the canonical momentum, up to the normalization of $c$, and interactions invariant under the symmetry contain derivatives of the scalar field.

= Lagrangian density
{title2=$\mathcal L$}
{parent=Classical field theory}
{wiki}

A Lagrangian density is integrated over spacetime to give the action $S=\int d^dx\,\mathcal L$.

= Kinetic term
{parent=Lagrangian density}
{wiki}

A kinetic term contains derivatives of a field and determines its free propagation. Its quadratic differential operator becomes the inverse <quantum field theory propagator>.

= Gauge-covariant kinetic term
{parent=Kinetic term}

A gauge-covariant kinetic term replaces ordinary derivatives by covariant derivatives. Expanding it generates interactions between the matter field and one or more gauge fields, including contact vertices with two gauge bosons.

= Mass term
{parent=Lagrangian density}
{wiki}

A mass term is a quadratic term without derivatives whose coefficient sets the mass scale of a free field.

= Field interaction term
{parent=Lagrangian density}

A field interaction term is nonlinear in the fields and produces the interaction vertices of perturbation theory.

= Euler-Lagrange field equation
{parent=Lagrangian density}

For a field $\Phi$,
$$
\frac{\partial\mathcal L}{\partial\Phi}
-\partial_\mu\frac{\partial\mathcal L}{\partial(\partial_\mu\Phi)}=0.
$$

= On shell
{parent=Euler-Lagrange field equation}
{wiki}

A field configuration or identity is on shell when the <Euler-Lagrange field equations>[equations of motion] hold.

= Noether current
{title2=$j^\mu$}
{parent=Classical field theory}
{c}

A continuous variational symmetry gives a current satisfying $\partial_\mu j^\mu=0$ <on shell>. Integrating $j^0$ over space gives a conserved charge when boundary flux vanishes.

= Noether current for a first-derivative scalar field
{c}
{parent=Noether current}
{title2=$j^\mu=\mathcal L_{\partial_\mu\phi}X-K^\mu$}

For $\delta\phi=\varepsilon X$ and a <variational symmetry of a Lagrangian density>, write $\Pi^\mu=\partial\mathcal L/\partial(\partial_\mu\phi)$ and $\mathcal E=\mathcal L_\phi-\partial_\mu\Pi^\mu$. The product rule gives $\delta\mathcal L/\varepsilon=\mathcal E X+\partial_\mu(\Pi^\mu X)$. Therefore $j^\mu=\Pi^\mu X-K^\mu$ obeys $\partial_\mu j^\mu=-\mathcal E X$, so it is a <conserved current> <on shell>. Spatial integration gives a conserved <Noether charge> only when the boundary flux vanishes. The formula extends to several components by summation.

= Conserved current
{parent=Noether current}

= Current conservation
{synonym}

A conserved current is a vector field $j^\mu$ satisfying the continuity equation $\partial_\mu j^\mu=0$. Its spatial integral is constant in time when no current crosses the boundary.

= Conserved-current two-point function
{parent=Conserved current}
{title2=$G(k^2)$}

In a massless four-dimensional theory, current conservation gives a transverse tensor structure, $\int d^4x\,e^{-ikx}\langle J_\mu(x)J_\nu(0)\rangle=(k_\mu k_\nu-\delta_{\mu\nu}k^2)G(k^2)$. With current <mass dimension> three, the scalar coefficient has dimension zero.

= Noether charge
{title2=$Q$}
{c}
{parent=Noether current}

A Noether charge is the spatial integral $Q=\int j^0d^3x$ of a <Noether current>. It is time-independent when the current has vanishing flux through the spatial boundary, and in a Hamiltonian formulation it generates the associated continuous symmetry.

= Dilatation current
{title2=$D^\mu$}
{parent=Noether current}

The dilatation current associated with scale transformations is $D^\mu=x_\nu T^{\mu\nu}$ up to improvement terms. Its divergence is the trace of the stress-energy tensor, so classical scale invariance requires a traceless improved tensor on shell.

= Canonical stress-energy tensor
{title2=$T^{\mu\nu}_{\mathrm{can}}$}
{parent=Noether current}

Spacetime translations give the canonical stress-energy tensor
$$
T^\mu{}_{\nu}=\sum_a
\frac{\partial\mathcal L}{\partial(\partial_\mu\Phi_a)}
\partial_\nu\Phi_a-\delta^\mu_\nu\mathcal L.
$$

= Scalar-field momentum flux
{parent=Canonical stress-energy tensor}
{title2=$T^{01}=-\phi_t\phi_x,\quad T^{11}=\tfrac12\phi_t^2+\tfrac12\phi_x^2-V$}

For a real <scalar field> in one spatial dimension with metric signature $(+,-)$ and potential $V$, the <stress-energy conservation> law is $\partial_tT^{01}+\partial_xT^{11}=0$. The force on the field to the left of a fixed cut is $-T^{11}$ at the cut when the far-left boundary flux vanishes. <Finite-energy field configurations> also permit this conclusion by spatial cutoffs, without assuming pointwise decay of all derivatives.

= Four-momentum of a free real scalar field
{parent=Canonical stress-energy tensor}
{title2=$P^\mu=\int T^{0\mu}\,d^3x$}

For the free <real scalar field> with signature $(+,-,-,-)$,
$$
E=\frac12\int(\dot\phi^2+|\nabla\phi|^2+m^2\phi^2)d^3x,
\qquad \mathbf P=-\int\dot\phi\,\nabla\phi\,d^3x.
$$
The <canonical stress-energy tensor> is $T^{\mu\nu}=\partial^\mu\phi\partial^\nu\phi-\eta^{\mu\nu}\mathcal L$. Its divergence vanishes by the <Klein-Gordon equation>, giving constant integrated charges when integrals and boundary flux are well behaved. The minus sign in contravariant spatial <momentum> is from $\partial^i=-\partial_i$; lower-index spatial charges have the opposite sign. A <plane wave> $\cos(Et-\mathbf k\cdot\mathbf x)$ has <momentum> density pointing along $\mathbf k$.

= Canonical stress-energy tensor of the Maxwell field
{parent=Canonical stress-energy tensor}
{title2=$T^{\mu\nu}=-F^{\mu\rho}\partial^\nu A_\rho+\frac14\eta^{\mu\nu}F_{\rho\sigma}F^{\rho\sigma}$}

For signature $(+,-,-,-)$, translation invariance of the <Maxwell Lagrangian> gives this tensor. It is generally neither symmetric nor gauge invariant. The <antisymmetric superpotential improvement> $-\partial_\rho(F^{\rho\mu}A^\nu)$ produces, on the source-free equations of motion, the symmetric <electromagnetic stress-energy tensor> $\Theta^{\mu\nu}=-F^{\mu\rho}F^\nu{}_{\rho}+\eta^{\mu\nu}F^2/4$, which is traceless in four spacetime dimensions.

= Antisymmetric superpotential improvement
{parent=Canonical stress-energy tensor}
{title2=$T^{\mu\nu}\mapsto T^{\mu\nu}+\partial_\rho B^{\rho\mu\nu}$}

If $B^{\rho\mu\nu}=-B^{\mu\rho\nu}$, commuting partial derivatives gives $\partial_\mu\partial_\rho B^{\rho\mu\nu}=0$. Such an improvement preserves conservation of a <stress-energy tensor> and changes integrated charges only by a spatial boundary term. The <Belinfante-Rosenfeld stress-energy tensor> is obtained through this type of improvement.

= Belinfante-Rosenfeld stress-energy tensor
{c}
{parent=Canonical stress-energy tensor}
{wiki=Belinfante–Rosenfeld_stress–energy_tensor}

The Belinfante-Rosenfeld construction adds the divergence of a spin-current superpotential to the <canonical stress-energy tensor>, producing a symmetric conserved tensor with the same integrated four-momentum under suitable boundary conditions.

= Lorentz current
{title2=$J^{\mu\rho\sigma}$}
{c}
{parent=Canonical stress-energy tensor}

For a symmetric conserved stress-energy tensor, the Lorentz current is $J^{\mu\rho\sigma}=x^\rho T^{\mu\sigma}-x^\sigma T^{\mu\rho}$. Its spatial integrals generate rotations and boosts.

= Dirac current
{c}
{title2=$j^\mu=\bar\psi\gamma^\mu\psi$}
{parent=Noether current}
{wiki}

The global phase symmetry of a <Dirac field> gives the conserved Dirac current $j^\mu=\bar\psi\gamma^\mu\psi$.

= Gordon identity
{c}
{parent=Dirac current}
{title2=$2m\bar u\prime\gamma^a u$}

= Gordon decomposition
{c}
{synonym}

The <Gordon identity> rewrites a vector-current matrix element between equal-mass on-shell <Dirac spinors> using a momentum term and an antisymmetric spin term. In the mostly-minus convention $\{\Gamma^a,\Gamma^b\}=2\eta^{ab}$, $(\not p-m)u=0$, and $\Gamma^{ab}=[\Gamma^a,\Gamma^b]/2$, it reads $2m\bar u'\Gamma^au=\bar u'[(p'+p)^a-\Gamma^{ab}(p'_b-p_b)]u$. Derive it by adding the two external <Dirac equation> identities. The equivalent formula in the <mostly-plus Dirac convention> is $\bar u'[\gamma^{ab}(p'_b-p_b)+2m\gamma^a+p^a+p'^a]u=0$. The identity before division by $m$ remains valid for massless equal-mass states.

= Dirac electromagnetic current
{c}
{parent=Dirac current}

The Dirac electromagnetic current is the electric charge times the <Dirac current>. Its coupling $-ej^\mu A_\mu$ to the <electromagnetic four-potential> is the matter interaction of <quantum electrodynamics>.

= Derivative coupling to a conserved current
{parent=Dirac current}

An interaction $-\lambda j^\mu\partial_\mu\phi$ with an exactly <conserved current> is redundant: integration by parts gives $\lambda\phi\,\partial_\mu j^\mu$ up to a boundary term. For the <Dirac current>, the local phase redefinition $\psi=e^{-i\lambda\phi}\chi$ removes the coupling, so its on-shell scattering amplitudes vanish.

= Relativistic quantum field
{parent=Quantum field theory}

= Massive spin-two field
{parent=Relativistic quantum field}
{title2=$(\Box-m^2)h_{mn}=0,\quad\partial^mh_{mn}=0,\quad h^m{}_m=0$}

A free massive spin-two field is a <symmetric tensor> obeying a massive <Klein-Gordon equation>, vanishing divergence, and vanishing <trace>. Its rest-frame polarizations form the <symmetric traceless square> of the $SO(D-1)$ vector, with $(D-2)(D+1)/2$ degrees of freedom.

= Light-cone decomposition of a massive spin-two field
{parent=Massive spin-two field}
{title2=$\operatorname{Sym}_0^2(\mathbb R^{D-1})\downarrow SO(D-2)=\operatorname{Sym}_0^2(\mathbb R^{D-2})\oplus\mathbb R^{D-2}\oplus\mathbb R$}

When $\partial_-$ is invertible, divergence determines components with a plus index from $h_{--},h_{-I},h_{IJ}$. The <trace> constraint fixes the <trace> of $h_{IJ}$. The remaining transverse <group representations> are a <symmetric traceless square>, a <vector representation>, and a scalar.

= Decay width
{parent=Relativistic quantum field}
{title2=$\Gamma$}

In units with $\hbar=1$, the inverse lifetime of an unstable particle. A <neutral meson mixing> effective <Hamiltonian operator> incorporates decay through its anti-Hermitian part $-i\Gamma/2$.

= Lorentz-invariant phase space
{c}
{parent=Decay width}
{title2=$d\Phi_2$}

For two outgoing particles the <Lorentz-invariant phase space> is $d\Phi_2=(2\pi)^4\delta^{(4)}(p-k-k')\,d^3k/((2\pi)^32E_k)\,d^3k'/((2\pi)^32E_{k'})$. With massless daughters in their center-of-mass frame, $d\Phi_2=d\Omega/(32\pi^2)$ and $\int d\Phi_2=1/(8\pi)$. This fixes normalization factors in <decay widths> and scattering rates.

= Two-body Lorentz-invariant phase space
{title2=$d\Phi_2=d\Omega/(32\pi^2)$}
{parent=Lorentz-invariant phase space}

For two distinct massless final particles in their centre-of-momentum frame, integrating the energy-momentum delta function gives $d\Phi_2=d\Omega/(32\pi^2)$. A massless two-particle initial state has flux $2s$, hence $d\sigma/d\cos\theta=\overline{|\mathcal M|^2}/(32\pi s)$. Average only over the initial spin states physically present.

= Polarization vector
{parent=Relativistic quantum field}
{title2=$\varepsilon^\mu(p,\lambda)$}

A <Lorentz four-vector> coefficient selecting a spin-one polarization in a <mode expansion of a free field>. Its allowed components depend on the field equations and <gauge fixing>.

= Longitudinal polarization of a massive vector boson
{parent=Polarization vector}
{title2=$\varepsilon_L^\mu$}

= Longitudinal gauge-boson polarization
{synonym}

A physical polarization of a massive spin-one <gauge boson> whose spatial polarization points along its spatial momentum. Together with two transverse polarizations it supplies the three physical spin states. In the <Higgs mechanism>, a broken-direction <Goldstone boson> supplies this extra state as the <gauge boson> acquires mass.

= Photon polarization completeness relation
{parent=Polarization vector}
{title2=$\sum_r\epsilon_i^{(r)}\epsilon_j^{(r)*}=\delta_{ij}-k_ik_j/|\mathbf k|^2$}

Two orthonormal transverse <polarization vectors> span the plane perpendicular to a nonzero momentum. Summing their dyadic products gives the spatial transverse projector. It has trace and rank two and acts as the identity on physical <photon> polarizations.

= Polarization sum for a massive vector boson
{parent=Polarization vector}
{title2=$\sum_\lambda\epsilon_\mu(k,\lambda)\epsilon_\nu^*(k,\lambda)=-g_{\mu\nu}+k_\mu k_\nu/m^2$}

= Massive vector polarization sum
{synonym}

For $m>0$, the three physical <polarization vectors> are transverse to $k$ and have norm $-1$ in metric $(+---)$. In the rest frame their sum is $\operatorname{diag}(0,1,1,1)$; <Lorentz covariance> then gives the displayed expression for $k^2=m^2$. This includes the longitudinal mode. It cannot be specialized to $m=0$ by setting its denominator to zero.

= Timelike photon polarization
{parent=Polarization vector}
{title2=$\epsilon^{0\mu}=(1,0,0,0)$}

A timelike basis polarization in covariant photon quantization has positive <Minkowski metric> squared length, conventionally $\epsilon^{0\mu}=(1,\mathbf0)$ with signature $(+,-,-,-)$. It is distinct from the spatial <longitudinal polarization> $\epsilon^{3\mu}=(0,\widehat{\mathbf p})$. The extra minus sign in the covariant oscillator <commutator> makes its one-photon state a <negative-norm photon state>. Neither this temporal polarization nor an isolated longitudinal photon survives the <Gupta-Bleuler null-state quotient>.

= Real scalar field
{parent=Relativistic quantum field}
{wiki=Scalar_field_theory}

A free real scalar field obeys the <Klein-Gordon equation> and creates neutral spin-zero particles.

= Sine-Gordon theory
{parent=Real scalar field}
{c}
{title2=$\mathcal L=\frac12(\partial\varphi)^2-\frac{m^2}{\beta^2}(1-\cos\beta\varphi)$}

A relativistic <real scalar field> theory with a periodic cosine potential. In physical coordinates $y^\mu$, one normalization is $\mathcal L=\tfrac12\partial_\mu\varphi\partial^\mu\varphi-m^2(1-\cos\beta\varphi)/\beta^2$. With $\phi=\beta\varphi$ and $x^\mu=my^\mu$, its <action> is $S=\beta^{-2}\int d^2x[\tfrac12(\partial\phi)^2+\cos\phi-1]$. Its <Euler-Lagrange field equation> is the <sine-Gordon equation>. Distinct <scalar-field vacua> differ by $2\pi$ in $\phi$, permitting a <sine-Gordon kink>.

= Sine-Gordon vacuum tadpole counterterm
{parent=Sine-Gordon theory}
{c}
{title2=$\Delta M_{\rm ct}=4\delta m^2/(m\beta^2)$}

With canonical field $\varphi=\phi/\beta$, the quartic interaction has coupling $-m^2\beta^2$. Cancelling the vacuum <tadpole diagram> requires the divergent <mass counterterm> $\delta m^2=(m^2\beta^2/4)\int_{-\Lambda}^{\Lambda}dk/[2\pi\sqrt{k^2+1}]$. Its <kink> energy is $\Delta M_{\rm ct}=4\delta m^2/(m\beta^2)$ because $\int(1-\cos\phi_K)dx=4$. This cancels the logarithmic <ultraviolet divergence> in the <one-loop soliton mass correction>. Finite parts depend on the <renormalization condition>.

= Sine-Gordon multisoliton tau representation
{parent=Sine-Gordon theory}
{c}
{title2=$\phi=4\arg(f+ig)$}

For real parameters with $\kappa_i^2-b_i^2=1$, set $E_i=e^{\kappa_i x-b_i t+\gamma_i}$ and $a_{ij}=[(\kappa_i-\kappa_j)^2-(b_i-b_j)^2]/[(\kappa_i+\kappa_j)^2-(b_i+b_j)^2]$. Sum $\prod_i E_i^{\mu_i}\prod_{i<j}a_{ij}^{\mu_i\mu_j}$ over binary vectors of even parity for $f$ and odd parity for $g$. The <sine-Gordon equation> solution is the continuous field $4\arg(f+ig)$. In the all-<kink> sector, $\kappa_i=\cosh\theta_i$, $b_i=\sinh\theta_i$ and $a_{ij}=-\tanh^2[(\theta_i-\theta_j)/2]$. Treating these coefficients as positive would change the solution. Distinct <rapidities> give separated incoming and outgoing <solitons>.

= Sine-Gordon kink
{parent=Sine-Gordon theory}
{c}
{title2=$\phi_K=4\arctan e^{x-a}$}

The static <kink> $\phi_K(x)=4\arctan e^{x-a}$ joins adjacent <scalar-field vacua> $0$ and $2\pi$. It obeys $\phi_K'=2\operatorname{sech}(x-a)$ and has <topological charge> $Q=[\phi(+\infty)-\phi(-\infty)]/(2\pi)=1$. In the <sine-Gordon theory> normalization with physical mass scale $m$ and coupling $\beta$, its classical <mass> is $8m/\beta^2$. A <Lorentz boost> gives $\phi_K=4\arctan\exp[(x-vt-a)/\sqrt{1-v^2}]$. Spatial reflection gives an <antikink>.

= Sine-Gordon kink fluctuation operator
{parent=Sine-Gordon kink}
{c}
{title2=$\Delta_x=-\partial_x^2+1-2\operatorname{sech}^2x$}

The <second variation> of the <sine-Gordon theory> action about its static <kink> gives $\Delta_x=-\partial_x^2+1-2\operatorname{sech}^2x$. Let $A=\partial_x+\tanh x$. Then $\Delta_x=A^\dagger A$ and $AA^\dagger=-\partial_x^2+1$. Thus $\Delta_x$ is nonnegative, with its <translational zero mode of a sine-Gordon kink> and a continuum at $\omega^2=k^2+1$. The construction is the <supersymmetric factorization of the one-soliton potential>.

= Translational zero mode of a sine-Gordon kink
{parent=Sine-Gordon kink fluctuation operator}
{title2=$\psi_0=\operatorname{sech}x/\sqrt2$}

The normalized <eigenfunction> $\psi_0(x)=\operatorname{sech}x/\sqrt2$ has zero <eigenvalue> under the <sine-Gordon kink fluctuation operator>. It is proportional to $\partial_x\phi_K$ and comes from shifting the <collective coordinate> of the <kink>. Its frequency is zero, so it contributes no oscillator <zero-point energy>; it must be handled separately from a Gaussian <functional determinant>.

= Scalar field configuration eigenstate
{parent=Real scalar field}
{title2=$\widehat\phi(\mathbf x)|\varphi\rangle=\varphi(\mathbf x)|\varphi\rangle$}

= Field configuration eigenstate
{synonym}

A <scalar field configuration eigenstate> assigns eigenvalues of the equal-time field operator at every spatial point. These generalized states play the role of position eigenstates for a system of infinitely many coordinates. Inserting their regulated completeness relations on time slices constructs the <scalar field path integral>.

= Scalar propagator
{parent=Real scalar field}
{title2=$\Delta_E(p)$}

For a free massive scalar in a <Euclidean path integral>, inversion of the quadratic kernel gives $\Delta_E(p)=1/(p^2+m^2)$. The Minkowski version follows by <Wick rotation>.

= Shell-restricted scalar propagator
{parent=Scalar propagator}
{title2=$\widetilde C_>(p)=\mathbf1_{\Lambda^\prime<|p|\leq\Lambda}/(p^2+m^2)$}

The Gaussian contraction used while integrating out a momentum shell. Its Fourier support lies entirely in that shell. In particular, convolution with a purely low-momentum field is zero. This distinguishes shell <Feynman diagrams> from unrestricted loop diagrams.

= Nonminimally coupled scalar field
{parent=Real scalar field}

A nonminimal scalar coupling includes an interaction between the field and spacetime curvature, such as $-\xi R\Phi^2$ in the <Lagrangian density>. The <Euler-Lagrange field equation> for $\mathcal L=-\frac12(\nabla\Phi)^2-\xi R\Phi^2$ is $\Box\Phi-2\xi R\Phi=0$. The coefficient is convention dependent: an action written with $-\frac12\xi R\Phi^2$ has $\xi$ instead of $2\xi$.

= Internal rotation symmetry of two real scalar fields
{parent=Real scalar field}

= Scalar doublet rotation symmetry
{synonym}

For two free <real scalar fields>, the internal rotation $\delta\phi_1=\theta\phi_2$, $\delta\phi_2=-\theta\phi_1$ is a symmetry precisely when their squared masses agree. Its <Noether current> is $j^\mu=\phi_2\partial^\mu\phi_1-\phi_1\partial^\mu\phi_2$. Without mass degeneracy, $\partial_\mu j^\mu=(m_2^2-m_1^2)\phi_1\phi_2$.

= Canonical quantization of a real scalar field
{parent=Real scalar field}

The equal-time <canonical commutation relation> is $[\phi(t,\mathbf x),\pi(t,\mathbf y)]=i\delta^3(\mathbf x-\mathbf y)$ in units $\hbar=1$, with the two field-field and momentum-momentum commutators zero. The quadratic <Hamiltonian operator> gives $\dot\phi=\pi$ and $\dot\pi=\nabla^2\phi-m^2\phi$, hence the <Klein-Gordon equation> as an operator identity.

= Scalar field oscillator inversion
{parent=Canonical quantization of a real scalar field}
{title2=$a_{\mathbf p}=e^{iEt}\int e^{-i\mathbf p\cdot\mathbf x}(\sqrt{E/2}\,\phi+i\pi/\sqrt{2E})\,d^3x$}

For the <real scalar field> expansion $\phi=\int d^3p\,[a_{\mathbf p}e^{-ipx}+a^\dagger_{\mathbf p}e^{ipx}]/((2\pi)^3\sqrt{2E_{\mathbf p}})$, the equal-time fields extract $a_{\mathbf p}=e^{iEt}\int d^3x\,e^{-i\mathbf p\cdot\mathbf x}[\sqrt{E/2}\,\phi+i\pi/\sqrt{2E}]$. The two mixed <canonical commutation relation> terms yield $[a_{\mathbf p},a^\dagger_{\mathbf q}]=(2\pi)^3\delta^3(\mathbf p-\mathbf q)$, while the other oscillator <commutators> vanish. This verifies the normalization of the mode expansion directly from the canonical fields.

= Phi-fourth theory
{title2=$\phi^4$ theory}
{parent=Real scalar field}
{wiki=Quartic_interaction}

= Quartic scalar field theory
{synonym}

Phi-fourth theory has a scalar interaction $-\lambda\phi^4/4!$. In four dimensions its coupling is classically marginal, and its one-loop four-point function receives bubble corrections in the three Mandelstam channels.

= One-loop quartic scalar beta function
{parent=Phi-fourth theory}
{title2=$\beta(\lambda)=3\lambda^2/(16\pi^2)+O(\lambda^3)$}

With interaction $\lambda\phi^4/4!$ for one real scalar, the three one-loop four-point bubble channels give the four-dimensional <renormalization-group beta function> $\beta(\lambda)=3\lambda^2/(16\pi^2)+O(\lambda^3)$. Its positive sign for $\lambda>0$ excludes <asymptotic freedom> and produces a perturbative <Landau pole> on extrapolation.

= Renormalized quartic scalar vertex
{parent=Phi-fourth theory}
{title2=$\Gamma_R^{(4)}$}

= Phi cubed theory
{title2=$\phi^3$ theory}
{parent=Real scalar field}

= Cubic scalar field theory
{synonym}

Phi cubed theory has interaction $-g\phi^3/3!$, giving a cubic vertex $-ig$. In four spacetime dimensions the scalar field has mass dimension one and $g$ has mass dimension one.

= One-loop two-point divergence in six-dimensional cubic scalar theory
{parent=Phi cubed theory}
{title2=$I_{\mathrm{div}}(k)=-g^2(m^2+k^2/6)/[(4\pi)^3\epsilon]$}

The two-vertex cubic bubble in $d=6-\epsilon$ dimensions has the displayed amputated pole. A <Feynman parameter> gives mass $m^2+x(1-x)k^2$, and $\Gamma(-1+\epsilon/2)$ supplies the pole. The full propagator receives $D_0^2 I$, while its inverse receives $-I$. This sign distinguishes a two-point insertion from an inverse-propagator correction.

= Minimal-subtraction two-point counterterms in cubic scalar theory
{parent=One-loop two-point divergence in six-dimensional cubic scalar theory}
{title2=$\delta Z=-g^2/[6(4\pi)^3\epsilon],\quad\delta m^2=-g^2m^2/[(4\pi)^3\epsilon]$}

With additive <counterterms> $\tfrac12\delta Z(\partial\phi)^2+\tfrac12\delta m^2\phi^2$, their Euclidean propagator insertion is $-(\delta Zk^2+\delta m^2)$. These <minimal subtraction scheme> coefficients cancel the cubic bubble insertion. A bare mass shift also subtracts $m^2\delta Z$ after <wavefunction renormalization>; it is not the same coefficient as the additive mass counterterm.

= Six-dimensional cubic scalar field theory
{parent=Phi cubed theory}
{title2=$\mathcal L=\tfrac12(\partial\phi)^2+\tfrac12m^2\phi^2+g\phi^3/3!$}

A canonically normalized <real scalar field> in six dimensions has <mass dimension> two. The cubic coupling is classically a <marginal coupling>, whereas a local quartic coupling has <mass dimension> minus two. This theory is perturbatively renormalizable by <power counting in quantum field theory>, but a real cubic Euclidean potential is unbounded below. Its expansion about a massive Gaussian theory is therefore formal perturbation theory, not a convergent positive functional integral at nonzero real $g$.

= Perturbative renormalizability of cubic scalar theory in six dimensions
{parent=Six-dimensional cubic scalar field theory}

For a cubic graph with $E$ external legs, $3V=2I+E$ and $L=I-V+1$. Its <superficial degree of divergence> is therefore $6L-2I=6-2E$. Divergences in vacuum, one-, two-, and three-point functions can be absorbed into vacuum energy, a linear term, mass, <wave-function renormalization>, and cubic-coupling <counterterms>. Higher-point proper diagrams are superficially convergent after subtraction of divergent subgraphs. This establishes perturbative renormalizability, without asserting a nonperturbative positive measure for the unstable real cubic potential.

= Cubic scalar box contribution to a quartic coupling
{parent=Six-dimensional cubic scalar field theory}
{title2=$\delta g_4=-3g^4I_4$}

In a <six-dimensional cubic scalar field theory>, three labelled <box Feynman diagrams> give the one-loop local quartic vertex at zero external momentum. With an effective-action term $g_4\phi^4/4!$, their contribution is $\delta g_4=-3g^4I_4$. To check its sign and multiplicity, expand the <one-loop scalar effective action> as $\tfrac12\operatorname{Tr}\log(D+g\phi)$, where $D=-\partial^2+m^2$. Its fourth-order term is $-g^4\operatorname{Tr}(D^{-1}\phi)^4/8$, giving the stated coefficient. The associated amputated connected diagram insertion has the opposite sign.

= Scalar shell integral in six dimensions
{parent=Six-dimensional cubic scalar field theory}
{title2=$I_r(\Lambda,\Lambda_0;m)$}

For $m>0$ and $0<\Lambda<\Lambda_0$, define
$$
I_r=\int_{\Lambda<|q|<\Lambda_0}\frac{d^6q}{(2\pi)^6}(q^2+m^2)^{-r}=\frac1{64\pi^3}\int_\Lambda^{\Lambda_0}\frac{q^5\,dq}{(q^2+m^2)^r}.
$$
The radial formula uses the area $\pi^3$ of the unit five-sphere. Its <mass dimension> is $6-2r$. As $\Lambda_0\to\infty$, $I_1$ diverges quartically, $I_2$ quadratically, $I_3$ logarithmically, and $I_r$ is ultraviolet finite for $r\ge4$ at fixed $\Lambda,m$. This follows directly by comparison with $q^{5-2r}$.

= Four-point tree amplitude in phi cubed theory
{parent=Phi cubed theory}

The four-point <tree-level Feynman diagram> has two cubic vertices joined by one propagator. The three ways to divide four labeled external legs into vertex pairs give the $s$, $t$, and $u$ channels. For interaction $+\lambda\phi^3/3!$, the amplitude is $\mathcal M=-\lambda^2[(s-m^2)^{-1}+(t-m^2)^{-1}+(u-m^2)^{-1}]$, with the <Feynman i-epsilon prescription> understood. All channels interfere in its modulus squared.

= Five-point tree amplitude in phi cubed theory
{parent=Phi cubed theory}

A connected five-point tree in phi cubed theory has three cubic vertices and two internal propagators. For labeled external legs there are fifteen diagrams: choose the leg attached to the middle vertex and partition the other four legs into two unordered pairs.

= Phi-six theory
{title2=$\phi^6$ theory}
{parent=Real scalar field}

Phi-six theory has scalar interaction $-\lambda\phi^6/6!$. In four spacetime dimensions its coupling has mass dimension $-2$, and a six-valent vertex contributes the momentum-space factor $-i\lambda$.

= Complex scalar field
{parent=Relativistic quantum field}
{wiki=Scalar_field_theory}

A complex scalar field has distinct particle and antiparticle excitations and a global phase symmetry.

= Global phase symmetry of a complex scalar field
{parent=Complex scalar field}
{title2=$\psi\mapsto e^{-i\alpha}\psi$}

For $\mathcal L=\partial_\mu\psi^*\partial^\mu\psi-V(|\psi|^2)$, constant $\alpha$ preserves both terms. This is an <internal symmetry of a classical field theory> with <circle group> $U(1)$. A spacetime-dependent phase would introduce derivative terms, so the ordinary-derivative density has only the global symmetry. The associated current is the <Noether charge of a complex scalar field> construction; the orientation $e^{-i\alpha}$ fixes its overall sign.

= Noether charge of a complex scalar field
{c}
{parent=Global phase symmetry of a complex scalar field}
{title2=$Q=i\int(\psi^*\dot\psi-\psi\dot\psi^*)\,d^3x$}

For the <global phase symmetry of a complex scalar field> oriented as $\delta\psi=-i\varepsilon\psi$, the <Noether current> is $j^\mu=i(\psi^*\partial^\mu\psi-\psi\partial^\mu\psi^*)$. The <Euler-Lagrange field equations> $\Box\psi+V'(|\psi|^2)\psi=0$ and its <complex conjugate> give $\partial_\mu j^\mu=i(\psi^*\Box\psi-\psi\Box\psi^*)=0$ for a real differentiable potential. Integrating $j^0$ gives $Q$ when boundary flux vanishes. A charged <scalar> coupled to electromagnetism has <electric charge> $qQ$ with the chosen charge normalization; without that physical identification it is an internal charge.

= Fierz-Pauli equations
{c}
{parent=Relativistic quantum field}

The Fierz-Pauli equations describe a free massive spin-two field. A symmetric tensor $h_{\mu\nu}$ obeys the Klein-Gordon equation together with $\partial^\mu h_{\mu\nu}=0$ and $h^\mu{}_{\mu}=0$, leaving five propagating polarizations in four dimensions.

= Dirac field
{c}
{parent=Relativistic quantum field}
{wiki}

A free Dirac field has Lagrangian density $\mathcal L=i\bar\psi\gamma^\mu\partial_\mu\psi-m\bar\psi\psi$ and obeys the <Dirac equation>.

= Fermion bilinear
{parent=Dirac field}
{title2=$\bar\psi\Gamma\psi$}

A local expression containing two <fermion> fields and a matrix acting on <Dirac spinor> indices. The choices $\Gamma=\gamma^\mu$ and $\Gamma=\gamma^\mu\gamma^5$ give the <Dirac current> and <axial current>.

= Vector current
{parent=Fermion bilinear}
{title2=$J^\mu_{ij}=\bar\psi_i\gamma^\mu\psi_j$}

A <fermion bilinear> transforming as a <Lorentz four-vector>. For a single <Dirac field> this is the <Dirac current>; distinct flavours can give a charged or flavour-changing current.

= Mode expansion of a Dirac field
{parent=Dirac field}

The mode expansion of a free Dirac field decomposes it into positive-frequency particle modes and negative-frequency antiparticle modes:
$$
\psi(x)=\sum_s\int d\Pi_p\left[b^s(p)u^s(p)e^{-ip\cdot x}+d^{s\dagger}(p)v^s(p)e^{ip\cdot x}\right].
$$
The operators $b^s$ and $d^s$ obey fermionic anticommutation relations.

= Antiparticle
{parent=Mode expansion of a Dirac field}
{wiki}

An antiparticle has the same mass and spin as its particle and opposite additive internal charges. In a relativistic quantum field, antiparticles are created by the negative-frequency part of the field expansion.

= Fermionic annihilation operator
{parent=Mode expansion of a Dirac field}

A fermionic annihilation operator removes one fermion from a specified one-particle mode. Together with its adjoint it satisfies a canonical anticommutation relation such as $\{b_r,b_s^\dagger\}=\delta_{rs}$.

= Fermionic creation operator
{parent=Mode expansion of a Dirac field}

A fermionic creation operator is the adjoint of a <fermionic annihilation operator> and adds one fermion to a mode. Its square vanishes, implementing the <Pauli exclusion principle>.

= Dirac equation
{c}
{title2=$(i\gamma^\mu\partial_\mu-m)\psi=0$}
{parent=Dirac field}
{wiki}

The Dirac equation is the relativistic spin-one-half field equation $(i\gamma^\mu\partial_\mu-m)\psi=0$.

= Lorentz covariance of the Dirac operator
{parent=Dirac equation}
{c}
{title2=$\mathcal D'\Psi'=S\mathcal D\Psi$}

A spinor Lorentz matrix satisfies $S^{-1}\gamma^\mu S=\Lambda^\mu{}_{\nu}\gamma^\nu$. Combining this identity with the transformed derivative shows that $(i\gamma^\mu\partial'_\mu-m)\Psi'=S(i\gamma^\mu\partial_\mu-m)\Psi$. Thus a <Lorentz transformation> carries every solution of the <Dirac equation> to another.

= Adjoint Dirac equation
{parent=Dirac equation}
{title2=$i(\partial_\mu\bar\Psi)\gamma^\mu+m\bar\Psi=0$}

Varying the <Dirac action> with respect to the spinor, rather than its <Dirac adjoint>, gives the left-acting field equation after integration by parts. Together with the <Dirac equation> it proves conservation of the vector current.

= Massless Dirac equation
{parent=Dirac equation}
{title2=$\gamma^m\partial_m\Psi=0$}

The massless <Dirac equation> is $\gamma^m\partial_m\Psi=0$. The <Clifford algebra> makes its square the massless wave equation. Quantization of a worldline spin variable as $\widehat\psi^m=\gamma^m/\sqrt2$ turns its fermionic constraint into this equation.

= Mostly-plus Dirac convention
{parent=Dirac equation}
{title2=$\eta=(-+++),\quad\{\gamma^a,\gamma^b\}=-2\eta^{ab}$}

One consistent <Dirac equation> convention uses $\eta=\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, $\{\gamma^a,\gamma^b\}=-2\eta^{ab}$, and $(i\gamma^a\partial_a+m)\psi=0$. Taking the negatives of the standard mostly-minus <gamma matrices>, and defining the <Dirac adjoint> with the new $\gamma^0$, gives the same physical <Dirac action>. The positive-frequency plane wave is $e^{ip\cdot x}u(p)$ with $p^0>0$, and its equation is $(\not p-m)u=0$. The <mass shell> is $p^2=-m^2$. These signs must be translated together when using a formula stated in another convention.

= Dirac adjoint
{title2=$\bar\psi=\psi^\dagger\gamma^0$}
{c}
{parent=Dirac field}

The Dirac adjoint of a spinor is $\bar\psi=\psi^\dagger\gamma^0$. It transforms contragrediently, making $\bar\psi\psi$ a Lorentz scalar and $\bar\psi\gamma^\mu\psi$ a Lorentz vector.

= Dirac scalar bilinear
{parent=Dirac adjoint}
{c}
{title2=$\bar\Psi\Psi$}

The <Dirac scalar bilinear> is invariant under the simultaneous spinor and adjoint transformations because $\bar\Psi'\Psi'=\bar\Psi S^{-1}S\Psi$. It is the <mass term> of the <Dirac action>. At fixed coordinates it transforms as a <scalar field>, with an orbital transport term and no spin term.

= Dirac action
{c}
{parent=Dirac field}

The free Dirac action is
$$
S_D=\int d^4x\,\bar\psi(i\gamma^\mu\partial_\mu-m)\psi.
$$
Its Euler-Lagrange equations are the Dirac equation and its adjoint.

= Fermion spin sum
{parent=Dirac field}

For on-shell Dirac spinors in standard relativistic normalization,
$$
\sum_ru_r(p)\bar u_r(p)=\not p+m,
\qquad
\sum_rv_r(p)\bar v_r(p)=\not p-m.
$$
These completeness identities turn spin-summed squared amplitudes into gamma-matrix traces.

= Weyl spinor
{c}
{parent=Dirac field}
{wiki}

A left- or right-handed Weyl spinor transforms in the Lorentz representation $(1/2,0)$ or $(0,1/2)$. Parity exchanges the two chiralities.

= Chirality
{title2=$\gamma^5$ eigenvalue}
{parent=Weyl spinor}
{wiki=Chirality_(physics)}

Chirality distinguishes the two irreducible Weyl representations of the proper Lorentz group. For a massless particle it agrees with helicity up to the particle-antiparticle convention, while a mass term couples opposite chiralities.

= Chiral projector
{title2=$P_{L,R}=(1\mp\gamma^5)/2$}
{parent=Chirality}

The projectors $P_L=(1-\gamma^5)/2$ and $P_R=(1+\gamma^5)/2$ select the two <chirality> eigenspaces of a <Dirac field>. They satisfy $P_L+P_R=I$, $P_LP_R=0$, and $P_L^2=P_L$, $P_R^2=P_R$.

= Vectorlike gauge spectrum
{parent=Chirality}

A vectorlike gauge spectrum pairs complex <gauge group representations> $R$ and $\bar R$ among independent left-handed <Weyl spinors>. Such a pair permits a gauge-invariant <fermion> mass and occurs in a full charged <hypermultiplet> of four-dimensional $\mathcal N=2$ <extended supersymmetry>.

= Chiral gauge spectrum
{parent=Chirality}

= Chiral gauge spectra
{synonym}

A chiral gauge spectrum has independent left-handed <Weyl spinors> in complex <gauge group representations> without an obligatory conjugate pair of left-handed <Weyl spinors>. The right-handed antiparticles required by the <CPT theorem> do not themselves make a spectrum vectorlike.

= Weyl representation of the gamma matrices
{c}
{parent=Weyl spinor}

In the Weyl representation,
$$
\gamma^\mu=\begin{pmatrix}0&\sigma^\mu\\\bar\sigma^\mu&0\end{pmatrix},
\qquad
\gamma^5=\begin{pmatrix}-I&0\\0&I\end{pmatrix}.
$$
A Dirac spinor therefore splits into left- and right-handed two-component spinors.

= Majorana spinor
{c}
{parent=Weyl spinor}
{wiki}

A Majorana spinor equals its charge conjugate. In the Weyl representation it can be written $\psi_M=(u_L,i\sigma^2u_L^*)^T$ from one left-handed Weyl spinor.

= Majorana-Weyl spinor
{c}
{parent=Majorana spinor}
{title2=$\Gamma_*\psi=\pm\psi,\quad\psi^c=\psi$}

= Majorana-Weyl
{c}
{synonym}

A Majorana-Weyl spinor satisfies both a <Majorana spinor> reality condition and a <Weyl spinor> chirality condition when dimension and signature permit them. In ten-dimensional Lorentzian spacetime it has sixteen real components off shell and eight physical massless spin-one-half polarizations.

= Majorana Grassmann bilinear interchange
{c}
{parent=Majorana spinor}
{title2=$\bar\chi\epsilon=\bar\epsilon\chi$}

For Grassmann-odd spinors in two dimensions with $\bar\psi=\psi^TC$, $C^T=-C$ and $(\gamma^\mu)^TC=-C\gamma^\mu$, moving components past each other gives $\bar\chi\epsilon=\bar\epsilon\chi$ and $\bar\chi\gamma^\mu\gamma^\nu\epsilon=\bar\epsilon\gamma^\nu\gamma^\mu\chi$. The transpose of a two-gamma product reverses its order; the Grassmann sign and the antisymmetry of $C$ cancel. For $\delta\psi=B\gamma^\mu\partial_\mu X\epsilon$, this also gives $\delta\bar\psi=-B\bar\epsilon\gamma^\mu\partial_\mu X$. These identities fix the boundary variation in <worldsheet supersymmetry>; commuting spinors would not obey the same interchange rule.

= Majorana mass term
{c}
{parent=Majorana spinor}

A Majorana mass term pairs a Weyl field with itself and violates its continuous phase symmetry. Gauge invariance therefore permits it only when the field is neutral under every unbroken gauge charge.

= Dirac spinor
{c}
{parent=Dirac field}
{wiki}

A Dirac spinor transforms as $(1/2,0)\oplus(0,1/2)$, so it combines the two <Weyl spinor>[Weyl chiralities] into a parity-invariant representation.

= Euclidean adjoint Dirac Feynman rules
{c}
{parent=Dirac spinor}

Take Hermitian generators with $[T^a,T^b]=if^{abc}T^c$, $(T^b_{\mathrm{ad}})_{ac}=-if^{bac}$, and $D_\mu=\partial_\mu-igA_\mu^bT^b_{\mathrm{ad}}$. For $S_\psi=\int\bar\psi(\gamma_\mu D_\mu+m)\psi$ and $\{\gamma_\mu,\gamma_\nu\}=2\delta_{\mu\nu}$, the <Dirac propagator> is $\delta^{ac}(-i\not p+m)/(p^2+m^2)$. Expansion of $e^{-S_\psi}$ gives the fermion-gluon vertex $+ig\gamma_\mu(T^b_{\mathrm{ad}})_{ac}$. There is no two-gluon seagull vertex in this first-order kinetic action, and a closed fermion loop has a minus sign.

= Spinor representation of the Lorentz group
{c}
{parent=Dirac spinor}

With $S^{\mu\nu}=\frac14[\gamma^\mu,\gamma^\nu]$, the spinor representation is
$$
S[\Lambda]=\exp\left(\frac12\Omega_{\mu\nu}S^{\mu\nu}\right).
$$
It obeys $S^{-1}\gamma^\mu S=\Lambda^\mu{}_\nu\gamma^\nu$.

= Pseudo-unitarity of the spinor Lorentz representation
{parent=Spinor representation of the Lorentz group}
{title2=$S^\dagger\gamma^0S=\gamma^0$}

The spinor Lorentz matrices preserve the indefinite form defined by $\gamma^0$: $S^\dagger\gamma^0S=\gamma^0$. This follows from the gamma-matrix adjoint identities for infinitesimal generators and extends to finite connected transformations. Consequently the <Dirac adjoint> transforms with $S^{-1}$, even though a boost matrix is not ordinarily unitary.

= Infinitesimal transformation of a Dirac field
{parent=Spinor representation of the Lorentz group}
{title2=$\delta\Psi=-\omega^\mu{}_{\nu}x^\nu\partial_\mu\Psi-\tfrac i4\omega_{\mu\nu}\sigma^{\mu\nu}\Psi$}

For $\Lambda=1+\omega$ with antisymmetric lowered parameters, the spin matrix is $S=1-(i/4)\omega_{\mu\nu}\sigma^{\mu\nu}$, where $\sigma^{\mu\nu}=(i/2)[\gamma^\mu,\gamma^\nu]$. At transformed coordinates this is the full component transformation. At fixed coordinates an additional orbital term $-\omega^\mu{}_{\nu}x^\nu\partial_\mu\Psi$ appears.

= Spinor sign under a full spatial rotation
{parent=Spinor representation of the Lorentz group}

For a <Dirac spinor> a spatial rotation acts in each chiral block by a <matrix exponential> $e^{\pm i\theta\mathbf n\cdot\boldsymbol\sigma/2}$, depending on angle orientation. Since $(\mathbf n\cdot\boldsymbol\sigma)^2=I_2$ for a unit axis, the <Pauli matrix multiplication law> gives this exponential as $\cos(\theta/2)I_2\pm i\sin(\theta/2)\mathbf n\cdot\boldsymbol\sigma$. At $\theta=2\pi$ it is $-I_2$, and at $4\pi$ it is $I_2$. A <vector> returns already at $2\pi$. This describes the lift of the rotation loop to the <spin group>, rather than a single-valued representation of the ordinary rotation group on spinors.

= Lorentz generator from gamma-matrix commutators
{c}
{parent=Spinor representation of the Lorentz group}
{title2=$S^{\mu\nu}=\tfrac14[\gamma^\mu,\gamma^\nu]$}

= Lorentz generators from gamma-matrix commutators
{synonym}
{c}

The <Clifford algebra> gives $S^{\mu\nu}=\frac12\gamma^\mu\gamma^\nu-\frac12\eta^{\mu\nu}I$. Reordering one more <gamma matrix> gives $[S^{\mu\nu},\gamma^\rho]=\eta^{\nu\rho}\gamma^\mu-\eta^{\mu\rho}\gamma^\nu$. The <commutator derivation identity> then gives
$$
[S^{\mu\nu},S^{\rho\sigma}]=\eta^{\nu\rho}S^{\mu\sigma}-\eta^{\mu\rho}S^{\nu\sigma}+\eta^{\nu\sigma}S^{\rho\mu}-\eta^{\mu\sigma}S^{\rho\nu},
$$
so these matrices represent the <Lorentz algebra>. This convention has no extra factor of $i$; the spatial generators are $-\frac i2\epsilon^{jkl}\operatorname{diag}(\sigma^l,\sigma^l)$.

= Dirac mass term
{title2=$m\bar\psi\psi$}
{c}
{parent=Dirac spinor}

A Dirac mass term pairs left- and right-handed Weyl fields in conjugate Lorentz representations. Gauge invariance requires the two chiral fields to carry matching internal quantum numbers, directly or after multiplication by a scalar field that acquires a vacuum expectation value.

= Spin sum
{parent=Dirac field}
{wiki}

A spin sum adds a squared <scattering amplitude> over the unobserved <Spin angular momentum>[spins] of external particles. For an on-shell <Dirac spinor>, the completeness relations $\sum_s u_s(p)\bar u_s(p)=\not p+m$ and $\sum_s v_s(p)\bar v_s(p)=\not p-m$ turn the sum into a <gamma-matrix trace>.

= Spin average
{parent=Spin sum}

For an unpolarized initial state, average the squared <scattering amplitude> over its equally populated spin states. With two incoming spin-one-half particles this gives a factor $1/(2\cdot2)=1/4$. Unobserved final spins are summed rather than averaged.

= Gamma-matrix trace
{parent=Spin sum}

A gamma-matrix trace is a <matrix trace> of products of <gamma matrix>[gamma matrices]. In four spacetime dimensions, $\operatorname{tr}(\gamma^\mu\gamma^\nu)=4\eta^{\mu\nu}$ and $\operatorname{tr}(\gamma^\mu\gamma^\nu\gamma^\rho\gamma^\sigma)=4(\eta^{\mu\nu}\eta^{\rho\sigma}-\eta^{\mu\rho}\eta^{\nu\sigma}+\eta^{\mu\sigma}\eta^{\nu\rho})$.

= Gauge field
{parent=Relativistic quantum field}
{title2=$A_\mu$}

= Gauge potential
{synonym}

A <gauge field> is a connection associated with a local symmetry in a <gauge theory>. Its <gauge potential> defines the internal-symmetry contribution to a <covariant derivative>. For an Abelian <gauge field>, the <gauge field strength> is $F_{\mu\nu}=\partial_\mu A_\nu-\partial_\nu A_\mu$.

= Abelian p-form gauge field
{c}
{parent=Gauge field}
{title2=$F_{p+1}=dA_p$}

An Abelian $p$-form gauge potential $A_p$ is an antisymmetric tensor field with gauge equivalence $A_p\mapsto A_p+d\Lambda_{p-1}$ and field strength $F_{p+1}=dA_p$. Its field strength obeys the <Bianchi identity for an Abelian p-form>. The potential can have global gauge data even when its local field strength vanishes.

= Massless p-form gauge field
{parent=Abelian p-form gauge field}
{title2=$n_p(D)=\binom{D-2}{p}$}

A massless Abelian $p$-form in $D$-dimensional flat spacetime transforms on shell as an antisymmetric rank-$p$ tensor of the rotational <little group> $SO(D-2)$. Its physical state count is $\binom{D-2}{p}$. A compatible real self-duality constraint halves this count. A four-dimensional three-form has no local propagating polarization.

= Nondynamical four-dimensional three-form
{parent=Massless p-form gauge field}
{title2=$F_4=g_3^2q\,\operatorname{vol}_4$}

A three-form potential in four dimensions has no local massless polarizations. Its four-form field strength can be a constant multiple of the spacetime volume form. The equation $d(g_3^{-2}*F_4)=0$ leaves a constant flux parameter, which can affect vacuum energy despite supplying no local wave.

= Two-form scalar duality
{parent=Massless p-form gauge field}
{title2=$H=g_B^2 *da$}

Locally in four dimensions, a massless two-form is dual to one real <scalar field>. In signature $(-+++)$, the first-order density $-H^2/(12g_B^2)+\epsilon^{\mu\nu\rho\sigma}H_{\mu\nu\rho}\partial_\sigma a/6$ gives $H^{\mu\nu\rho}=g_B^2\epsilon^{\mu\nu\rho\sigma}\partial_\sigma a$. Eliminating $H$ in both terms produces $-g_B^2(\partial a)^2/2$, so the fixed-normalization kinetic coefficient is inverted. Global flux sectors and scalar periodicities need separate treatment.

= Bianchi identity for an Abelian p-form
{c}
{parent=Abelian p-form gauge field}
{title2=$dF=0$}

= Bianchi identities for an Abelian p-form
{c}
{synonym}

For an <Abelian p-form gauge field> with local field strength $F=dA$, the <exterior derivative> identity $d^2=0$ gives $dF=0$. In a first-order duality formulation, this identity can be enforced with a Lagrange multiplier, whose elimination restores the local gauge potential.

= Two-form gauge field
{parent=Gauge field}
{title2=$B\mapsto B+d\alpha$}

An antisymmetric tensor potential $B$ has gauge transformation $B\mapsto B+d\alpha$, where $\alpha$ is a one-form. Its field strength is the three-form $H=dB$, invariant because $d^2=0$. The <Kalb-Ramond field> is the string-theory example.

= Gauge-invariant string coupling to a two-form
{parent=Two-form gauge field}
{title2=$q\int_\Sigma X^*B$}

The coupling $q\int_\Sigma X^*B$ integrates the pullback of the <two-form gauge field> over the <string worldsheet>. Its gauge variation is $q\int_{\partial\Sigma}X^*\alpha$, by <Stokes theorem>. It therefore vanishes for a closed worldsheet, while an open surface requires suitable boundary fields or boundary-state transformations.

= Gauss law constraint in gauge theory
{c}
{parent=Gauge field}
{title2=$D_iE_i=\rho$}

The time component of a gauge field's equation of motion constrains the spatial fields and their momenta. In a <Yang-Mills theory> it has the covariant form $D_iE_i=\rho$; its Abelian form is <Gauss's law>. Time-dependent <collective coordinates> of a <gauge-theory soliton> require a temporal gauge field, or an equivalent horizontal projection, that satisfies this constraint before the kinetic metric is read from the action.

= Gauge boson
{parent=Gauge field}
{wiki}

A gauge boson is a spin-one quantum excitation of a gauge field. Each generator of the gauge group supplies one gauge-boson species before symmetry breaking.

= Gauge-boson mass matrix
{parent=Gauge boson}
{title2=$\mathcal L_{\mathrm{mass}}=\tfrac12 A_\mu^a(M^2)_{ab}A^{b\mu}$}

With canonically normalized <gauge field> <kinetic terms>, a gauge-boson mass matrix is the real symmetric matrix of coefficients in the displayed quadratic mass term. Its <eigenvalues> are squared physical <gauge boson> masses. For a complex <scalar field> vacuum $\Phi_0$ in the <Higgs mechanism>, $(M^2)_{ab}=2\operatorname{Re}[(g_aT_a\Phi_0)^\dagger(g_bT_b\Phi_0)]$. Thus for real $x_a$, $x^TM^2x=2\|\sum_a x_ag_aT_a\Phi_0\|^2\geq0$: it is a <Gram matrix> and a <positive semidefinite matrix>. An unbroken generator annihilates the <vacuum expectation value> and lies in its null space. Diagonalizing the matrix identifies the massive and massless field combinations.

= Gauge group
{parent=Gauge field}
{wiki}

The gauge group is the group of local internal symmetries. Its <Lie algebra> labels the gauge-boson species, while a matter field carries a representation of the group.

= Gauge group representation
{parent=Gauge group}

A gauge group representation specifies how a matter field transforms under a <gauge group>. It fixes the gauge charges and whether gauge-invariant mass terms can pair a field with a field in the conjugate <group representation>.

= Gauge coupling
{title2=$g$}
{parent=Gauge field}

A gauge coupling is the constant multiplying the gauge potential in the <gauge covariant derivative>. Its normalization depends on that of the Lie-algebra generators; each simple or Abelian factor of a gauge algebra may have an independent coupling.

= Wilson line
{c}
{parent=Gauge field}
{wiki}

A Wilson line is the path-ordered parallel transporter
$$
U(y,x)=\mathcal P\exp\left(ig\int_x^y A_\mu(z)\,dz^\mu\right).
$$
Under a gauge transformation $V$, it transforms as $U(y,x)\mapsto V(y)U(y,x)V(x)^{-1}$.

= Wilson-line gauge transformation
{c}
{parent=Wilson line}

Expanding a short <Wilson line> determines the inhomogeneous gauge-potential transformation. For $V=1+i\alpha$ and Hermitian generators,
$$
A_\mu\mapsto A_\mu+\frac1g\partial_\mu\alpha+i[\alpha,A_\mu].
$$

= Gauge-field transformation law
{parent=Gauge field}

For $D_\mu=\partial_\mu+A_\mu$ and a matter field transforming as $\phi\mapsto g\phi$, covariance of $D_\mu\phi$ requires
$$
A_\mu\mapsto gA_\mu g^{-1}-(\partial_\mu g)g^{-1}.
$$

= Gauge field strength
{title2=$F_{\mu\nu}$}
{parent=Gauge field}

= Gauge curvature
{synonym}

The curvature of a non-Abelian gauge field is $F_{\mu\nu}=\partial_\mu A_\nu-\partial_\nu A_\mu+[A_\mu,A_\nu]$. It satisfies $[D_\mu,D_\nu]=F_{\mu\nu}$ and transforms as $F_{\mu\nu}\mapsto gF_{\mu\nu}g^{-1}$.

= Gauge-theory Bianchi identity
{parent=Gauge field strength}
{title2=$D_{[\mu}F_{\nu\rho]}=0$}

The <Jacobi identity> for <gauge covariant derivatives> gives $D_{[\mu}F_{\nu\rho]}=0$. In four dimensions this is $D_\mu\widetilde F^{\mu\nu}=0$, with $\widetilde F^{\mu\nu}=\frac12\varepsilon^{\mu\nu\rho\sigma}F_{\rho\sigma}$. It holds independently of the <Yang-Mills equations>.

= Flat connection
{parent=Gauge field}
{wiki}

A flat connection has vanishing curvature. On a simply connected patch it is gauge equivalent to the zero connection, because the parallel-transport equation has path-independent solutions.

= Gauge covariant derivative
{title2=$D_\mu$}
{parent=Gauge field}
{wiki}

A gauge covariant derivative combines an ordinary derivative with a <gauge field> so that differentiating a field preserves its gauge-transformation law. In a matrix representation one common convention is $D_\mu=\partial_\mu-igA_\mu$.

= Gauge covariance of a scalar covariant derivative
{title2=$\delta_X(D_\mu\phi)=\epsilon R(X)D_\mu\phi$}
{parent=Gauge covariant derivative}

With $D_\mu=\partial_\mu+R(A_\mu)$ and $\delta_X A_\mu=-\epsilon\partial_\mu X+\epsilon[X,A_\mu]$, the derivative terms in $\delta_X(D_\mu\phi)$ cancel. The remaining terms combine using $R([X,A_\mu])=[R(X),R(A_\mu)]$, so the covariant derivative transforms in the same <Lie algebra representation> as the scalar. This convention absorbs the gauge coupling into the connection.

= Adjoint covariant derivative
{parent=Gauge covariant derivative}

On an adjoint-valued field $X$, the covariant derivative is $D_\mu X=\partial_\mu X+[A_\mu,X]$. If $X\mapsto gXg^{-1}$, then $D_\mu X\mapsto g(D_\mu X)g^{-1}$.

= Gauge invariance
{parent=Gauge field}
{wiki=Gauge_theory}

= Gauge symmetry
{synonym}

= Gauge-invariant
{synonym}

Gauge invariance is invariance under local changes of gauge representative. Gauge-invariant observables depend only on the physical gauge orbit.

= Gauge-invariant operator
{parent=Gauge invariance}

A local field combination is a gauge-invariant operator when its gauge representations and charges combine to a singlet. In an <effective field theory>, such operators may be multiplied by <Wilson coefficients> and inverse powers of a heavy scale. Multiplication by $\phi^\dagger\phi$ preserves the invariance of an existing Higgs Yukawa operator.

= Gauge redundancy
{parent=Gauge invariance}

Gauge redundancy means that multiple field configurations or polarization representatives describe the same physical state. Physical amplitudes must be unchanged when an external polarization is shifted by a pure-gauge component.

= Gauge orbit
{parent=Gauge invariance}
{wiki}

A gauge orbit is the set of all field configurations related by gauge transformations. Every point on one orbit represents the same physical gauge configuration.

= Abelian gauge theory
{c}
{parent=Gauge field}

An Abelian gauge theory has a commutative <gauge group>. Its <gauge field strength> has no commutator term, so the pure gauge Lagrangian has no cubic or quartic gauge-boson self-interactions.

= U(1) gauge symmetry
{c}
{parent=Abelian gauge theory}

For $D_\mu=\partial_\mu+ieA_\mu$, the local U(1) transformation is
$$
\psi\mapsto e^{ie\chi}\psi,
\qquad
A_\mu\mapsto A_\mu-\partial_\mu\chi.
$$

= Large gauge transformation
{parent=U(1) gauge symmetry}

A large gauge transformation does not approach the identity at the relevant spatial or asymptotic boundary. Although it leaves the local field strength unchanged, its boundary charge can act nontrivially on physical states and constrain soft limits through a <Ward identity>.

= Yang-Mills theory
{c}
{parent=Gauge field}
{wiki}

= Non-Abelian gauge theory
{c}
{synonym}

Yang-Mills theory is the non-Abelian gauge theory with field strength
$$
F_{\mu\nu}=\partial_\mu A_\nu-\partial_\nu A_\mu-ig[A_\mu,A_\nu].
$$
Under an infinitesimal gauge transformation it transforms covariantly as $\delta F_{\mu\nu}=-ig[F_{\mu\nu},\alpha]$.

= Non-Abelian gauge transformation
{parent=Yang-Mills theory}
{title2=$A_\mu\mapsto RA_\mu R^{-1}-g^{-1}(\partial_\mu R)R^{-1}$}

For the convention $D_\mu=\partial_\mu+gA_\mu$, a <gauge group> transformation $\Phi\mapsto R\Phi$ acts on the <gauge potential> by $A_\mu\mapsto RA_\mu R^{-1}-g^{-1}(\partial_\mu R)R^{-1}$. The <gauge field strength> transforms by conjugation. This makes $D_\mu\Phi$ transform in the same <gauge group representation> as $\Phi$.

= Yang-Mills equations
{c}
{parent=Yang-Mills theory}
{title2=$D_\mu F^{\mu\nu}=0$}

Stationarity of the <Yang-Mills action> in the absence of sources gives $D_\mu F^{\mu\nu}=0$, using the adjoint <gauge covariant derivative>. This dynamical equation differs from the geometric <gauge-theory Bianchi identity>.

= Yang-Mills theory coupled to an arbitrary scalar representation
{parent=Yang-Mills theory}
{c}
{title2=$\mathcal L=-B(\mathcal F,\mathcal F)/(4g^2)+h(D\Phi,D\Phi)-V$}

For a compact simple <gauge group>, use $B=-\kappa$ on its algebra and an averaged positive invariant <Hermitian form> $h$ on any supplied finite-dimensional complex representation. With anti-Hermitian connection and signature $(+---)$, $\mathcal L=-B(\mathcal F_{\mu\nu},\mathcal F^{\mu\nu})/(4g^2)+h(D_\mu\Phi,D^\mu\Phi)-V(\Phi)$ is <gauge-invariant> whenever $V$ is invariant. A real scalar representation instead has a symmetric form and kinetic prefactor $1/2$.

= Gauge-invariant scalar potential
{parent=Yang-Mills theory coupled to an arbitrary scalar representation}
{title2=$V(R(U)\Phi)=V(\Phi)$}

A real potential $V$ for a <scalar field> is invariant when $V(R(U)\Phi)=V(\Phi)$ for every gauge transformation. In a unitary representation, $m^2h(\Phi,\Phi)+\lambda h(\Phi,\Phi)^2$ is always an example. Particular representations may admit additional invariant interactions.

= Killing-form Yang-Mills Lagrangian
{parent=Yang-Mills theory}
{c}
{title2=$\mathcal L_{\rm YM}$}

For a real compact semisimple algebra, $B=-\kappa$ is a positive internal metric. With signature $(+---)$ and connection $D_\mu=\partial_\mu+A_\mu$, the healthy Lagrangian is $\mathcal L=-B(F_{\mu\nu},F^{\mu\nu})/(4g^2)=\kappa(F_{\mu\nu},F^{\mu\nu})/(4g^2)$. <Gauge invariance> follows from Killing-form invariance and $\delta_XF=\epsilon[X,F]$. The physical <Hamiltonian> density is $[B(E_i,E_i)+B(B_i,B_i)]/(2g^2)$ after imposing the <Gauss law constraint in gauge theory> and treating the spatial boundary flux appropriately. An Abelian factor needs a separate positive invariant metric because its <Killing form> vanishes.

= Killing-form Lagrangian for an adjoint scalar
{c}
{parent=Yang-Mills theory}

An adjoint scalar has <adjoint covariant derivative> $D_\mu\phi=\partial_\mu\phi+[A_\mu,\phi]$. A symmetric <invariant bilinear form on a Lie algebra> contracts the <gauge field strength> and scalar kinetic terms to give <gauge invariance>. On a <compact real form> the positive internal metric is $-\kappa$, because the <Killing form> itself is negative definite; complex scalar components require the associated Hermitian pairing. The Killing form is degenerate on Abelian factors, where another invariant metric is needed for a nondegenerate kinetic term.

= SU(2) gauge theory
{title2=$SU(2)$ gauge theory}
{c}
{parent=Yang-Mills theory}

An SU(2) gauge theory is a <Yang-Mills theory> with gauge group $SU(2)$. In its defining representation the generators are $T^a=\sigma^a/2$, and its structure constants are the Levi-Civita symbols $\epsilon^{abc}$.

= SU2 gauge self-interaction vertices
{c}
{parent=SU(2) gauge theory}

For field strength $F^a_{\mu\nu}=f^a_{\mu\nu}-g\epsilon^{abc}A_\mu^bA_\nu^c$, the square produces $\mathcal L_3=g\epsilon^{abc}(\partial_\mu A_\nu^a)A^{b\mu}A^{c\nu}$ and $\mathcal L_4=-g^2\epsilon^{abc}\epsilon^{ade}A_\mu^bA_\nu^cA^{d\mu}A^{e\nu}/4$. The cubic <Feynman vertex> has one derivative; the quartic one has none. No higher pure-gauge vertex occurs.

= Complete breaking by an SU2 scalar doublet
{title2=$SU(2)\to\{1\}$}
{parent=SU(2) gauge theory}

A nonzero complex fundamental doublet has trivial stabilizer in the gauged $SU(2)$. Its three angular directions are gauge directions, absorbed by the <Higgs mechanism>. For vacuum $\phi_0=(0,v/\sqrt2)^T$, the three gauge bosons have equal mass $gv/2$; one radial scalar remains. There is no unbroken photon-like gauge generator in this theory.

= Scalar interactions after complete SU2 breaking
{title2=$m_h^2=\lambda v^2$}
{parent=Complete breaking by an SU2 scalar doublet}

In <unitary gauge>, $\phi=(0,(v+h)/\sqrt2)^T$ gives $\frac{g^2}{8}(v+h)^2A_\mu^aA^{a\mu}$. With potential $\frac{\lambda}{2}(\phi^\dagger\phi-v^2/2)^2$, the physical scalar has $m_h^2=\lambda v^2$ and interactions $-\lambda vh^3/2-\lambda h^4/8$. This normalization matters when extracting both masses and identical-leg <Feynman vertices>.

= SU(2) gauge theory with an adjoint Higgs field
{c}
{parent=SU(2) gauge theory}
{title2=$SU(2)\to U(1)$}

A real <scalar field> in the <adjoint representation> can have a nonzero <vacuum expectation value>, leaving one massless and two massive <gauge bosons>. This breaking differs from <electroweak symmetry breaking> by a complex <Higgs doublet>.

= SU(3) gauge theory
{title2=$SU(3)$ gauge theory}
{c}
{parent=Yang-Mills theory}

An SU(3) gauge theory has eight gauge fields, one for each generator of $SU(3)$. A vacuum expectation value of one scalar in the <fundamental representation> generically leaves an $SU(2)$ subgroup unbroken.

= Fundamental-Higgs breaking of SU(3) to SU(2)
{title2=$SU(3)\to SU(2)$}
{c}
{parent=SU(3) gauge theory}

For a complex fundamental scalar with vacuum expectation value proportional to $(0,0,v)$, the stabilizer is the $SU(2)$ acting on the first two components. Five broken generators produce five massive vector bosons, while three gauge bosons remain massless and one radial scalar remains physical.

= Yang-Mills action
{title2=$S_{\rm YM}$}
{c}
{parent=Yang-Mills theory}

On an oriented Riemannian four-manifold, one positive Euclidean convention is
$$
S_{\rm YM}=-\frac1{g_{\rm YM}^2}
\int\operatorname{Tr}(F\wedge{}^\star F)
$$
for anti-Hermitian gauge fields.

= Yang-Mills gauge transformation
{c}
{parent=Yang-Mills theory}

For $D_\mu=\partial_\mu-igA_\mu$ and matter transforming as $\psi\mapsto U\psi$, a Yang-Mills connection transforms inhomogeneously while its curvature transforms by conjugation, $F_{\mu\nu}\mapsto UF_{\mu\nu}U^{-1}$.

= Infinitesimal gauge transformation with an anti-Hermitian connection
{parent=Yang-Mills gauge transformation}
{title2=$\delta\mathcal A_\mu=-\partial_\mu\epsilon+[\epsilon,\mathcal A_\mu]$}

With $D_\mu=\partial_\mu+\rho(\mathcal A_\mu)$ and $\Phi\mapsto R(U)\Phi$, a local $U=1+\epsilon$ gives $\delta\mathcal A_\mu=-\partial_\mu\epsilon+[\epsilon,\mathcal A_\mu]$, $\delta\Phi=\rho(\epsilon)\Phi$ and $\delta\mathcal F_{\mu\nu}=[\epsilon,\mathcal F_{\mu\nu}]$. Derivative terms cancel in $\delta(D_\mu\Phi)=\rho(\epsilon)D_\mu\Phi$. Here the <gauge coupling> is absorbed into the connection.

= Gluon propagator
{title2=$D_{\mu\nu}^{ab}(p)$}
{parent=Yang-Mills theory}

A gluon propagator is the inverse of the gauge-fixed quadratic Yang-Mills operator. It carries Lorentz and adjoint color indices and depends on the gauge-fixing condition, while gauge-invariant observables do not.

= Yang-Mills theta term
{c}
{title2=$\theta$ term}
{parent=Yang-Mills theory}
{wiki=Theta_vacuum}

In four dimensions the Yang-Mills theta term is proportional to $\theta\,\operatorname{Tr}(F_{\mu\nu}{}^\star F^{\mu\nu})$. Its density is a total derivative, but nontrivial gauge-field topology can make its spacetime integral physically relevant in the quantum theory.

= QCD theta angle
{title2=$\bar\theta$}
{c}
{parent=Yang-Mills theta term}

The physical QCD theta angle is $\bar\theta=\theta_{\rm QCD}+\arg\det(M_uM_d)$. An anomalous chiral quark rotation shifts the two terms oppositely, so only their sum is invariant.

= Strong CP problem
{c}
{parent=QCD theta angle}
{wiki}

The strong CP problem asks why the physical QCD theta angle is smaller than roughly $10^{-10}$ even though no Standard Model symmetry requires this small value.

= Peccei–Quinn theory
{c}
{parent=Strong CP problem}
{wiki}

= Peccei–Quinn mechanism
{c}
{synonym}

Peccei–Quinn theory promotes the physical QCD theta angle to a dynamical field through an anomalous global $U(1)$ symmetry. Nonperturbative QCD drives its vacuum expectation value to a CP-conserving minimum.

= Axion
{parent=Peccei–Quinn theory}
{wiki}

The axion is the pseudo-Goldstone boson of spontaneously broken Peccei–Quinn symmetry. Its QCD-generated potential dynamically relaxes the physical theta angle toward zero.

= Electroweak theta angle
{title2=$\theta_{\rm EW}$}
{parent=Yang-Mills theta term}

The electroweak theta angle multiplies the $SU(2)_L$ topological density. In the renormalizable Standard Model it is unphysical because an anomalous baryon-plus-lepton rephasing shifts it, but explicit baryon-plus-lepton violation can make an invariant combination observable.

= Chern-Simons current
{c}
{title2=$K^\mu$}
{parent=Yang-Mills theta term}
{wiki=Chern–Simons_form}

The Chern-Simons current satisfies $\partial_\mu K^\mu=\operatorname{Tr}(F_{\mu\nu}{}^\star F^{\mu\nu})$. It is not gauge invariant even though its divergence is.

= Quantum anomaly
{parent=Yang-Mills theory}
{wiki=Anomaly_(physics)}

A quantum anomaly is the failure of a classical symmetry to survive quantization because the functional measure or regulator cannot preserve it.

= Gauge anomaly
{parent=Quantum anomaly}
{wiki}

A gauge anomaly destroys a gauge redundancy needed to remove unphysical states and makes the quantum gauge theory inconsistent unless the anomaly cancels.

= Anomaly cancellation
{parent=Gauge anomaly}

Anomaly cancellation is the vanishing of every gauge and mixed gauge-gravitational anomaly after summing over all chiral fermions. It is a consistency condition for a quantum gauge theory.

= Standard Model anomaly cancellation
{c}
{parent=Anomaly cancellation}

In each Standard Model generation, quark and lepton hypercharges cancel the $SU(3)_c^3$, $SU(3)_c^2U(1)_Y$, $SU(2)_L^2U(1)_Y$, $U(1)_Y^3$, and mixed gauge-gravitational anomalies. The four left-handed $SU(2)_L$ doublets also avoid the global Witten anomaly.

= Witten SU(2) anomaly
{c}
{parent=Gauge anomaly}
{wiki=Global_anomaly}

The Witten SU(2) anomaly is a four-dimensional global gauge anomaly present for an odd number of left-handed Weyl fermion doublets. A consistent SU(2) gauge theory must have an even number modulo the corresponding representation index.

= Mixed gauge-gravitational anomaly
{parent=Gauge anomaly}

A mixed gauge-gravitational anomaly has one gauge-current insertion and two stress-tensor insertions. For a four-dimensional $U(1)$ symmetry its coefficient is the sum of the left-handed Weyl-fermion charges, with right-handed fermions counted as left-handed conjugates.

= Chiral anomaly
{parent=Quantum anomaly}
{wiki}

= Adler-Bell-Jackiw anomaly
{c}
{synonym}

The chiral anomaly is the quantum nonconservation of a classically conserved axial current in a gauge-field background.

= Axial current
{title2=$J_A^\mu$}
{parent=Chiral anomaly}
{wiki}

For massless Dirac fermions, the classical axial current is $J_A^\mu=\bar\psi\gamma^\mu\gamma^5\psi$. The chiral anomaly makes its divergence proportional to $F_{\mu\nu}{}^\star F^{\mu\nu}$.

= Chiral transformation
{parent=Axial current}

A chiral transformation rotates a Dirac field by $\psi\mapsto e^{i\alpha\gamma^5}\psi$. A massless Dirac kinetic term is invariant under a global rotation, while a mass term is not.

= 't Hooft anomaly
{c}
{parent=Quantum anomaly}
{wiki}

A 't Hooft anomaly is an obstruction to gauging a global symmetry. It is preserved by renormalization-group flow and constrains possible infrared phases.

= 't Hooft anomaly matching
{c}
{parent='t Hooft anomaly}
{wiki}

't Hooft anomaly matching requires the massless infrared degrees of freedom, topological sector, or symmetry-breaking pattern to reproduce every anomaly of an unbroken global symmetry measured in the ultraviolet theory.

= Gauge fixing
{parent=Gauge field}
{wiki}

Gauge fixing removes the degeneracy among gauge-equivalent field configurations so that the kinetic operator has an inverse propagator.

= Gauge-fixed action
{parent=Gauge fixing}

A <gauge-fixed action> supplements a gauge-invariant action with a term imposing the gauge condition and with <Faddeev-Popov ghost fields> representing the <Faddeev-Popov determinant>. A local gauge condition gives local differential operators and a local action.

= Dynamical gauge-fixing parameter
{parent=Gauge fixing}
{title2=$\delta S/\delta\xi=-(\partial\cdot A)^2/(2\xi^2)$}

If a real nonzero field $\xi(x)$ appears in the Lorentzian density $-F_{ab}F^{ab}/4+(\partial_aA^a)^2/(2\xi)$ and is varied, its algebraic <Euler-Lagrange equation> is $-(\partial_aA^a)^2/(2\xi^2)=0$. Hence it imposes the <Lorenz gauge> condition on real classical fields. Varying $A_b$ gives $\partial_aF^{ab}-\partial^b[(\partial\cdot A)/\xi]=0$, including derivatives of $\xi$. This constrained field theory differs from holding the gauge parameter fixed in a <Gaussian functional integral>.

= Nonlinear Abelian gauge fixing
{parent=Gauge fixing}

For an <Abelian gauge theory> with $A_\mu\mapsto A_\mu+\partial_\mu\alpha$, a nonlinear gauge functional $F[A]$ has <Faddeev-Popov determinant> $\det(\delta F[A+\partial\alpha]/\delta\alpha)$. This operator can depend on $A$ even though the <adjoint representation> of $U(1)$ is trivial. Consequently <Faddeev-Popov ghost fields> need not decouple in a nonlinear Abelian gauge, unlike a field-independent linear gauge condition.

= Complex quadratic Abelian gauge condition
{parent=Nonlinear Abelian gauge fixing}
{title2=$F[A]=\partial_\mu A_\mu+iA_\mu A_\mu$}

The formal variation of this condition is $\delta F=(\partial^2+2iA\cdot\partial)\alpha$. Thus its <Faddeev-Popov ghost field> operator can be chosen as $\mathcal O_A=-\partial^2-2iA\cdot\partial$. With $D_\mu=\partial_\mu+iA_\mu$, one has $D^2=\partial^2+2iA\cdot\partial+iF[A]$, so $\mathcal O_A=-D^2$ on the formal gauge slice. The <reality obstruction for a complex Euclidean gauge condition> prevents interpreting this slice as an ordinary real Euclidean <gauge fixing> without an additional complex-contour prescription.

= Ghost-scalar determinant cancellation in a complex quadratic gauge
{parent=Complex quadratic Abelian gauge condition}

A massless complex bosonic <charged scalar field> with $D_\mu=\partial_\mu+iA_\mu$ contributes $[\det(-D^2)]^{-1}$ to a Gaussian background-field integral. A <Faddeev-Popov ghost field> pair contributes $\det\mathcal O_A$. On the formal <complex quadratic Abelian gauge condition>, $\mathcal O_A=-D^2$, so the two determinants cancel and their effective-action contributions sum to zero. Use matching regulators, boundary conditions and removed zero modes. This is a formal cancellation of closed loops; the <reality obstruction for a complex Euclidean gauge condition> precludes deducing that physical <scalar quantum electrodynamics> has no quantum effects.

= Reality obstruction for a complex Euclidean gauge condition
{parent=Complex quadratic Abelian gauge condition}

For real $A_\mu$ in positive-definite Euclidean signature, $\partial\cdot A$ is real and $A_\mu A_\mu\ge0$. Therefore $\partial\cdot A+iA^2=0$ forces $A^2=0$, and hence $A=0$. Its only real gauge orbit is the zero-field orbit; a field with nonzero electromagnetic curvature cannot be gauge-transformed to zero. Consequently this complex equation is not an admissible <gauge fixing> for general Hermitian Euclidean <U(1) gauge symmetry> configurations. A formal complexified treatment needs a specified contour and cannot be justified by a real delta-functional insertion.

= Scalar-dependent gauge fixing
{parent=Gauge fixing}

= Ghost vertex in scalar-dependent gauge fixing
{parent=Scalar-dependent gauge fixing}

= Ghost vertices in scalar-dependent gauge fixing
{synonym}

The <Faddeev-Popov ghost field> couples to both the <gauge field> and the <adjoint scalar field>. In a convention with $D_\mu=\partial_\mu+[A_\mu,\cdot]$, the ghost action after <integration by parts> contains $f^{abc}(\partial_\mu\bar c^a)A_\mu^b c^c$ and $f^{abc}(n\cdot\partial\bar c^a)\phi^b c^c/\sqrt2$.

= Free adjoint-scalar propagator in scalar-dependent gauge fixing
{parent=Scalar-dependent gauge fixing}

For <gauge fixing> with $F^a=\partial\cdot A^a+(n\cdot\partial\phi^a)/\sqrt2$, integrating out the <Nakanishi-Lautrup field> produces gauge-scalar mixing. The <Schur complement> of the free gauge-field kernel cancels the extra $(n\cdot p)^2/2$ in the scalar kernel, leaving $\langle\phi^a(p)\phi^b(-p)\rangle=\delta^{ab}/p^2$. This is independent of the gauge vector $n$, but still depends on the full momentum $p$.

= Zero mode in field theory
{parent=Gauge fixing}

= Zero modes in field theory
{synonym}

A zero mode of a quadratic field operator is a nonzero field variation that the operator maps to zero. Infinitesimal pure-gauge variations are zero modes of an unfixed gauge kinetic operator, preventing it from having an inverse propagator.

= Gribov ambiguity
{c}
{parent=Gauge fixing}
{wiki}

The Gribov ambiguity is the nonperturbative failure of a local gauge condition to intersect every gauge orbit exactly once. Distinct gauge-equivalent fields satisfying the same condition are Gribov copies.

= Covariant gauge
{parent=Gauge fixing}
{wiki}

Adding $-(\partial_\mu A^\mu)^2/(2\alpha)$ gives a family of Lorentz-covariant gauges; $\alpha=1$ is <Feynman gauge>.

= Inversion of the gauge-fixed Maxwell kinetic operator
{parent=Covariant gauge}
{title2=$K^{\mu\nu}D^F_{\nu\rho}=i\delta^\mu{}_{\rho}$}

<Gauge fixing> removes the noninvertible longitudinal direction of the Maxwell quadratic action. In a gauge with parameter $\xi$, the Fourier kernel is $-k^2\eta^{\mu\nu}+(1-\xi^{-1})k^\mu k^\nu$. Multiplying its inverse by $i$ gives the <photon propagator>, with the <Feynman i-epsilon prescription>. At $\xi=1$ the answer is $-i\eta_{\mu\nu}/(k^2+i0)$.

= Positive-sign covariant gauge-fixing inverse
{parent=Covariant gauge}
{title2=$G_{ab}=-\eta_{ab}/p^2+(1+\xi)p_ap_b/(p^2)^2$}

For fixed $\xi\ne0$, the Lorentzian quadratic density $-F^2/4+(\partial\cdot A)^2/(2\xi)$ has momentum kernel $K^{ab}=-p^2\eta^{ab}+(1+1/\xi)p^ap^b$. At $p^2\ne0$ its inverse, defined by $K^{ac}G_{cb}=\delta^a_b$, is $G_{ab}=-\eta_{ab}/p^2+(1+\xi)p_ap_b/(p^2)^2$. A vacuum <photon propagator> is $iG$ with the appropriate <Feynman i-epsilon prescription>. The usual parameter in the negative-sign <covariant gauge> term is $\alpha=-\xi$, so <Feynman gauge> is $\xi=-1$.

= Longitudinal gauge propagator contraction
{parent=Positive-sign covariant gauge-fixing inverse}
{title2=$p^aG_{ab}=\xi p_b/p^2$}

For the <positive-sign covariant gauge-fixing inverse>, $p^aG_{ab}=\xi p_b/p^2$. It is not identically zero for fixed $\xi\ne0$ and is a nonzero vector at a nonzero non-null <four-momentum>. Null <momenta> require the chosen pole prescription, and individual zero components are possible. The <Landau gauge> limit suppresses this longitudinal part. The <Ward identity> removes longitudinal contributions from physical conserved-current amplitudes.

= Feynman gauge
{c}
{parent=Covariant gauge}
{wiki}

= Feynman-gauge Maxwell kinetic density after a boundary-term subtraction
{c}
{parent=Feynman gauge}
{title2=$\mathcal L'=-\tfrac12\partial_\mu A_\nu\partial^\mu A^\nu$}

With $D=\partial_\mu A^\mu$, expand the <Maxwell Lagrangian> and its <Feynman gauge> term to find
$$
-\frac14F_{\mu\nu}F^{\mu\nu}-\frac12D^2
=-\frac12\partial_\mu A_\nu\partial^\mu A^\nu+\partial_\mu K^\mu,
\qquad K^\mu=\frac12(A_\nu\partial^\nu A^\mu-A^\mu D).
$$
Commuting <partial derivatives> verifies the divergence identity. Both densities give $\Box A^\mu=0$ under the usual variational <boundary conditions>. Their <canonical momentum> components differ: the original density gives $-F^{0\nu}-\eta^{0\nu}D$, whereas the subtracted density gives $-\dot A^\nu$. Thus a free-photon <momentum> expansion using the latter requires a stated boundary-term convention.

= Gupta-Bleuler quantization
{c}
{parent=Feynman gauge}
{wiki=Gupta–Bleuler_formalism}

Gupta-Bleuler quantization keeps Lorentz covariance by first quantizing all four photon polarizations in an indefinite inner-product space. The condition $(\partial_\mu A^\mu)^{(+)}|\mathrm{phys}\rangle=0$, followed by quotienting null states, leaves the two transverse physical polarizations.

= Gupta-Bleuler null-state quotient
{c}
{parent=Gupta-Bleuler quantization}
{title2=$\mathcal H_{\mathrm{phys}}=\ker C/\operatorname{rad}(\ker C)$}

Choose $C_{\mathbf p}=a^0_{\mathbf p}-a^3_{\mathbf p}$ with the standard temporal and longitudinal <polarization vectors>. The physical pre-space is the common kernel of all $C_{\mathbf p}$. In each regulated mode, $a^0$ acts on creator <polynomials> as $-\partial_{a^{0\dagger}}$ and $a^3$ as $\partial_{a^{3\dagger}}$, so the constraint kernel consists of transverse creator <polynomials> and <polynomials> in $C^\dagger=a^{0\dagger}-a^{3\dagger}$. Since $[C,C^\dagger]=0$, these latter excitations remain constrained. They are orthogonal to every constrained state because $\langle\Phi|C^\dagger=\langle C\Phi|=0$. Quotienting this <radical of a Hermitian form> leaves only the transverse <bosonic Fock space> with a positive <inner product>. The constraint alone gives a <positive semidefinite Hermitian form>; quotienting removes its null directions. If starting from finite-particle creator <polynomials>, take the <Hilbert space completion> of this positive quotient to obtain the physical <Hilbert space>.

= Covariant photon Fock space
{parent=Gupta-Bleuler quantization}

The covariant free-photon oscillator construction uses four <polarization vectors> and $[a^\lambda,a^{\lambda'\dagger}]=-\eta^{\lambda\lambda'}$. Starting from a positive <Fock vacuum>, it induces an <indefinite Hermitian form> on the multiparticle state space: the temporal oscillator has negative norm while the three spatial oscillators have positive norm. It is therefore not the physical positive <Hilbert space>. The <Gupta-Bleuler null-state quotient> selects a positive physical space with two transverse photon polarizations. Continuum <momentum> oscillators and their states are understood after smearing or finite-volume regularization.

= Negative-norm photon state
{parent=Covariant photon Fock space}

For a temporal <photon> <wave packet> $|f,0\rangle=\int d^3p\,f(\mathbf p)a_{\mathbf p}^{0\dagger}|0\rangle/(2\pi)^3$, the oscillator <commutator> gives $\langle f,0|f,0\rangle=-\int d^3p\,|f(\mathbf p)|^2/(2\pi)^3<0$ when $f\ne0$. This is the negative direction of the <indefinite Hermitian form>, not the divergent normalization of an unsmeared <momentum eigenstate>. It is excluded by the physical condition in <Gupta-Bleuler quantization>.

= Gauge-boson propagator
{parent=Covariant gauge}

For the Euclidean covariant gauge term $(\partial_\mu A^\mu)^2/(2\xi)$, the free gauge-boson propagator is
$$
D_{\mu\nu}(k)=\frac1{k^2}\left(\delta_{\mu\nu}+(\xi-1)\frac{k_\mu k_\nu}{k^2}\right).
$$

= Feynman-gauge photon propagator
{c}
{parent=Gauge-boson propagator}

At $\xi=1$, the Euclidean photon propagator is $D_{\mu\nu}(k)=\delta_{\mu\nu}/k^2$. Its Lorentz-index contraction simplifies loop numerators in <quantum electrodynamics>.

= Transverse projector of a vector field
{title2=$P_T^{\mu\nu}$}
{parent=Gauge-boson propagator}

At a nonzero Euclidean <momentum> $k$, the <transverse projector of a vector field> is $P_T^{ab}=\delta^{ab}-k^ak^b/k^2$. In a Lorentzian <Minkowski metric>, its mixed-index form is $(P_T)^a{}_b=\delta^a_b-p^ap_b/p^2$, defined for $p^2\ne0$. It obeys $P_Tp=0$ and $P_T^2=P_T$. A nonzero null <four-momentum> is insufficient for this particular decomposition because the denominator vanishes.

= Longitudinal projector of a vector field
{title2=$P_L^{\mu\nu}$}
{parent=Gauge-boson propagator}

At a nonzero Euclidean <momentum> $k$, the <longitudinal projector of a vector field> is $P_L^{ab}=k^ak^b/k^2$. Its Lorentzian mixed-index form is $(P_L)^a{}_b=p^ap_b/p^2$ for non-null <four-momentum>. Together with the <transverse projector of a vector field>, it obeys $P_T+P_L=I$, $P_TP_L=0$, $P_T^2=P_T$, and $P_L^2=P_L$. This formula is not a pointwise decomposition at null <four-momentum>.

= Faddeev-Popov determinant
{c}
{title2=$\Delta_{\mathrm{FP}}$}
{parent=Gauge fixing}
{wiki}

The Faddeev-Popov determinant is the functional Jacobian
$$
\Delta_{\mathrm{FP}}[A]=\det\left(\frac{\delta G(A^\alpha)}{\delta\alpha}\right)_{G=0}
$$
that compensates for the change from integration along a gauge orbit to a gauge-fixing condition $G(A)=0$. It can be represented by a path integral over a <Faddeev-Popov ghost field> pair.

= Faddeev-Popov operator
{c}
{parent=Faddeev-Popov determinant}
{title2=$\mathcal M=\delta\chi[A+D\omega]/\delta\omega\vert_{\omega=0}$}

For a gauge condition $\chi[A]=0$, the linearization of $\chi[A+D\omega]$ in the gauge parameter is the Faddeev-Popov operator. Its determinant is represented by <Faddeev-Popov ghost fields>.

= Faddeev-Popov ghost field
{c}
{parent=Faddeev-Popov determinant}
{wiki=Faddeev–Popov_ghost}

= Faddeev-Popov ghost
{c}
{synonym}

A Faddeev-Popov ghost field is a Grassmann-valued scalar field whose Gaussian functional integral represents the <Faddeev-Popov determinant>. Ghosts occur only on internal lines and cancel unphysical gauge-field contributions.

= Faddeev-Popov antighost field
{c}
{parent=Faddeev-Popov ghost field}
{title2=$b^a=\bar c^a,\quad\operatorname{gh}(b)=-1$}

= Antighost field
{synonym}

The Grassmann-odd partner of the <Faddeev-Popov ghost field> in the gauge-fixing determinant representation. In an auxiliary-field <BRST symmetry> description it obeys $sb=h$, where $h$ is the <Nakanishi-Lautrup field>. Its ordinary spacetime derivative remains odd, which matters when moving an anticommuting BRST parameter past it.

= Ghost number
{parent=Faddeev-Popov ghost field}
{title2=$\operatorname{gh}(c)=1,\quad\operatorname{gh}(\bar c)=-1$}

The additive grading assigns ghost number $+1$ to $c$ and $-1$ to $\bar c$. Physical observables have ghost number zero. This grading is distinct from spin or the <adjoint representation>.

= Ghost loop
{parent=Faddeev-Popov ghost field}

A ghost loop is a closed cycle of ghost propagators in a <Feynman diagram>. Its Grassmann statistics contribute an additional minus sign.

= Axial gauge
{parent=Gauge fixing}
{wiki}

An axial gauge imposes $n^\mu A_\mu=0$ for a fixed vector $n^\mu$. Its Faddeev-Popov operator is $n^\mu D_\mu$; on the strict gauge slice its gauge-field-dependent part vanishes, so its ghosts decouple.

= Axial-gauge pole prescription
{parent=Axial gauge}

The inverse of $n\cdot\partial$ and the <axial-gauge propagator> are singular when $n\cdot k=0$. A pole prescription and residual-gauge boundary conditions specify these inverses. They must be compatible throughout a calculation; formal ghost decoupling alone does not specify them.

= Axial-gauge propagator
{parent=Axial gauge}
{title2=$D_{\mu\nu}(k;n)$}

In strict <axial gauge>, the <gauge-boson propagator> obeys $n^\mu D_{\mu\nu}(k)=0$ and contains poles at $n\cdot k=0$. Its contraction with conserved external currents removes the gauge-choice terms. Consistent calculations require an <axial-gauge pole prescription>.

= Temporal gauge
{parent=Gauge fixing}
{wiki}

Temporal gauge sets the time component of a gauge potential to zero, $A_0=0$. The mixed curvature components then reduce to time derivatives of the spatial connection, $F_{0i}=\partial_0A_i$.

= BRST symmetry
{c}
{parent=Gauge fixing}
{wiki}

BRST symmetry is a nilpotent fermionic symmetry of a gauge-fixed action. Its differential $s$ replaces an infinitesimal gauge parameter by the ghost field and satisfies $s^2=0$.

= Left-acting BRST differential
{parent=BRST symmetry}
{title2=$s(UV)=(sU)V+(-1)^{|U|}U(sV),\quad s^2=0$}

Writing a transformation as $\delta\Phi=\epsilon s\Phi$ with the odd parameter on the left defines this graded derivation convention. For Yang-Mills fields, $sA=Dc$, $sc=-[c,c]/2$, $sb=h$, and $sh=0$. The <Jacobi identity> supplies nilpotence; the placement of the odd parameter controls signs in a <Noether current>.

= Right-acting BRST differential
{parent=BRST symmetry}
{title2=$Q_R$}

A <right-acting BRST differential> obeys the right <graded Leibniz rule>. This convention permits $Q\phi=[c,\phi]$ together with $Qc=-[c,c]/2$. Using a left <graded Leibniz rule> with those same two signs does not give a <nilpotent operator>; one of the signs must change.

= Nakanishi-Lautrup field
{title2=$B^a$}
{c}
{parent=BRST symmetry}
{wiki}

= BRST transformation
{c}
{synonym}

The Nakanishi-Lautrup field is an auxiliary bosonic field used to write gauge fixing as a BRST-exact term. Its algebraic field equation produces the usual quadratic gauge-fixing term.

= BRST charge
{title2=$Q$}
{c}
{parent=BRST symmetry}
{wiki=BRST_quantization}

The BRST charge generates <BRST transformation>[BRST transformations]. It is Grassmann odd and nilpotent, $Q^2=0$.

= Yang-Mills BRST Noether current
{c}
{parent=BRST charge}
{title2=$j^\mu=-F^{\mu\nu a}(D_\nu c)^a/g^2+h^a(D^\mu c)^a+\tfrac12(\partial^\mu b^a)f^a{}_{bc}c^bc^c$}

For the left-parameter convention and ghost kinetic term $(\partial b)Dc$, localization of the <BRST symmetry> yields this conserved <Noether current>. Subtract the total-derivative contribution $K^\mu=h^a(D^\mu c)^a$ in the Lagrangian variation. The <BRST charge> is the spatial integral of $j^0$. The antighost term has the displayed sign because the parameter and <antighost field> derivative anticommute.

= BRST Ward identity
{c}
{parent=BRST charge}

For a BRST-invariant action and functional measure, the expectation of a BRST-exact insertion vanishes: $\langle QX\rangle=0$, subject to anomaly and boundary qualifications.

= Gauge-fixing parameter independence from BRST symmetry
{parent=BRST Ward identity}
{title2=$\partial_\xi\mathcal L=s(-b^ah^a/2)$}

For auxiliary-field covariant gauge fixing, varying the parameter inserts a <BRST-exact operator>. Its correlations with separated physical <BRST-closed> operators vanish by the <BRST Ward identity>. Normalized physical correlators are therefore independent of the parameter under the invariant-measure and boundary assumptions.

= Graded BRST Ward identity
{parent=BRST Ward identity}
{title2=$\langle[Q_{\mathrm{BRST}},\mathcal O\}\prod_iG_i\rangle=0$}

An invariant path-integral measure and <BRST symmetry> action imply this identity when every separated insertion $G_i$ is <BRST-closed>. The bracket is the <graded commutator>, so it becomes an anticommutator for an odd $\mathcal O$. The arbitrary operator need not itself be closed. Exact insertions therefore decouple from physical correlators.

= Gauge-fixing fermion
{title2=$\Psi$}
{parent=BRST symmetry}
{wiki}

A gauge-fixing fermion is a Grassmann-odd functional whose <BRST transformation> $s\Psi$ supplies the gauge-fixing and ghost terms. Nilpotence gives $s(s\Psi)=0$ immediately.

= BRST cohomology
{c}
{parent=BRST symmetry}
{wiki}

BRST cohomology identifies physical states and observables with BRST-closed objects modulo BRST-exact ones. A change of gauge-fixing fermion changes the action by a BRST-exact term and therefore leaves BRST-cohomology classes unchanged when the measure has no BRST anomaly.

= BRST-closed operator
{c}
{parent=BRST cohomology}

= BRST-closed
{c}
{synonym}

A BRST-closed operator $O$ satisfies $QO=0$. It represents a physical observable when it is not also BRST exact.

= BRST-exact operator
{c}
{parent=BRST cohomology}

A BRST-exact operator has the form $O=QX$. It is BRST closed by nilpotence and represents the zero class in BRST cohomology.

= BRST-exact insertions in physical correlation functions
{c}
{parent=BRST-exact operator}
{title2=$\langle(s\mathcal O)\prod_iG_i\rangle=0$}

When separated operators $G_i$ are <BRST-closed>, the <graded BRST Ward identity> makes an exact insertion vanish. This is the correlator form of identifying closed representatives that differ by exact terms in <BRST cohomology>. The statement presupposes an invariant measure and suitable boundary conditions.

= Quantized field
{parent=Quantum field theory}

= Fock space
{c}
{parent=Quantized field}
{title2=$\mathcal F_\pm(H)$}

For a one-particle <Hilbert space> $H$, the bosonic or fermionic <Fock space> is $\mathcal F_\pm(H)=\bigoplus_{n=0}^{\infty}H^{\otimes_\pm n}$, using symmetric or antisymmetric tensor powers. Its zeroth summand is the one-dimensional vacuum sector. <Creation operators> and <annihilation operators> change particle number. The <bosonic Fock space> and <fermionic Fock space> implement the corresponding exchange statistics.

= Occupation number
{parent=Fock space}
{title2=$n_k$}

The eigenvalue of a mode number operator counts excitations in that mode. <Bosonic occupation numbers> range over all nonnegative integers; <fermionic occupation numbers> are zero or one.

= Fermionic occupation number
{parent=Occupation number}
{title2=$n^F_k\in\{0,1\}$}

For $\{f,f^\dagger\}_+=1$ and $f^2=0$, the <occupation number> operator $f^\dagger f$ has eigenvalues zero and one. This gives the single-mode exclusion principle.

= Bosonic occupation number
{parent=Occupation number}
{title2=$n^B_k\in\mathbb Z_{\geq0}$}

For $[a,a^\dagger]=1$, the <occupation number> operator $a^\dagger a$ has eigenvalues $0,1,2,\ldots$ on the bosonic oscillator <Fock space>.

= Operator-valued distribution
{parent=Quantized field}
{title2=$\phi(f)=\int\phi(x)f(x)\,d^dx$}

An operator-valued distribution assigns a <linear operator> on a common dense domain to each <smooth> <test function> of <compact support> $f$, continuously and linearly in the appropriate weak sense. A <quantized field> is generally of this type: $\phi(x)$ is formal point notation, while the smeared $\phi(f)$ has operator meaning. Equal-time <canonical commutation relations> and <momentum> oscillators are interpreted after smearing, which avoids treating a <Dirac delta distribution> or a coincident field as a finite ordinary operator. Products at coincident points can need further regularization even when individual smeared fields are defined.

= Mode expansion of a free field
{parent=Quantized field}

A free field is expanded in positive- and negative-frequency plane waves multiplied by annihilation and creation operators.

= Coincident-point vacuum field variance
{parent=Mode expansion of a free field}
{title2=$\langle X(\mathbf x)^2\rangle=\int d\log k\,\Delta_X(k)$}

For a free field in the annihilation-operator vacuum, the coincident two-point function is its mode-power integral. Unsmeared vacuum products generally have an ultraviolet divergence. A finite band or spatial smearing defines a regulated variance without altering the meaning of a mode-by-mode <dimensionless power spectrum>.

= Positive-frequency part of a quantum field
{parent=Mode expansion of a free field}
{title2=$A^{(+)}$}

For a free field, its positive-frequency part consists of the annihilation terms multiplying $e^{-i\omega t}$ with $\omega>0$; its negative-frequency part consists of the creation terms. The sign refers to positive <energy>, even though the exponential has a minus sign. Imposing the <Lorenz gauge> only through $(\partial\cdot A)^{(+)}|\mathrm{phys}\rangle=0$ is the subsidiary condition in <Gupta-Bleuler quantization>, not an operator identity for the whole divergence.

= Wronskian normalization
{parent=Mode expansion of a free field}

The <canonical commutation relation> fixes the antisymmetric product of a mode function $f$ and its canonical-momentum mode $g$. In the convention $[a,a^\dagger]=1$, one commonly has $fg^*-f^*g=i$.

= Fock state
{parent=Quantized field}
{c}
{wiki}

A Fock state has specified occupation numbers. Applying creation operators to the vacuum constructs multiparticle momentum eigenstates.

= Fock vacuum
{c}
{parent=Fock state}
{title2=$|0\rangle$}

The oscillator vacuum is annihilated by all <annihilation operators>. Acting with <creation operators> constructs <Fock states>. In single-string quantization this is the unexcited string, not the empty spacetime state.

= Intrinsic parity
{title2=$\gamma_p$}
{parent=Quantized field}
{wiki}

Intrinsic parity is the phase by which a field transforms under spatial inversion after its spacetime argument is reflected. For a Hermitian scalar field it is $+1$ or $-1$.

= Ghost field
{parent=Quantized field}

A ghost field in the general dynamical sense has a kinetic term with the wrong sign and makes the Hamiltonian unbounded below or produces negative-norm states. This use is distinct from a <Faddeev-Popov ghost field>, which is an auxiliary Grassmann field for gauge fixing.

= Transverse-longitudinal decomposition
{parent=Quantized field}

A vector field can be decomposed into a divergence-free part and a gradient, $V_\mu=A_\mu+\partial_\mu\pi$ with $\partial_\mu A^\mu=0$, subject to boundary conditions and zero-mode qualifications.

= Constraint equation in field theory
{parent=Quantized field}

= Constraint equations in field theory
{synonym}

A constraint equation follows by varying a field component that enters an action without its own time derivative. It restricts initial data or determines a nondynamical field instead of supplying an independent propagating degree of freedom.

= Gaussian functional integral
{parent=Quantized field}

A Gaussian functional integral has an exponent quadratic in a bosonic field. Completing the square expresses its source dependence through the inverse quadratic operator, the propagator.

= Gaussian evaluation of a free scalar generating functional
{parent=Gaussian functional integral}
{c}
{title2=$Z_0[J]=\exp[-\tfrac12\int J\Delta_FJ]$}

Completing the square in the free scalar <path integral> gives a quadratic exponential in the source. If $\Delta_F$ includes the factor $i$ in the momentum-space <Feynman propagator>, the normalized Minkowski functional is $\exp[-\int J\Delta_FJ/2]$. Two source derivatives with their $1/i^2$ factor recover $\Delta_F$, while higher derivatives give <Wick contractions>.

= Functional determinant
{parent=Gaussian functional integral}
{wiki}

A Gaussian path integral over a bosonic field with quadratic operator $A$ produces a factor proportional to $(\det A)^{-1/2}$. When $A$ depends on background fields, this functional determinant contributes to their quantum effective action.

= Grassmann Gaussian integral
{parent=Quantized field}

A Grassmann Gaussian integral satisfies $\int D\bar\psi D\psi\,e^{-\bar\psi A\psi}=\det A$. Adding Grassmann sources and completing the square produces an exponent containing $A^{-1}$.

= Grassmann field
{c}
{parent=Grassmann Gaussian integral}

= Grassmann-valued field
{c}
{synonym}

A Grassmann field takes values in a Grassmann algebra, so its components anticommute. A closed loop of Grassmann propagators acquires a minus sign in perturbation theory.

= Quantum field theory propagator
{parent=Quantized field}
{wiki=Propagator}

A propagator is a time-ordered two-point function and a Green function for the quadratic kinetic operator with an $i\epsilon$ boundary prescription.

= Feynman propagator
{parent=Quantum field theory propagator}
{wiki}

The Feynman propagator is the time-ordered Green function selected by the $i\epsilon$ prescription.

= Scalar Feynman propagator pole prescription
{parent=Feynman propagator}
{title2=$\widetilde\Delta_F(p)=i/(p^2-m^2+i0)$}

With $\Delta_F=\langle0|T\{\phi(x)\phi(y)\}|0\rangle$, for a mode of positive <energy> $E>0$ the <Feynman i-epsilon prescription> puts poles $+E-i0$ and $-E+i0$ in opposite half-planes. The <energy> <contour integral> closes clockwise below for positive time separation and counterclockwise above for negative separation. The <residue theorem> gives $e^{-iE|t|}/(2E)$ in either case. Thus the four-dimensional Fourier integral has numerator $i$ and denominator $p^2-m^2+i0$. The integral is understood as a <distribution> limit, not an ordinary absolutely convergent integral; changing the definition to $i\Delta_F$ would change the displayed normalization.

= Derivative jump of a free scalar time-ordered two-point function
{parent=Scalar Feynman propagator pole prescription}
{title2=$g'_E(0^+)-g'_E(0^-)=-i$}

At fixed spatial <momentum> with mode <energy> $E>0$, $g_E(t)=e^{-iE|t|}/(2E)$ is continuous, with derivatives $-i/2$ and $+i/2$ at zero from opposite sides. Hence its second <distributional derivative> has a $-i\delta(t)$ term, while away from zero $g''_E+E^2g_E=0$. Therefore $(\partial_t^2+E^2)g_E=-i\delta(t)$ and, after the spatial <Fourier transform>, $(\Box+m^2)\Delta_F=-i\delta^4$. This checks the propagator numerator, contour signs and equal-time <canonical commutation relations> independently of residue calculation.

= Feynman i-epsilon prescription
{title2=$i\epsilon$ prescription}
{c}
{parent=Feynman propagator}

= i-epsilon prescription
{synonym}
{c}

The Feynman $i\epsilon$ prescription displaces poles or tilts an integration contour by an infinitesimal imaginary part. It selects vacuum boundary conditions and damps oscillatory integrals at infinite time.

= Wightman function
{parent=Quantum field theory propagator}
{wiki}

A Wightman function is an unordered vacuum correlation function. For a free scalar mode $f_k(t)$, the positive-frequency two-point function is $G^>(t_1,t_2;k)=f_k(t_1)f_k^*(t_2)$.

= Dirac propagator
{parent=Quantum field theory propagator}

The free Dirac propagator is $S_F(p)=i(\not p+m)/(p^2-m^2+i\epsilon)$ and inverts the Dirac kinetic operator with Feynman boundary conditions.

= Photon propagator
{parent=Quantum field theory propagator}
{wiki}

In a covariant gauge, the photon propagator is the inverse of the gauge-fixed Maxwell kinetic operator.

= Radiation-gauge photon propagator
{parent=Photon propagator}
{title2=$\widetilde D^F_{ij}(k)=i(\delta_{ij}-k_ik_j/|\mathbf k|^2)/(k^2+i0)$}

Time-ordering the <canonical transverse photon field> gives a propagator with numerator equal to the spatial transverse projector and denominator $k^2+i0$. Its two energy poles encode forward propagation of the positive-frequency term and backward propagation of the negative-frequency term. This is the reduced free-field propagator; it is distinct from the covariant four-potential propagator.

= Perturbative quantum field theory
{parent=Quantum field theory}

= Radiative correction
{parent=Perturbative quantum field theory}

A radiative correction is a quantum correction beyond the classical or tree approximation, represented in <perturbative quantum field theory> by <Feynman diagrams> of positive <loop order>. Such corrections can alter a <scalar potential> and its derivatives; symmetries and <renormalization> constrain which changes are allowed.

= Perturbation series
{parent=Perturbative quantum field theory}

A perturbation series expands a correlation function or scattering amplitude in powers of interaction couplings. In quantum field theory it is commonly asymptotic because diagram counts grow factorially and nonperturbative effects are invisible to every finite order.

= Dyson series
{c}
{parent=Perturbative quantum field theory}
{wiki}

The Dyson series expands the interaction-picture time-evolution operator as
$$
S=T\exp\!\left(-i\int d^4x\,\mathcal H_{\mathrm{int}}(x)\right)
=\sum_{n\geq0}\frac{(-i)^n}{n!}\int d^4x_1\cdots d^4x_n\,T\!\left\{\prod_{j=1}^n\mathcal H_{\mathrm{int}}(x_j)\right\}.
$$

= Differentiation of the Dyson time-ordered exponential
{parent=Dyson series}
{title2=$i\partial_tU=H_I(t)U$}

For bounded norm-continuous <Hamiltonian operators> on $[t_0,t]$, the <Dyson series> has an $n$-th term bounded by $M^n|t-t_0|^n/n!$, so it converges in operator norm. In its nested-integral form, differentiating the outer endpoint puts $H_I(t)$ on the left of the $(n-1)$-fold term. Summing yields $\partial_tU=-iH_I(t)U$ and $U(t_0,t_0)=I$. If $H_I$ is Hermitian, differentiating $U^\dagger U$ proves unitarity. For unbounded field operators the same algebra is a formal perturbative identity unless domain and convergence assumptions or a regulator justify the steps. Evolution backward uses inverse, anti-time-ordered evolution.

= Wick theorem
{c}
{parent=Perturbative quantum field theory}
{wiki=Wick's_theorem}

Wick theorem expresses a time-ordered product of free fields as its normal-ordered product plus the sum over every possible contraction. It turns the <Dyson series> into <Feynman diagrams>.

= Free scalar four-point function
{parent=Wick theorem}
{title2=$\langle T\phi_1\phi_2\phi_3\phi_4\rangle$}

For a centered free <real scalar field>, its vacuum <time-ordered product> has expectation $\langle T\phi_1\phi_2\phi_3\phi_4\rangle=G_{12}G_{34}+G_{13}G_{24}+G_{14}G_{23}$, where $G_{ij}$ is the <Feynman propagator> between insertions $i,j$. These are the three complete pairings in the <Wick theorem>. All remaining <normal-ordered products> have zero vacuum expectation. No fermionic interchange signs occur for a bosonic field.

= Vanishing odd correlators of a free Gaussian field
{title2=$\langle\widehat f_1\cdots\widehat f_{2n+1}\rangle=0$}
{parent=Wick theorem}

A centered free field in a Gaussian vacuum has only pair contractions, so every odd correlator vanishes by <Wick theorem>. Equivalently, number parity reverses the field while preserving the vacuum. This statement applies to smeared or regulated fields; it does not assert that unregulated coincident products exist. Interactions or a nonzero field mean can invalidate it.

= Time ordering
{parent=Wick theorem}
{wiki}

Time ordering places operators with later time arguments to the left. Interchanging fermionic operators also contributes the sign of the required permutation.

= Time-ordered product
{parent=Time ordering}
{title2=$T\{\mathcal O_1\cdots\mathcal O_n\}$}

A <time-ordered product> sorts operator insertions from latest time on the left to earliest time on the right. Reordering odd <fermionic operators> contributes the sign of their permutation. For bosonic $A,B$, $T[A(t)B(t')]=\theta(t-t')A(t)B(t')+\theta(t'-t)B(t')A(t)$. Vacuum expectations of free <time-ordered products> are evaluated by the <Wick theorem>.

= Normal ordering
{parent=Wick theorem}
{wiki}

Normal ordering places creation operators to the left of annihilation operators. For free fields it removes vacuum self-contractions from a composite operator.

= Normal-ordered product
{parent=Normal ordering}
{title2=$:\mathcal O_1\cdots\mathcal O_n:$}

A <normal-ordered product> of free-field oscillator operators places all <creation operators> to the left of all <annihilation operators>, including the <fermionic sign> required to reorder fermions. Its vacuum expectation vanishes if any nontrivial oscillator factors remain. The <Wick theorem> expresses a free-field <time-ordered product> through these products and all <Wick contractions>.

= Wick contraction
{c}
{parent=Wick theorem}

A Wick contraction replaces a selected pair of free fields by their two-point function. Summing pairings computes Gaussian correlation functions and supplies the propagator lines and combinatorial factors of a <Feynman diagram>.

= Fermionic sign
{parent=Perturbative quantum field theory}

A fermionic sign is a factor $-1$ produced whenever two Grassmann-odd fields or fermionic creation and annihilation operators are interchanged. Relative signs between diagrams must be computed after putting every external fermion operator into one common order.

= Wick rotation
{c}
{parent=Perturbative quantum field theory}
{wiki}

A Wick rotation deforms a time or energy contour from real to imaginary values, converting suitable Minkowski integrals into Euclidean integrals while respecting pole prescriptions.

= Euclidean action
{c}
{parent=Wick rotation}
{title2=$S_E$}

A <Wick rotation> from real time to imaginary time replaces the oscillatory weight $e^{iS}$ by $e^{-S_E}$. For a real scalar with positive kinetic normalization, $S_E=\int[\tfrac12(\partial\phi)^2+V(\phi)]\,d^dx$. Positivity of its kinetic term does not ensure convergence if the potential is unbounded below. Fermionic integrals instead use independent <Grassmann fields> and need not define a positive measure.

= Generating functional
{title2=$Z[J]$}
{parent=Perturbative quantum field theory}
{wiki}

A generating functional is a <path integral> with a source $J$ coupled to a field. Its <functional derivatives> with respect to $J$ generate time-ordered correlation functions.

= Source differentiation inserts time-ordered field operators
{parent=Generating functional}
{title2=$G_r=\left.i^{-r}\delta^r Z/\delta J(x_1)\cdots\delta J(x_r)\right|_{J=0}$}

A <functional derivative> of the source term in a Minkowski <scalar field path integral> inserts $i\phi(x)$. Time-slicing identifies the resulting product with the <time-ordered product> of field operators. Dividing by the zero-source vacuum functional removes disconnected vacuum diagrams. The normalized formula has one factor $1/i$ for every insertion.

= Normalized vacuum generating functional
{parent=Generating functional}
{title2=$\widehat Z[J]=Z[J]/Z[0]$}

For a vacuum <generating functional> $Z[J]$, normalization gives $\widehat Z[J]=Z[J]/Z[0]$ and $\widehat Z[0]=1$. <Functional derivatives> of $\widehat Z$ give normalized <vacuum expectation values>. The <linked-cluster theorem> shows that this ratio cancels <vacuum Feynman diagrams>, while $-i\log\widehat Z[J]$ gives connected correlators. Components attached to external insertions are not removed merely by the normalization.

= Connected generating functional
{title2=$W[J]=-i\log Z[J]$}
{parent=Generating functional}

The logarithm of a <generating functional> generates connected correlation functions because its cumulant expansion removes products of disconnected vacuum components.

= Feynman diagram
{c}
{parent=Perturbative quantum field theory}
{wiki}

A Feynman diagram records a term in the perturbative expansion: edges represent propagators, vertices represent interactions, and loops represent unconstrained momentum integrals.

= Disconnected Feynman diagram
{parent=Feynman diagram}

A <Feynman diagram> with more than one connected component. Products of component contributions exponentiate; taking the logarithm of a Gaussian perturbation expansion removes disconnected products and keeps <connected Feynman diagrams>.

= Triangle Feynman diagram
{parent=Feynman diagram}

A triangle <Feynman diagram> has a loop of three propagators joining three vertices. In <six-dimensional cubic scalar field theory> it gives a one-loop three-point proper vertex. Attaching a cubic tree vertex makes a four-point connected diagram, but the connecting propagator is a bridge, so the latter is a <one-particle-reducible Feynman diagram>.

= Supergraph
{parent=Feynman diagram}

A supergraph is a <Feynman diagram> formulated with <superfield> propagators and vertices in <superspace>. Performing its spinor-derivative algebra organizes perturbative corrections into <superspace integration> terms and provides a proof of the local <superpotential> <non-renormalization theorem>.

= Box Feynman diagram
{parent=Feynman diagram}

A one-loop <Feynman diagram> with four vertices joined into a box. Two <W bosons> and two internal <quarks> give a short-distance contribution to <neutral D-meson mixing>.

= Interaction vertex
{parent=Feynman diagram}

= Feynman vertex
{c}
{synonym}

An <interaction vertex> joins the field insertions from one interaction term in a <Feynman diagram>. Its number of incident lines is the number of fields in that term, and its momentum-space <Feynman rule> includes the interaction coefficient and <momentum conservation>.

= Squared-amplitude diagram
{parent=Feynman diagram}

A squared-amplitude diagram consists of an amplitude graph and its complex-conjugate graph, with matching external states. Summing an external fermion spin sews the associated spinor indices using a <fermion spin sum>. The resulting closed spinor chains become <gamma-matrix traces>. This notation represents probabilities and must be distinguished from adding another physical interaction loop to the amplitude.

= Feynman rule
{c}
{parent=Feynman diagram}

Feynman rules translate each diagram into momentum-space factors for propagators, vertices, external states, momentum conservation and loop integration.

= Four-leg vertex of a factorial-normalized scalar interaction
{parent=Feynman rule}
{title2=$V_4=i\lambda$}

An interaction $\mathcal L_{\rm int}=+\lambda\phi^4/4!$ produces a four-leg momentum-space vertex $i\lambda$. The $4!$ assignments of external legs cancel the coefficient factorial. With a negative interaction sign the vertex is $-i\lambda$. Graph <Feynman-diagram symmetry factors> are a separate combinatorial issue.

= Feynman-diagram symmetry factor
{parent=Feynman rule}

The symmetry factor of a Feynman diagram divides out permutations of indistinguishable internal lines and vertices that leave the labeled diagram unchanged. It is the order of the corresponding automorphism group after external labels are fixed.

= Feynman parameter
{c}
{parent=Feynman rule}
{wiki}

A Feynman parameter combines propagator denominators; for example,
$$
\frac1{AB}=\int_0^1\frac{dx}{[xA+(1-x)B]^2}.
$$
After a momentum shift, this often converts a loop integral into a rotationally symmetric one.

= Schwinger parameterization
{c}
{parent=Feynman parameter}
{title2=$A^{-v}=\Gamma(v)^{-1}\int_0^\infty t^{v-1}e^{-tA}dt$}

For $\operatorname{Re}A>0$ and $\operatorname{Re}v>0$, rescaling the defining <gamma function> integral proves this representation, with compatible complex-power branches. It turns denominator powers into exponential factors, making <Gaussian integrals> possible. Combining two parameters by their sum and ratio gives the usual <Feynman parameter> identity. Outside its convergence domain a further continuation or pole prescription is needed.

= Vacuum Feynman diagram
{c}
{parent=Feynman diagram}

= Vacuum bubble
{synonym}

A vacuum Feynman diagram has no external lines and contributes a source-independent factor to the partition function. Taking the logarithm retains only connected vacuum diagrams, while normalizing $Z[0]=1$ removes all of them.

= Cancellation of vacuum bubbles
{parent=Vacuum Feynman diagram}

A perturbative correlator factors into an exponential of connected <vacuum Feynman diagrams> times a sum of <Feynman diagrams> attached to external insertions. Dividing its <path integral> by the vacuum <generating functional> at zero source cancels that exponential, including all disconnected products of vacuum components. The corresponding <operator formalism> uses the same denominator in the vacuum expectation of the <Dyson series>. This leaves externally disconnected correlators intact; the <connected generating functional> removes their products.

= Vacuum energy
{parent=Vacuum Feynman diagram}
{title2=$E_0$}
{wiki}

= Linked-cluster theorem
{parent=Vacuum Feynman diagram}
{wiki}

The linked-cluster theorem states that the logarithm of a vacuum generating functional is the sum of connected vacuum diagrams. Exponentiating that sum reconstructs arbitrary products of disconnected connected components with their correct factorials.

= Tadpole diagram
{parent=Feynman diagram}

A tadpole diagram contains an internal line that begins and ends at the same interaction vertex. In a scalar theory it commonly gives a momentum-independent correction to a mass or lower-order coupling.

= Tadpole subtraction
{parent=Tadpole diagram}

A linear <counterterm> can be chosen so that the renormalized one-point function, or equivalently the chosen <vacuum expectation value>, is zero. This cancels attached <tadpole diagrams> in the perturbative expansion about that vacuum. In a massive <six-dimensional cubic scalar field theory>, the one-loop proper tadpole is $t=gI_1/2$; without subtraction the stationary background shifts by $v=-t/m^2+O(g^3)$ at one-loop order. One must specify this convention when comparing reducible exchange diagrams or mass corrections.

= Bubble diagram
{parent=Feynman diagram}

A bubble diagram has two interaction vertices joined by two internal propagators to form one loop. In scalar quartic theory it gives the one-loop correction to the four-point coupling.

= Scalar bubble integral
{parent=Bubble diagram}
{title2=$B(P)$}

The <loop momentum> integral with two massive <quantum field theory propagators> is
$$
B(P)=\int\frac{d^dq}{(2\pi)^d}\frac{1}{(q^2+m^2)((q+P)^2+m^2)}.
$$
Its momentum dependence gives a one-loop <self-energy> in <cubic scalar field theory> and a quartic effective vertex in <quartic scalar field theory>.

= Sunset diagram
{parent=Feynman diagram}

A sunset diagram is a two-point diagram with two interaction vertices joined by three internal propagators. Its dependence on the external momentum produces <wave-function renormalization> in scalar quartic theory.

= Tree-level Feynman diagram
{parent=Feynman diagram}

A tree-level diagram has no loops and gives the leading classical contribution allowed by the interaction vertices.

= Loop order
{parent=Feynman diagram}

The loop order of a connected <Feynman diagram> is the number $L=I-V+1$ of independent momentum cycles, where $I$ is the number of internal lines and $V$ the number of vertices. Each loop introduces an unconstrained momentum integral.

= Loop momentum
{parent=Loop order}

A loop momentum is an independent momentum integrated over in a <Feynman diagram>. Momentum conservation leaves one independent loop momentum for each cycle counted by the <loop order>.

= Connected Feynman diagram
{parent=Feynman diagram}

A connected Feynman diagram has a path between every pair of its vertices. Normalized correlation functions discard disconnected vacuum bubbles, while full correlators can still factor into disconnected components carrying external insertions.

= One-particle-reducible Feynman diagram
{parent=Connected Feynman diagram}

= 1PR Feynman diagram
{c}
{synonym}

A connected <Feynman diagram> is one-particle reducible if cutting one internal propagator disconnects it. These diagrams occur in <connected correlation functions> but do not contribute to a <quantum effective action> defined by a <Legendre transform>. For a strict <Wilsonian effective action>, a bridge also cannot be a high-momentum propagator when the sum of external momenta on one side lies below the integrated shell.

= Cubic-scalar exchange corrections at zero momentum
{parent=One-particle-reducible Feynman diagram}

In the <six-dimensional cubic scalar field theory>, use full external-propagator amputation and <tadpole subtraction>. The exchange part of the zero-momentum four-point amplitude is
$$
\mathcal A_{4,\mathrm{exchange}}=\frac{3g^2}{m^2}+\frac{6g^4}{m^2}I_3+\frac{3g^4}{2m^4}I_2+O(\text{two loops}).
$$
The triangle corrects either end of each of three exchanges, and the symmetry factor of an internal bubble is $1/2$. Equivalently insert $\Gamma^{(3)}=g+g^3I_3$ and $\Gamma^{(2)}=m^2-g^2I_2/2$ into $3[\Gamma^{(3)}]^2/\Gamma^{(2)}$. Without tadpole subtraction, the shift $v=-gI_1/(2m^2)$ adds $3g^4I_1/(2m^6)$. None of these is an additional local 1PI quartic coupling; zero-momentum bridges are also excluded from a strict high-mode Wilsonian shell. If bare rather than full external propagators are amputated, external-line decorations additionally contribute $6g^4I_2/m^4$, and $6g^4I_1/m^6$ without tadpole subtraction. Every displayed term has <mass dimension> minus two. In a renormalized expansion, the exchange diagrams also receive the <counterterm> insertions $6g\delta g/m^2-3g^2\delta m^2/m^4$. Without enforcing a zero one-point function, a linear <counterterm> $\delta h\phi$ contributes $3g^3\delta h/m^6$ through the background shift; choosing $\delta h=-gI_1/2$ cancels the attached tadpole. A kinetic <counterterm> is proportional to the exchanged momentum squared, so its insertion vanishes at this zero-momentum point.

= One-particle-irreducible Feynman diagram
{title2=1PI Feynman diagram}
{parent=Connected Feynman diagram}
{wiki=One-particle-irreducible}

A one-particle-irreducible Feynman diagram is a connected <Feynman diagram> that remains connected after any one internal line is cut. The vertices of the <quantum effective action> generate precisely these diagrams.

= One-particle-irreducible correlation function
{title2=$\Gamma^{(n)}$}
{parent=One-particle-irreducible Feynman diagram}

An $n$-point one-particle-irreducible correlation function is obtained by differentiating the <quantum effective action> $n$ times. Its diagrammatic expansion contains the amputated one-particle-irreducible diagrams with $n$ external legs.

= One-particle-irreducible two-point vertex
{parent=One-particle-irreducible correlation function}
{title2=$\widetilde\Gamma^{(2)}(p)=\widetilde G(p)^{-1}$}

The second derivative of a Legendre effective action is the inverse connected two-point kernel. In a translation-invariant scalar theory it is $\widetilde\Gamma^{(2)}(p)=\widetilde G(p)^{-1}$. Its loop expansion contains the reference inverse propagator, any separately displayed counterterms, and the <self-energy>. A generic amputated connected higher-point function is not the same as a one-particle-irreducible vertex.

= Amputation of external propagators
{parent=One-particle-irreducible correlation function}

= Amputation
{synonym}

= Self-energy
{title2=$\Pi(p^2)$}
{parent=One-particle-irreducible correlation function}
{wiki}

A self-energy is the loop contribution to a one-particle-irreducible two-point function. Repeated self-energy insertions form a geometric series that shifts the propagator pole and changes its residue.

= Dyson resummation
{c}
{parent=Self-energy}

Dyson resummation sums repeated self-energy insertions as a geometric series. If $G_0$ is the free propagator and $\Sigma$ is the proper two-point insertion, then
$$
G=G_0+G_0\Sigma G_0+\cdots=(G_0^{-1}-\Sigma)^{-1}
$$
for the convention in which an insertion contributes $+\Sigma$ inside the series.

= Fermion self-energy
{parent=Self-energy}

The fermion self-energy is the amputated one-particle-irreducible two-point function $\Sigma(\not p)$. It corrects both the coefficient of $\not p$ and the fermion mass and thereby shifts the pole and residue of the exact propagator.

= Pole mass
{title2=$m_{\rm pole}$}
{parent=Self-energy}
{wiki}

The pole mass is the value of the invariant momentum at a pole of the full propagator. It is independent of the renormalization scheme, whereas a running mass parameter generally depends on the <renormalization scale>.

= Four-point one-particle-irreducible correlation function
{title2=$\Gamma^{(4)}$}
{parent=One-particle-irreducible correlation function}

The four-point one-particle-irreducible correlation function is the fourth functional derivative of the <quantum effective action>. It defines a renormalized quartic coupling after evaluation at the kinematic point specified by a <renormalization condition>.

= Derivative expansion of a four-point vertex
{parent=Four-point one-particle-irreducible correlation function}

The small-external-momentum expansion of a <four-point one-particle-irreducible correlation function> generates a local quartic term followed by four-field terms with derivatives, for example $\phi^2(\partial\phi)^2$ and higher-derivative operators, modulo <integration by parts>. This is an analytic expansion when there is an appropriate gap and regulator smoothness. A sharp projector on every internal momentum can instead generate nonanalytic boundary artifacts; then the local expansion is a smooth-regulator or fixed-loop-cutoff convention, not an automatic theorem about the entire sharp-cutoff vertex.

= Schwinger-Dyson equation
{c}
{parent=Perturbative quantum field theory}
{wiki=Schwinger–Dyson_equation}

A Schwinger-Dyson equation follows from invariance of a path integral under a change of integration variable and relates correlation functions through the field equations and contact terms.

= LSZ reduction formula
{c}
{parent=Perturbative quantum field theory}
{wiki}

The LSZ reduction formula obtains an S-matrix element from a time-ordered correlation function by amputating external propagators, taking external momenta on shell, and multiplying by pole-residue factors and external wavefunctions.

= Equivalence theorem for field redefinitions
{parent=Perturbative quantum field theory}

The equivalence theorem states that an invertible local field redefinition leaves on-shell S-matrix elements unchanged, although it changes off-shell correlation functions and individual interaction vertices.

= Field redefinition
{parent=Equivalence theorem for field redefinitions}

A field redefinition is an invertible change of variables among fields. Local field redefinitions can rearrange higher-derivative operators without changing on-shell scattering amplitudes.

= Redundant operator
{parent=Field redefinition}
{wiki}

A redundant operator is an interaction that can be removed by an invertible local field redefinition or by integration by parts together with the equations of motion. It can change off-shell formulas while leaving the on-shell S-matrix unchanged.

= Quantum effective action
{title2=$\Gamma$}
{parent=Perturbative quantum field theory}
{wiki=Effective_action}

The quantum effective action is the <Legendre transform> of the generating functional of connected correlation functions. Its functional derivatives are the <one-particle-irreducible Feynman diagram>[one-particle-irreducible vertex functions].

= Finite higher-point scalar vertex
{parent=Quantum effective action}

A finite higher-point scalar vertex is a term in the <quantum effective action> with more external scalar fields than the original interaction, whose coefficient remains finite after <renormalization>. It can survive removal of an <ultraviolet cutoff> even when no corresponding independent <counterterm> is needed. Its coefficient is then predicted by the renormalized parameters and external momenta.

= One-loop scalar effective action
{parent=Quantum effective action}
{title2=$\Gamma_1[\phi]$}

For a canonically normalized <real scalar field> with potential $V$, the one-loop <quantum effective action> is $\Gamma_1[\phi]=\tfrac12\operatorname{Tr}\log[-\partial^2+V''(\phi)]$, with a suitable <regularization in quantum field theory>. Expanding this <functional determinant> generates <one-particle-irreducible Feynman diagrams> with arbitrarily many external fields.

= Gaussian fluctuation approximation
{parent=One-loop scalar effective action}

Expanding an <action> about a classical stationary field and retaining its <second variation> gives a Gaussian <path integral>. Its nonzero fluctuation modes determine a <functional determinant> and a <one-loop soliton mass correction>. Symmetry-generated <zero modes in field theory> must be removed from that determinant and integrated as <collective coordinates>.

= Wilsonian effective action
{c}
{parent=Quantum effective action}

A Wilsonian effective action is obtained by integrating out field modes above a chosen momentum scale while retaining lower-momentum modes as backgrounds. Its local couplings change with the scale according to a <renormalization-group flow>.

= Wilsonian rescaling of a scalar coupling
{c}
{parent=Wilsonian effective action}
{title2=$\kappa^\prime=(\kappa+\Delta\kappa)b^{m+n(d-2)/2-d}/(1+\Delta Z)^{n/2}$}

After shell integration and $x^\prime=bx$, restore the <kinetic term> by $\phi^\prime=b^{-(d-2)/2}\sqrt{1+\Delta Z}\,\phi$. An operator with $n$ scalar fields and $m$ derivatives scales by $b^{m+n(d-2)/2-d}$. In four dimensions this gives the displayed factor with $m+n-4$; the general exponent is $m+n(d-2)/2-d$. The coupling shift and <wavefunction renormalization> arise before the rescaling.

= Connected shell-contraction expansion
{parent=Wilsonian effective action}
{title2=$-\log\langle e^{-V}\rangle_>=\langle V\rangle_>-\tfrac12\langle V^2\rangle_{>,c}+\cdots$}

The <cumulant expansion> of the shell integral keeps precisely <connected Feynman diagrams> built from <shell-restricted scalar propagators>. The external low fields are backgrounds, not propagators. Connected graphs with bridges can contribute, so the expansion is not limited to <one-particle-irreducible Feynman diagrams>.

= Momentum-support exclusion for Wilsonian bridge diagrams
{parent=Connected shell-contraction expansion}
{title2=$C_>*\phi_<=0$}

If a bridge is the only line carrying momentum away from a low external field, tadpole pairs do not change its momentum and the <shell-restricted scalar propagator> has zero support there. A bridge attached to a product such as $\phi_<^3$ can survive because the sum of three low momenta can enter the shell. This matters before a local <derivative expansion>.

= Momentum-shell decomposition of a scalar field
{parent=Wilsonian effective action}
{title2=$\Phi=\phi_<+\phi_>$}

Split <Fourier transform> modes into a low-momentum ball and its disjoint shell. The quadratic scalar action has no cross term between these supports. Integrating the shell at fixed low field defines the lower-cutoff <Wilsonian effective action>.

= Renormalization
{parent=Perturbative quantum field theory}
{wiki}

Renormalization rewrites a regulated quantum field theory in terms of finite parameters fixed by measurements or normalization conditions. Dependence on the regulator is absorbed into <counterterms>, while dependence on the chosen <renormalization scale> is governed by a <beta function (physics)>.

= Zero-momentum renormalized mass
{parent=Renormalization}
{title2=$R=\widetilde\Gamma^{(2)}(0)$}

In a specified scalar-field normalisation, one can define a renormalized squared mass by $R=\widetilde\Gamma^{(2)}(0)$. Its value depends on the renormalization prescription and is not automatically the pole mass in a theory with momentum-dependent <self-energy>. A reference propagator $(p^2+R)^{-1}$ and <mass counterterm> then organise perturbation theory around that prescription.

= Renormalizable quantum field theory
{parent=Renormalization}

= Renormalizability
{synonym}

A renormalizable <quantum field theory> permits ultraviolet divergences to be absorbed into a fixed finite set of parameters and field normalizations. In ordinary four-dimensional perturbative <power counting in quantum field theory>, its interaction coefficients have nonnegative <mass dimension>.

= Counterterm
{parent=Renormalization}
{wiki}

A counterterm is a local term added to a regulated <Lagrangian density> to cancel ultraviolet divergences and impose chosen <renormalization conditions>. Field-strength, mass and coupling counterterms respectively adjust propagator normalization, pole position and interaction strength.

= Counterterm closure
{parent=Counterterm}

A family of actions is closed under <counterterms> when all required divergent local terms can be absorbed into that family. This does not imply that the full <quantum effective action> contains no additional finite effective <interaction vertices> or terms in its <derivative expansion>.

= Mass counterterm
{title2=$\delta m$}
{parent=Counterterm}

A mass counterterm cancels the divergent part of a self-energy that has the tensor structure of a mass term. Its finite part depends on the renormalization scheme, while an on-shell scheme chooses it so that the renormalized mass equals the pole mass.

= Renormalization condition
{parent=Renormalization}

A renormalization condition defines a renormalized field or parameter by prescribing a correlation function at a chosen kinematic point. Changing that point changes the renormalized parameters while leaving physical predictions invariant.

= Momentum-subtraction scheme
{parent=Renormalization condition}
{wiki=Renormalization_scheme#Momentum_subtraction_schemes}

A momentum-subtraction scheme defines renormalized masses, fields, and couplings by fixing correlation functions at specified external momenta.

= On-shell renormalization scheme
{parent=Renormalization condition}
{wiki=On-shell_scheme}

The on-shell renormalization scheme fixes masses at physical propagator poles, fixes field normalizations by pole residues, and fixes couplings through amplitudes evaluated with external particles on their mass shells.

= Callan-Symanzik equation
{c}
{parent=Renormalization}
{wiki}

The Callan-Symanzik equation states that a renormalized correlation function is independent of the arbitrary renormalization scale after the couplings and field normalization run. For an $n$-point function it has the form
$$
\left(\mu\partial_\mu+\beta(g)\partial_g+n\gamma_\phi\right)\Gamma_R^{(n)}=0
$$
up to sign conventions for the anomalous dimension.

= Callan-Symanzik equation with additive renormalization
{c}
{parent=Callan-Symanzik equation}
{title2=$\mathcal D G_R=a$}

= Inhomogeneous Callan-Symanzik equation
{synonym}

An additive subtraction can produce a source in the renormalization-group equation. If $G_R=G_B-a\log(\Lambda_0/\Lambda)$ and the bare correlator is invariant under $\mathcal D=\Lambda\partial_\Lambda+\beta(g)\partial_g$, then $\mathcal D G_R=a$. A vanishing operator <anomalous dimension> does not remove this source.

= Continuum limit of a quantum field theory
{parent=Renormalization}

A continuum limit removes an ultraviolet cutoff while keeping specified physical observables finite. In renormalization-group language, the bare trajectory must approach an ultraviolet fixed point and the correlation length measured in cutoff units must diverge.

= Asymptotic safety
{parent=Continuum limit of a quantum field theory}
{wiki}

= Asymptotically safe quantum field theory
{synonym}

An asymptotically safe quantum field theory approaches a non-Gaussian ultraviolet fixed point with finitely many relevant directions. Those relevant coordinates are fixed by finitely many measurements.

= Quantum triviality
{parent=Continuum limit of a quantum field theory}
{wiki}

= Trivial quantum field theory
{synonym}

Quantum triviality means that removal of the cutoff forces the renormalized interaction to vanish, leaving a free continuum theory. Keeping a nonzero interaction then requires treating the model as an effective theory with finite cutoff.

= Renormalization scale
{title2=$\mu$}
{parent=Renormalization}
{wiki}

The renormalization scale is the auxiliary momentum or energy scale at which <renormalization conditions> define the parameters of a quantum field theory.

= Principle of minimal sensitivity
{parent=Renormalization scale}
{title2=$dO_{\mathrm{trunc}}/d\ln\mu^2=0$}

This prescription selects an arbitrary scale or scheme parameter by making a truncated perturbative observable stationary under its variation. The complete physical observable is independent of the <renormalization scale>, but truncation leaves residual dependence. Stationarity is a prescription, not a guarantee of uniquely optimal accuracy or of a stationary point's existence.

= One-loop stationary scale for the hadronic annihilation correction
{parent=Principle of minimal sensitivity}
{title2=$\mu_*^2=q^2e^{-C/b}$}

Suppose $K=1+a+a^2[C-b\ln(q^2/\mu^2)]$, with $da/d\ln\mu^2=-ba^2$. Differentiation gives $dK/d\ln\mu^2=-2ba^3[C-b\ln(q^2/\mu^2)]$. For nonzero $a,b$, the <principle of minimal sensitivity> therefore gives $\boxed{\mu_*^2=q^2e^{-C/b}}$. The derivative vanishes identically through order $a^2$: this prescription retains the residual $a^3$ term generated by one-loop running of the truncated expression. Higher-order running and coefficients can shift the stationary scale. For $b=0$ or $a=0$, no unique scale is selected.

= Running coupling
{parent=Renormalization scale}
{wiki}

A running coupling is a renormalized coupling regarded as a function of the <renormalization scale>. Its scale derivative is its <beta function (physics)>.

= One-loop running of the strong coupling
{parent=Running coupling}
{title2=$\alpha_s(\mu)=4\pi/[\beta_0\ln(\mu^2/\Lambda^2)]$}

With $\beta_0>0$, differentiating $\alpha_s=g^2/(4\pi)$ under $\mu\,dg/d\mu=-\beta_0g^3/(16\pi^2)$ gives $d(1/\alpha_s)/d\ln\mu=\beta_0/(2\pi)$. Integration defines the <strong-coupling scale> $\Lambda$ and gives the displayed positive branch for $\mu>\Lambda$. The pole signals breakdown of <perturbative quantum field theory>. The equivalence between specifying $\alpha_s$ and specifying $\Lambda$ is <dimensional transmutation>.

= Matching the QCD scale across a quark threshold
{parent=One-loop running of the strong coupling}

At a heavy-quark mass $m$, leading-order continuity gives $\beta_- \ln(m/\Lambda_-)=\beta_+\ln(m/\Lambda_+)$. Therefore $\boxed{\Lambda_+=m(\Lambda_-/m)^{\beta_-/\beta_+}}$. This is a change of the <strong-coupling scale> parametrization across a change of active flavour number, not a discontinuity of the matched <QCD coupling>. For four to five active flavours in $SU(3)$, $\beta_-=25/3$ and $\beta_+=23/3$, hence $\Lambda_5=\Lambda_4(m_b/\Lambda_4)^{-2/23}$.

= Landau pole
{c}
{parent=Running coupling}
{wiki}

A Landau pole is a finite renormalization scale at which a perturbatively running coupling diverges. It signals that the perturbative description cannot be continued to that scale without new physics or a nonperturbative completion.

= Beta function
{title2=$\beta(g)=\mu\,dg/d\mu$}
{disambiguate=physics}
{parent=Running coupling}
{wiki}

= Renormalization-group beta function
{synonym}

The beta function of a coupling $g$ is
$$
\beta(g)=\mu\frac{dg}{d\mu}=\frac{dg}{d\log\mu}.
$$
It describes how the <running coupling> changes when the <renormalization scale> changes.

= Asymptotic freedom
{parent=Beta function (physics)}
{wiki}

= Asymptotically free
{synonym}

An asymptotically free theory has a <running coupling> that tends to zero at arbitrarily high energy. Its negative weak-coupling beta function makes short-distance perturbation theory increasingly accurate.

= Strong-coupling scale
{title2=$\Lambda$}
{parent=Asymptotic freedom}

The strong-coupling scale of an asymptotically free gauge theory is the renormalization-group invariant scale at which the one-loop running coupling formally diverges. It replaces a dimensionless ultraviolet coupling through <dimensional transmutation>.

= Dimensional transmutation
{parent=Strong-coupling scale}
{wiki}

Dimensional transmutation replaces a dimensionless coupling by a dimensionful renormalization-group invariant scale. In an asymptotically free theory, specifying the coupling at one scale is equivalent to specifying its <strong-coupling scale>.

= Gauge coupling unification
{parent=Running coupling}
{wiki}

Gauge coupling unification is the convergence of several low-energy gauge couplings to one coupling at a high scale because the gauge factors embed into one simple group.

= SU(5) grand unified theory
{c}
{parent=Gauge coupling unification}

An SU(5) grand unified theory embeds $SU(3)_c\times SU(2)_L\times U(1)_Y$ into $SU(5)$. Canonical normalization of its hypercharge generator predicts $g_s^2=g_w^2=(5/3)g_Y^2$ at the unification scale.

= Wave-function renormalization
{title2=$Z_\phi$}
{parent=Renormalization}
{wiki}

= Wavefunction renormalization
{synonym}

Wave-function renormalization rescales a field so that its kinetic term or propagator residue has the chosen normalization. Its scale dependence gives the field's <anomalous dimension>.

= Canonical field normalization
{parent=Wave-function renormalization}

Canonical field normalization rescales a field so that its kinetic term has unit coefficient. For a <chiral superfield> with $K=Z\Phi^\dagger\Phi$, the change $\Phi_c=Z^{1/2}\Phi$ turns holomorphic <superpotential> parameters into canonical parameters $m_c=m/Z$ and $g_c=g/Z^{3/2}$.

= Charge renormalization
{parent=Renormalization}

Charge renormalization relates a bare gauge coupling to a coupling fixed by a physical normalization condition. In QED, the <Ward identity> equates vertex and fermion wave-function renormalization, leaving photon wave-function renormalization to determine the charge counterterm.

= Pure Yang-Mills one-loop beta function
{parent=Charge renormalization}
{title2=$\beta(g)=-11C_Ag^3/[3(4\pi)^2]+O(g^5)$}

For pure <Yang-Mills theory> with a compact simple <gauge group> and canonically normalized <gauge field>, the one-loop <renormalization-group beta function> is $\mu\,dg/d\mu=-11C_Ag^3/[3(4\pi)^2]+O(g^5)$, where $C_A>0$ is the <Casimir operator> eigenvalue in the <adjoint representation>. This coefficient includes the gauge-field and <Faddeev-Popov ghost field> loops; it is the standard pure-gauge input when adding matter-loop contributions. Its negative sign gives <asymptotic freedom>. Integrating the leading equation gives $g(\mu)^{-2}=g(\mu_0)^{-2}+11C_A\log(\mu/\mu_0)/[24\pi^2]$, while perturbation theory is valid. Combining this term with the <adjoint Dirac contribution to the Yang-Mills beta function> gives the <integer obstruction to one-loop marginality with adjoint Dirac fermions>.

= Background gauge-field renormalization identity
{parent=Charge renormalization}
{title2=$Z_gZ_A^{1/2}=1$}

For a background <gauge field> with $A_0=Z_A^{1/2}A$ and $g_0=\mu^{\epsilon/2}Z_gg$, background gauge covariance preserves the combination $gA$ in the <gauge covariant derivative>. Its <Ward identity> therefore gives the displayed relation. This refers to background-field <renormalization>, not an unrestricted identity between the coupling and the ordinary quantum-gluon wave-function factor. Gauge-invariant one-loop matter determinants can be renormalized with this identity to extract their coupling running from vacuum polarization.

= Adjoint Dirac contribution to the Yang-Mills beta function
{parent=Charge renormalization}
{title2=$\beta_{\mathrm{Dirac,ad}}(g)=4C_Ag^3/[3(4\pi)^2]$}

One adjoint <Dirac spinor> contributes the displayed screening term to the four-dimensional <renormalization-group beta function> in the <modified minimal subtraction scheme>. The fermion contribution to the gluon <self-energy> insertion has pole $-8g^2C_A(k^2\delta_{\mu\nu}-k_\mu k_\nu)/(3\epsilon(4\pi)^2)$. The corresponding background gauge-field <counterterm> is $\delta Z_A=-8g^2C_A/(3\epsilon(4\pi)^2)$; the <background gauge-field renormalization identity> $Z_g=Z_A^{-1/2}$ gives $g_0=\mu^{\epsilon/2}(g+4C_Ag^3/[3\epsilon(4\pi)^2])$. Differentiation at fixed $g_0$ yields the screening coefficient. The mass-independent subtraction includes massive fields until effective-theory threshold matching is performed.

= Integer obstruction to one-loop marginality with adjoint Dirac fermions
{parent=Adjoint Dirac contribution to the Yang-Mills beta function}

For a non-Abelian simple gauge factor with $C_A>0$, pure <Yang-Mills theory> contributes $-11C_Ag^3/[3(4\pi)^2]$. Adding $N_D$ adjoint <Dirac spinors> changes this to $-C_A(11-4N_D)g^3/[3(4\pi)^2]$. Vanishing would require $N_D=11/4$, which is not a nonnegative integer. This excludes cancellation by these fermions alone, apart from the trivial value $g=0$; other matter representations or fields change the coefficient.

= Regularization in quantum field theory
{parent=Renormalization}

Regularization modifies divergent loop integrals by introducing an auxiliary parameter. The regulator is removed after its dependence has been absorbed into <counterterms>.

= Ultraviolet divergence
{parent=Regularization in quantum field theory}
{wiki}

An ultraviolet divergence comes from arbitrarily large loop momentum, equivalently from singular short-distance behavior. Locality makes its regulator dependence cancellable by local counterterms in a renormalizable theory.

= Cutoff regularization
{parent=Regularization in quantum field theory}
{wiki}

Cutoff regularization restricts loop momenta to $|\ell|<\Lambda$. The ultraviolet cutoff $\Lambda$ makes individual integrals finite while displaying power and logarithmic ultraviolet divergences explicitly.

= Dimensional regularization
{parent=Regularization in quantum field theory}
{wiki}

Dimensional regularization analytically continues loop integrals from an integer spacetime dimension to $d=d_0-\epsilon$. Ultraviolet logarithms then appear as poles in $1/\epsilon$.

= Euclidean massive loop integral
{c}
{parent=Dimensional regularization}
{title2=$I_{d,n}(m)$}

For $m^2>0$ and $n>d/2$, <Schwinger parameterization> and a <Gaussian integral> give $I_{d,n}(m)=\int d^dk\,(2\pi)^{-d}(k^2+m^2)^{-n}=\Gamma(n-d/2)(m^2)^{d/2-n}/[(4\pi)^{d/2}\Gamma(n)]$. Its meromorphic continuation supplies <dimensional regularization> poles.

= Minimal subtraction scheme
{title2=$\mathrm{MS}$ scheme}
{parent=Dimensional regularization}
{wiki}

The minimal subtraction scheme chooses <counterterms> that remove only the poles in the dimensional regulator $\epsilon$, without additional finite terms.

= Modified minimal subtraction scheme
{title2=$\overline{\mathrm{MS}}$ scheme}
{parent=Dimensional regularization}

The modified minimal subtraction scheme removes the dimensional pole together with the universal constants conventionally grouped as $-\gamma+\log4\pi$. This simplifies renormalization-group formulas while differing from minimal subtraction only by finite parameter redefinitions.

= Quantum electrodynamics
{title2=$\mathrm{QED}$}
{parent=Perturbative quantum field theory}
{wiki}

Quantum electrodynamics couples a <Dirac field> to an Abelian <gauge field> through $-e\bar\psi\gamma^\mu\psi A_\mu$.

= Minimal electromagnetic coupling of a Dirac field
{parent=Quantum electrodynamics}
{title2=$\mathcal L_{\rm int}=-qA_\mu\bar\Psi\gamma^\mu\Psi$}

Replace the derivative in the <Dirac action> by the <gauge covariant derivative> $D_\mu=\partial_\mu+iqA_\mu$. The interaction is then $-qA_\mu\bar\Psi\gamma^\mu\Psi$. With $\Psi\mapsto e^{-iq\alpha}\Psi$ and $A_\mu\mapsto A_\mu+\partial_\mu\alpha$, the covariant derivative transforms in the same way as the spinor, proving local <gauge invariance>.

= Gauge covariance of the charged Dirac equation
{parent=Minimal electromagnetic coupling of a Dirac field}
{title2=$D'_\mu\Psi'=e^{-iq\alpha}D_\mu\Psi$}

The derivative of the local phase in $\Psi\mapsto e^{-iq\alpha}\Psi$ cancels the added gradient in the electromagnetic potential. Thus $D'_\mu\Psi'=e^{-iq\alpha}D_\mu\Psi$, and the charged <Dirac equation> maps solutions to solutions. The same cancellation makes its action gauge invariant.

= Electron-positron annihilation into a muon pair
{parent=Quantum electrodynamics}
{title2=$e^-e^+\to\mu^-\mu^+$}

At tree level, the <Feynman diagram> has an electron-positron current and a muon-antimuon current joined by one virtual <photon>. In the massless limit, the <spin average> and final-state <spin sum> give $\overline{|\mathcal M|^2}=e^4(1+\cos^2\theta)$, and the integrated <relativistic scattering cross-section> is $4\pi\alpha^2/(3s)$.

= QED Feynman rules
{c}
{parent=Quantum electrodynamics}

The QED Feynman rules combine the <Dirac propagator>, <photon propagator>, and vertex $-ie\gamma^\mu$ with external Dirac spinors and photon polarization vectors. In a covariant gauge they generate perturbative electron-photon scattering amplitudes.

= Bhabha scattering
{c}
{parent=Quantum electrodynamics}
{wiki}

Bhabha scattering is electron-positron elastic scattering $e^-e^+\to e^-e^+$. At tree level it receives an annihilation diagram in the $s$ channel and a photon-exchange diagram in the $t$ channel.

= Pauli term
{c}
{parent=Quantum electrodynamics}
{wiki}

The Pauli term couples a fermion bilinear to electromagnetic field strength through $\bar\psi\sigma^{\mu\nu}F_{\mu\nu}\psi$. In four dimensions it is a dimension-five interaction.

= Photon vacuum polarization
{title2=$\Pi^{\mu\nu}(k)$}
{parent=Quantum electrodynamics}
{wiki=Vacuum_polarization}

Photon vacuum polarization is the one-particle-irreducible photon two-point function generated by charged-particle loops.

= Scalar quantum electrodynamics
{title2=Scalar QED}
{parent=Quantum electrodynamics}
{wiki=Scalar_electrodynamics}

Scalar quantum electrodynamics minimally couples a <complex scalar field> to the electromagnetic <gauge field> through $D_\mu=\partial_\mu+ieA_\mu$.

= Seagull vertex
{parent=Scalar quantum electrodynamics}

The scalar-QED term $e^2A_\mu A^\mu\phi^*\phi$ produces a local two-photon two-scalar vertex, conventionally called the seagull vertex.

= Ward identity
{c}
{parent=Quantum electrodynamics}

= Ward-Takahashi identity
{c}
{synonym}

For an on-shell amplitude $\mathcal M=\epsilon_\mu(k)\mathcal M^\mu$ with an external photon, gauge invariance implies $k_\mu\mathcal M^\mu=0$.

= Scalar quantum electrodynamics Ward identity
{parent=Ward identity}

= Scalar QED Ward identity
{synonym}

= Two-photon scalar Ward identity
{parent=Scalar quantum electrodynamics Ward identity}

= Soft photon theorem
{c}
{parent=Ward identity}
{wiki}

The soft photon theorem factorizes emission of a low-energy photon into the hard amplitude times $\sum_i\eta_iQ_i\,p_i\cdot\epsilon/(p_i\cdot q)$. Its Ward identity is equivalent to conservation of total electric charge.

= Power counting in quantum field theory
{parent=Perturbative quantum field theory}

Power counting assigns mass dimensions to fields and couplings. An interaction is relevant, marginal or irrelevant when its coupling has positive, zero or negative mass dimension, respectively.

= Mass dimension
{title2=$[X]$}
{parent=Power counting in quantum field theory}

In units with $\hbar=c=1$, the mass dimension $[X]$ is the exponent with which a quantity scales under a common rescaling of energies and momenta. In $d$ spacetime dimensions a Lagrangian density has mass dimension $d$.

= Superficial degree of divergence
{title2=$D$}
{parent=Power counting in quantum field theory}
{wiki}

The superficial degree of divergence $D$ is the ultraviolet power predicted by rescaling all independent loop momenta together. For a scalar graph with $L$ loops and $I$ propagators behaving as $p^{-2}$ in $d$ dimensions, $D=dL-2I$ before cancellations and subdivergences are considered.

= Marginal coupling
{parent=Power counting in quantum field theory}
{wiki=Marginal_operator}

A marginal coupling is dimensionless under classical power counting.

= Relevant coupling
{parent=Power counting in quantum field theory}

A relevant coupling has positive mass dimension and grows under scaling toward long distances.

= Irrelevant coupling
{parent=Power counting in quantum field theory}

An irrelevant coupling has negative mass dimension and is suppressed toward long distances.

= Nonrenormalizable interaction
{parent=Irrelevant coupling}

A power-counting nonrenormalizable interaction has a coupling of negative mass dimension, so higher loop orders generally require counterterms of unbounded operator dimension. It is naturally interpreted within an effective field theory below a cutoff.

= Quantum field theory in curved spacetime
{parent=Quantum field theory}
{wiki}

Quantum field theory in curved spacetime quantizes matter fields on a prescribed classical spacetime geometry.

= Hadamard condition
{parent=Quantum field theory in curved spacetime}
{c}

The <Hadamard condition> requires the two-point function of a physical free-field state to have the universal local short-distance singularity prescribed by the spacetime geometry and field equation. It permits state-dependent smooth contributions and enables local renormalization of the <stress-energy tensor>. It does not select a unique <Fock vacuum>.

= Klein-Gordon field
{parent=Quantum field theory in curved spacetime}
{c}
{title2=$\phi$}

A free <Klein-Gordon field> is a scalar obeying $(\Box-m^2-\xi R)\phi=0$ in the mostly-plus <metric signature>. <Global hyperbolicity> provides a well-defined initial-value problem. Quantization additionally needs a state or positive-norm mode splitting; the equation alone does not select particles.

= Complex structure on the Klein-Gordon solution space
{parent=Klein-Gordon field}
{title2=$J^2=-I$}

On the real <Klein-Gordon field> solution space with conserved <symplectic form> $\Omega$, a compatible real-linear map $J$ obeys $J^2=-I$, preserves $\Omega$, and makes $\mu(f,g)=\Omega(f,Jg)$ positive definite. Its $+i$ eigenspace in the complexified space has positive <Klein-Gordon inner product> with the convention $(u,v)_{KG}=i\Omega(\bar u,v)$. It defines the one-particle <Hilbert space> and a free-field <Fock vacuum>.

= Conformally coupled scalar field
{parent=Quantum field theory in curved spacetime}
{wiki}

A conformally coupled scalar field includes the curvature coupling that makes the massless scalar action invariant under local conformal rescaling in the appropriate dimension. In four-dimensional de Sitter spacetime its mode equation is equivalent to that of a flat-space massless scalar after conformal rescaling.

= Klein-Gordon inner product
{c}
{parent=Quantum field theory in curved spacetime}

For complex solutions of the <Klein-Gordon equation>,
$$
(\phi_1,\phi_2)_{KG}
=i\int_\Sigma d\Sigma^a
\left(\phi_1^*\nabla_a\phi_2-\phi_2\nabla_a\phi_1^*\right).
$$
The associated current is conserved, so the inner product is independent of the Cauchy hypersurface when boundary flux vanishes.

= Positive-frequency solution
{parent=Quantum field theory in curved spacetime}

= Positive frequency
{synonym}

Relative to a timelike Killing field $K$, a positive-frequency solution obeys $i\mathcal L_Ku=\omega u$ with $\omega>0$. Such solutions define the one-particle space and annihilation operators of a stationary vacuum.

= Klein-Gordon plane-wave normalization
{c}
{parent=Positive-frequency solution}
{title2=$\psi_{\mathbf p}=e^{-ip^0t+i\mathbf p\cdot\mathbf x}/[(2\pi)^{3/2}\sqrt{2p^0}]$}

For $p^0=\sqrt{\mathbf p^2+m^2}>0$ in <Minkowski spacetime>, these modes obey $i\partial_t\psi_{\mathbf p}=p^0\psi_{\mathbf p}$ and have <Klein-Gordon inner product> $(\psi_{\mathbf p},\psi_{\mathbf q})=\delta^3(\mathbf p-\mathbf q)$. Their conjugates have the opposite norm and are orthogonal to the positive-frequency modes. Plane waves are a generalized, delta-normalized basis; normalizable wave packets have positive norm $\int |a(\mathbf p)|^2d^3p$. Completeness follows by applying the spatial <Fourier transform> to the <Klein-Gordon equation>, which gives the two branches $e^{\mp ip^0t}$ for each momentum.

= Vacuum state in a stationary spacetime
{parent=Positive-frequency solution}

In a strictly stationary spacetime, the timelike Killing flow gives a preferred positive-frequency splitting. The corresponding vacuum is annihilated by every annihilation operator associated with a positive-frequency mode.

= Bosonic Fock space
{parent=Vacuum state in a stationary spacetime}
{wiki=Fock_space}

For one-particle Hilbert space $\mathcal H_1$, the bosonic Fock space is
$$
\mathcal F_s(\mathcal H_1)=\bigoplus_{n=0}^\infty\operatorname{Sym}^n\mathcal H_1.
$$
Creation operators add one symmetrized particle and annihilation operators remove one.

= Vacuum ambiguity in a nonstationary spacetime
{parent=Quantum field theory in curved spacetime}

Without a preferred timelike Killing flow there is no canonical positive-frequency splitting. Different choices are related by Bogoliubov transformations and generally define different particle notions and vacuum states.

= Bogoliubov transformation
{c}
{parent=Vacuum ambiguity in a nonstationary spacetime}
{wiki}

A Bogoliubov transformation is a linear canonical transformation that mixes positive- and negative-frequency modes, and therefore mixes annihilation and creation operators. A nonzero mixing coefficient makes one vacuum contain particles relative to the other mode decomposition.

= Canonical identities for a bosonic Bogoliubov transformation
{parent=Bogoliubov transformation}
{title2=$AA^\dagger-BB^\dagger=I,\quad AB^T=BA^T$}

For mode mixing $\psi'_i=\sum_j(A_{ij}\psi_j+B_{ij}\overline\psi_j)$, the <Klein-Gordon inner product> extracts $a'_i=\sum_j(\overline A_{ij}a_j-\overline B_{ij}a_j^\dagger)$. The <canonical commutation relation> is preserved exactly when $AA^\dagger-BB^\dagger=I$ and $AB^T=BA^T$. These follow by computing $[a'_i,a_k'{}^\dagger]$ and $[a'_i,a'_k]$; they are also the positive- and negative-frequency mode orthonormality identities. In infinitely many modes, convergence and the existence of a common <bosonic Fock space> representation require additional analysis; the expected total particle number is finite only if $B$ is a <Hilbert-Schmidt operator>.

= In-vacuum and out-vacuum
{parent=Bogoliubov transformation}

If a background is stationary in the remote past and future, positive-frequency modes in those two regions define an in-vacuum and an out-vacuum. The intervening time dependence generally makes the two mode bases differ by a <Bogoliubov transformation>.

= Out-vacuum
{parent=In-vacuum and out-vacuum}
{title2=$b_i|0_{\rm out}\rangle=0$}

The state annihilated by every annihilation operator of the positive-frequency mode basis chosen in the asymptotic future. It need not coincide with the <in-vacuum> on a time-dependent spacetime.

= In-vacuum
{parent=In-vacuum and out-vacuum}
{title2=$a_i|0_{\rm in}\rangle=0$}

The state annihilated by every annihilation operator of the positive-frequency mode basis chosen in the asymptotic past. A <Bogoliubov transformation> can make it contain particles when tested by future out-mode <number operators>.

= Particle number from Bogoliubov coefficients
{parent=In-vacuum and out-vacuum}

If
$$
u_i^{\rm out}=\sum_j(\alpha_{ij}u_j^{\rm in}+\beta_{ij}u_j^{{\rm in}*}),
$$
then the expected out-particle number in mode $i$ in the in-vacuum is
$$
\langle0_{\rm in}|N_i^{\rm out}|0_{\rm in}\rangle
=\sum_j|\beta_{ij}|^2.
$$

= Sudden frequency quench
{parent=Bogoliubov transformation}

For an oscillator whose positive frequency jumps from $\omega_-$ to $\omega_+$, continuity of the normalized mode and its first derivative gives the Bogoliubov coefficients
$$
\alpha=\frac{\omega_++\omega_-}{2\sqrt{\omega_+\omega_-}},
\qquad
\beta=\frac{\omega_+-\omega_-}{2\sqrt{\omega_+\omega_-}}.
$$
The initial vacuum therefore contains $|\beta|^2$ final particles in that mode.

= In-in formalism
{parent=Quantum field theory}
{wiki=Schwinger–Keldysh_formalism}

The in-in formalism computes expectation values at a finite time by evolving a state forward and backward along a closed time contour. At first order in the interaction Hamiltonian,
$$
\langle Q(\tau)\rangle
=\langle Q_I(\tau)\rangle
-i\int_{-\infty}^{\tau}d\tau'
\langle[Q_I(\tau),H_I(\tau')]\rangle.
$$
An $i\epsilon$ tilt of the early-time contour projects onto the interacting vacuum.

= In-in bispectrum conjugation rule
{parent=In-in formalism}
{title2=$\langle\zeta(0)\zeta^{\prime}(\tau)\rangle\propto u(0)u^{\prime *}(\tau)$}

With $\zeta_k(\tau)=u_k(\tau)a_k+u_k^*(\tau)a^\dagger_{-k}$, the operator order external field then vertex gives $\langle\zeta_k(0)\zeta_p'(\tau)\rangle=(2\pi)^3\delta(k+p)u_k(0)u_p'{}^*(\tau)$. For positive-frequency $u\propto e^{-ik\tau}$, these vertex contractions contain $e^{+iK\tau}$ and converge on $\tau_i=-\infty(1-i0)$. Conjugating the contraction representation also conjugates the outside in-in prefactor. Mixing the two changes signs and defeats the vacuum prescription.

= Vacuum prescription for inflationary in-in integrals
{parent=In-in formalism}
{title2=$\tau_i=-\infty(1-i\varepsilon),\quad\varepsilon>0$}

The Gaussian <Bunch-Davies vacuum> is selected by tilting the early endpoint to $-\infty(1-i\varepsilon)$ for integrands $e^{iK\tau}$ with $K>0$. They then decay at the early boundary. The complex-conjugate integrand requires the opposite tilt. Ordered contractions and their contour must be conjugated together.

= Schwinger-Keldysh propagator
{c}
{parent=In-in formalism}

The closed time contour of the <in-in formalism> gives four bulk propagators: time-ordered $G_{RR}$, anti-time-ordered $G_{LL}$, and the two Wightman functions $G_{LR}$ and $G_{RL}$. Vertices on the two contour branches carry opposite factors of $i$.

= Cosmological bulk-to-boundary propagator
{parent=Schwinger-Keldysh propagator}

For a free mode function $f(k,\eta)$ and a correlator evaluated at $\eta_0$, the two cosmological bulk-to-boundary propagators are $K_R=f(k,\eta)f^*(k,\eta_0)$ and $K_L=f^*(k,\eta)f(k,\eta_0)$, up to the chosen contour-label convention.

= Cosmological bulk-to-bulk propagator
{parent=Schwinger-Keldysh propagator}

For a free mode $f(k,\eta)$, the right-right bulk propagator is time ordered,
$$
G_{RR}(\eta_1,\eta_2;k)=
\theta(\eta_1-\eta_2)f(\eta_1)f^*(\eta_2)
+\theta(\eta_2-\eta_1)f(\eta_2)f^*(\eta_1),
$$
whereas the mixed propagators are Wightman functions.

= Schwinger-Keldysh conjugation relation
{c}
{parent=Schwinger-Keldysh propagator}

For real couplings and real external data, reflection of every contour label complex conjugates an in-in diagram: $B_{LL}=B_{RR}^*$ and $B_{RL}=B_{LR}^*$.

= Interaction Hamiltonian
{title2=$H_I$}
{parent=In-in formalism}

The interaction Hamiltonian generates interaction-picture time evolution. To leading order for many cubic field interactions it is the negative of the cubic interaction Lagrangian, although derivative interactions require the canonical Legendre transform to justify this relation.

= Bogomolny equations
{c}
{parent=Quantum field theory}
{wiki}

= Bogomolny equation
{c}
{synonym}

Bogomolny equations are first-order field equations obtained by setting every nonnegative square in a <Bogomolny bound> to zero. Their solutions automatically satisfy the corresponding second-order Euler-Lagrange equations.

= Bogomolny bound
{c}
{parent=Bogomolny equations}

A Bogomolny bound rewrites a field-theory energy as a sum of nonnegative squares plus a topological term. Configurations satisfying the resulting first-order <Bogomolny equations> saturate the bound.

= Square completion for a one-dimensional kink
{parent=Bogomolny bound}
{title2=$E\geq|W(\phi_+)-W(\phi_-)|$}

For the static energy $E=\int[\phi'^2+W'(\phi)^2]dx/2$, <completing the square> gives
$$
E=\frac12\int(\phi'\mp W')^2dx\pm[W(\phi_+)-W(\phi_-)].
$$
The <Bogomolny bound> is the absolute boundary difference. Equality requires the <Bogomolny equation> $\phi'=\pm W'(\phi)$ with the sign selected by that difference. Differentiating it gives the <Euler-Lagrange field equation> $\phi''=W'W''$. A stationary solution of that second-order equation need not saturate the bound in a general field theory.

= Classical field-theory soliton
{parent=Quantum field theory}

A classical field-theory soliton is a smooth, localized finite-energy solution that behaves as a persistent object. Stability may follow from a <topological charge> or from a balance among energy terms with different scaling behavior.

= Semiclassical soliton mass
{parent=Classical field-theory soliton}
{title2=$M=M_{\rm cl}+\Delta M_{1\mathrm{loop}}+\cdots$}

A weak-coupling expansion of a <vacuum-subtracted soliton mass> begins with the classical energy and adds the <Gaussian fluctuation approximation>. For <sine-Gordon theory>, $M=8m/\beta^2+\Delta M_{1\mathrm{loop}}+O(m\beta^2)$. The term of order $m$ includes both the vacuum-subtracted fluctuation frequencies and the appropriate <mass counterterm>.

= One-loop soliton mass correction
{parent=Semiclassical soliton mass}
{title2=$\Delta M_{\rm osc}=\tfrac12\sum_n(\Omega_n-\Omega_n^{(0)})$}

In a common finite-volume <regularization in quantum field theory>, the oscillator contribution is $\Delta M_{\rm osc}=\tfrac12\sum_n(\Omega_n-\Omega_n^{(0)})$, including every discrete and continuum-derived mode and treating translation as a <collective coordinate>. Physical frequencies are $\Omega_n=m\omega_n$ when coordinates are measured in units of $m^{-1}$. A <mass counterterm> is added only afterwards. The vacuum subtraction removes the extensive <vacuum energy> but can leave an <ultraviolet divergence>.

= Mode-number regularization of soliton masses
{parent=One-loop soliton mass correction}

Compare the same total number of finite-box modes in the <soliton> and vacuum sectors before removing the <ultraviolet cutoff>. A localized <bound state> replaces a continuum mode, and its contribution must be retained. For the <sine-Gordon kink fluctuation operator> and the odd phase branch $\delta(k)=2\arctan(1/k)$, the bound translation mode replaces the free $k=0$ oscillator. Thus the complete oscillator correction is $-m/2-(m/2)\int dk\,\delta(k)k/[2\pi\sqrt{k^2+1}]$ in this convention. The continuum integral alone misses the finite $-m/2$ contribution.

= Vacuum-subtracted soliton mass
{parent=Classical field-theory soliton}
{title2=$M=E_{Q=1}-E_{Q=0}$}

The rest mass of a stable <soliton> is the lowest energy in its <topological sector> minus the vacuum energy, in the infinite-volume limit. If Euclidean kernels $K_Q(\tau)$ have nonzero overlap with the sector's lowest-energy states, then $M=-\lim_{\tau\to\infty}\tau^{-1}\log[K_1(\tau)/K_0(\tau)]$, followed by the infinite-volume limit. A <Euclidean path integral> represents these kernels, with boundary wavefunctionals included if necessary.

= Soliton time delay
{parent=Classical field-theory soliton}
{title2=$\Delta t=-\Delta x/v$}

If a labeled <soliton> approaches $x=vt+a_-$ before a collision and $x=vt+a_+$ afterwards, its spatial shift is $\Delta x=a_+-a_-$. For $v\ne0$, define its time delay at a fixed spatial point by $\Delta t=-\Delta x/v$. Thus a positive forward shift of a right-moving <soliton> is a negative time delay. For a stationary <soliton>, the spatial shift is well defined but this time-delay definition is not.

= Pairwise additivity of soliton shifts
{parent=Soliton time delay}
{title2=$\Delta x_i=\sum_{j\ne i}\Delta x_{i|j}$}

A many-<soliton> spatial shift is pairwise additive when $\Delta x_i=\sum_{j\ne i}\Delta x_{i|j}$. In the all-<kink> sector of <sine-Gordon theory>, $\Delta x_{i|j}=-\operatorname{sgn}(v_i-v_j)\log\tanh^2[(\theta_i-\theta_j)/2]/\cosh\theta_i$. Dominant spectator exponentials in the <sine-Gordon multisoliton tau representation> multiply the effective exponential of <kink> $i$, so their logarithms add. This is a classical signature of <factorized scattering>, with no independent many-body contribution to the asymptotic shift.

= Finite-energy field configuration
{parent=Classical field-theory soliton}
{title2=$E[\Phi]<\infty$}

A finite-energy field configuration has integrable <energy density> after the vacuum energy has been subtracted. The boundary conditions used for a localized <classical field-theory soliton> require approaching a vacuum at spatial infinity. In one dimension the two ends may approach distinct <scalar-field vacua>; in a gauge theory the asymptotic field can have nontrivial winding even when its local energy density tends to zero.

= Collective coordinate of a soliton
{parent=Classical field-theory soliton}
{title2=$q^a(t)$}

= Collective coordinate
{synonym}

A collective coordinate parametrizes a family of static <classical field-theory solitons>, for example their position or internal orientation. Derivatives with respect to these parameters are <zero modes in field theory> when the family has constant energy. Slowly varying coordinates describe low-energy motion, provided the excluded modes and radiation are negligible; gauge variations must first be removed in a <gauge-theory soliton>.

= Collective-coordinate effective Lagrangian for a soliton
{parent=Collective coordinate of a soliton}

= Collective-coordinate effective Lagrangian
{synonym}

A collective-coordinate approximation substitutes a finite-parameter soliton ansatz into the field action and integrates over space. Its Euler-Lagrange equations approximate the slow motion of the soliton parameters.

= Moduli-space approximation for solitons
{parent=Collective coordinate of a soliton}
{title2=$L_{\rm eff}=\tfrac12g_{ab}(q)\dot q^a\dot q^b-E_0$}

= Moduli-space approximation
{synonym}

Substitute a static soliton family with slowly time-dependent <collective coordinates> into the action. The kinetic energy induces a <Riemannian metric> $g$ on the family, after imposing any <Gauss law constraint in gauge theory> constraint and projecting out gauge directions. If its static energy is constant, the <Euler-Lagrange equations> are the <geodesic> equations of $g$. An approximately flat family instead carries a potential $V(q)$. This approximation neglects excitations of the other field modes and is justified only when their effects are small on the time and energy scales studied.

= Collective-coordinate quantization
{parent=Moduli-space approximation for solitons}
{title2=$\widehat H=-\hbar^2\Delta_g/2+V$}

Quantizing a <moduli-space approximation> gives wavefunctions with measure $\sqrt{\det g}\,d^dq$ and, under the minimal scalar ordering, the <Hamiltonian operator> $-\hbar^2\Delta_g/2+V$, where $\Delta_g$ is the <Laplace-Beltrami operator>. Global identifications, statistics and regularity supply additional restrictions on wavefunctions. Curvature-dependent ordering terms or quantum corrections are extra choices, not determined by the classical kinetic energy alone.

= Topological charge
{title2=$Q$}
{parent=Classical field-theory soliton}
{wiki}

A topological charge is an integer or other discrete invariant determined by the homotopy class or characteristic class of a field configuration. Continuous finite-energy deformations cannot change it without crossing a singular or forbidden configuration.

= Vacuum-boundary degree as a defect charge
{parent=Topological charge}
{title2=$Q=\deg(S^{d-1}\to S^{d-1})$}

A normalized vacuum field on a sphere surrounding a core can define $S^{d-1}\to S^{d-1}$ with integer <topological degree>. Nonzero degree obstructs its extension through the enclosed ball while staying in the <vacuum manifold>. This accounts for the <winding number> of a <vortex> and the spherical degree of a <magnetic monopole>, with the appropriate vacuum targets.

= Degree and energetic stability of a field configuration
{parent=Topological charge}
{title2=$E_2(R)\propto R,\quad E_4(R)\propto R^{-1}$}

A nonzero <topological degree> obstructs smooth unwinding with fixed boundary data, but does not by itself give a stable finite size. In three dimensions a two-derivative energy scales as $R$ and can decrease as a configuration shrinks at fixed degree. The four-derivative <Skyrme term> scales as $R^{-1}$ and can balance this tendency. <Derrick theorem> addresses the energetic issue; a singular limiting configuration can evade smooth <homotopy> conservation.

= Topological current
{parent=Topological charge}
{title2=$\partial_\mu j^\mu_{\rm top}=0$}

A local current whose conservation follows from field geometry rather than requiring the field equation. For <Skyrmions>, pulling back the closed volume form on the target three-sphere gives the current associated with <topological baryon number in the Skyrme model>. Its spatial integral is a <topological charge>, unchanged by smooth evolution with the prescribed boundary behaviour.

= Pullback-volume representation of a topological current
{parent=Topological current}
{title2=$d(\phi^*\omega)=0,\quad Q=\int_{\mathrm{space}}\phi^*\omega$}

A closed target $d$-form $\omega$ pulls back under a spacetime field $\phi$ to a closed form $\alpha=\phi^*\omega$. Its dual defines an identically conserved <topological current>. For a compact oriented spatial $d$-manifold and an equally dimensional oriented target with $\int\omega=1$, the spatial integral is the <topological degree>. Conservation follows without the field equations; changing boundary flux can change the charge.

= Topological sector
{parent=Topological charge}
{title2=$[\Phi]$}

A connected component, or homotopy class, of admissible field configurations with fixed asymptotic conditions. Distinct <topological sectors> cannot be joined by a continuous deformation within that configuration space. A nonzero <topological charge> labels a sector, but a stable finite-size energy minimum also depends on the energy functional.

= Bright soliton of the focusing nonlinear Schrödinger equation
{parent=Classical field-theory soliton}

For negative frequency $E$, the one-dimensional focusing nonlinear Schrödinger equation has the localized stationary profile
$$
f_E(x)=\sqrt{2|E|}\operatorname{sech}(\sqrt{2|E|}x).
$$
Galilean boosts generate moving profiles with a translational collective coordinate and constant velocity.

= Derrick theorem
{c}
{parent=Classical field-theory soliton}
{wiki=Derrick's_theorem}

Derrick's scaling argument tests a static field configuration by rescaling space. A soliton can be stationary only if energy terms with opposite scaling powers balance at the original scale.

= Derrick scaling
{title2=$\phi_\lambda(\mathbf x)=\phi(\lambda\mathbf x)$}
{c}
{parent=Derrick theorem}

Under Derrick scaling, an energy term containing $m$ spatial derivatives and homogeneous of degree $m$ in those derivatives scales as $\lambda^{m-D}$ in $D$ spatial dimensions.

= Derrick virial identity
{c}
{parent=Derrick theorem}

Stationarity of a static solution under <Derrick scaling> requires $dE(\lambda)/d\lambda|_{\lambda=1}=0$. This necessary relation among the separately scaling energy terms is the Derrick virial identity.

= Scalar-field kink
{parent=Classical field-theory soliton}

= Kink
{synonym}

A scalar-field kink is a one-dimensional finite-energy static solution approaching different vacua as $x\to-\infty$ and $x\to+\infty$. Its boundary values define a topological sector.

= Antikink
{parent=Scalar-field kink}
{title2=$Q=-1$}

An <antikink> is a <scalar-field kink> with the opposite orientation of its vacuum boundary values. If $\phi_K(x)$ joins vacua $\phi_-$ to $\phi_+$, then $\phi_K(-x)$ joins $\phi_+$ to $\phi_-$. For an even double-well potential and an odd centered <phi-four kink>, the centered <antikink> is $-\phi_K(x)$. With $Q=[\phi(+\infty)-\phi(-\infty)]/(\phi_+-\phi_-)$, a <kink> has $Q=1$ and an <antikink> has $Q=-1$. Their localized energies coincide by spatial reflection.

= Phi-four kink
{title2=$\phi^4$ kink}
{parent=Scalar-field kink}

For the static energy
$$
E[\phi]=\frac12\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}
\left[\phi'^2+(c^2-\phi^2)^2\right]dx,
$$
the kink joining the vacua $-c$ and $c$ is
$$
\phi(x)=c\tanh(c(x-x_0)).
$$
It saturates the <Bogomolny bound> $E\geq4c^3/3$ by satisfying the first-order equation $\phi'=c^2-\phi^2$.

= Kink–antikink attraction from the stress tensor
{parent=Phi-four kink}
{title2=$F\sim32m^2\kappa^2e^{-2\kappa d}$}

For $V(\phi)=\lambda(m^2-\phi^2)^2$ with $\lambda,m>0$, set $\kappa=\sqrt{2\lambda}m$. A well-separated <kink>–<antikink> pair at $-d/2,d/2$ is approximated by $\phi=m\tanh\kappa(x+d/2)-m\tanh\kappa(x-d/2)-m$. At its midpoint, $\phi_x=0$ and $\phi=m-4m e^{-\kappa d}+O(e^{-2\kappa d})$. The static <stress-energy tensor> component $T^{11}=\phi_x^2/2-V$ is therefore negative there. <Momentum> conservation gives the force on the left half-line as $T^{11}(-\infty)-T^{11}(0)$, which is positive. Its leading magnitude is $F\sim32m^2\kappa^2e^{-2\kappa d}$. Thus the pair attracts. This is a controlled leading-tail estimate at large separation, not an exact superposed solution or a theorem about all subsequent collision outcomes.

= At-rest force for a symmetric phi-four pair
{parent=Kink–antikink attraction from the stress tensor}
{title2=$F(c)=8\tanh^2c(1-\tanh c)^2\sim32e^{-4c}$}

For the initial field $\phi(x)=\tanh(x+c)-\tanh(x-c)-1$ and zero initial time derivative, evaluate the <scalar-field momentum flux> at the midpoint: $\phi_x(0)=0$ and $\phi(0)=2\tanh c-1$. The force on the left <kink> is the displayed positive value, attractive toward the right <antikink>. This exact initial expression has the large-separation asymptotic $32e^{-2d}$ for $d=2c$. If the initial velocity at the cut is $\psi(0)$, subtract $\psi(0)^2/2$.

= Fluctuation operator of a phi-four kink
{parent=Phi-four kink}
{title2=$H_K=-\partial_x^2+4c^2-6c^2\operatorname{sech}^2(cx)$}

Linearizing about the <phi-four kink> gives the displayed one-dimensional <Schrödinger operator> in unit kinetic normalization. Its translational <zero mode in field theory> is proportional to $\operatorname{sech}^2(cx)$. The localized shape eigenfunction $\operatorname{sech}(cx)\tanh(cx)$ has squared frequency $3c^2$, while the continuum starts at $4c^2$. Substitution verifies both bound-state eigenfunctions. The <Pöschl-Teller potential> permits a short completeness argument for the bound modes. In $y=cx$ let $A_\ell=\partial_y+\ell\tanh y$. Then $H_K/c^2=A_2^\dagger A_2$, its partner is $A_2A_2^\dagger=A_1^\dagger A_1+3$, and $A_1A_1^\dagger=-\partial_y^2+1$. The last operator has no bound states. The kernels of $A_1$ and $A_2$ give the two stated modes, while the partner spectra exclude any further normalizable bound modes.

= Translational dynamics of a phi-four kink
{parent=Phi-four kink}
{title2=$L_{\rm eff}=-M+\tfrac12M\dot X^2$}

For $\phi_K(x-X)=c\tanh(c(x-X))$ in the unit kinetic normalization, $\int(\phi_K')^2dx=M=4c^3/3$. Substitution of $X(t)$ therefore gives the free-particle <collective-coordinate effective Lagrangian> $-M+M\dot X^2/2$. Its quantization has <momentum> $p$ and energy $M+p^2/(2M)$ to this order. The exact uniformly moving classical kink is a <Lorentz boost> of the static one, with energy $M/\sqrt{1-v^2}$.

= Kink in a phi-six model
{title2=$\phi^6$ kink}
{parent=Scalar-field kink}

Kinks in a phi-six model commonly arise from a nonnegative degree-six potential with three degenerate vacua. For the unit-vacuum normalization
$$
U(\phi)=\frac12\phi^2(1-\phi^2)^2,
$$
the kink joining $0$ to $1$ is
$$
\phi(x)=\frac1{\sqrt{1+e^{-2(x-x_0)}}}
$$
and has mass $1/4$. A rescaled model with $U(\phi)=\phi^2(\phi^2-4)^2$ has the elementary kink
$$
\phi(x)=\frac2{\sqrt{1+e^{-8\sqrt2(x-x_0)}}},
$$
and its mass is $4\sqrt2$ in the normalization $E=\int[(\phi')^2/2+U]dx$.

= Intermediate-vacuum obstruction to a kink
{parent=Kink in a phi-six model}

If a proposed kink must cross a third degenerate vacuum, its first integral makes both the field derivative and potential vanish there. ODE uniqueness prevents it from crossing at finite distance, so the two elementary kink sectors can be joined only at infinite separation.

= Gauge-theory soliton
{parent=Classical field-theory soliton}

A gauge-theory soliton is a finite-energy localized classical field configuration stabilized by topology or a balance among differently scaling energy terms.

= Bogomolny-Prasad-Sommerfield monopole
{c}
{parent=Gauge-theory soliton}

A Bogomolny-Prasad-Sommerfield monopole is a finite-energy Yang-Mills-Higgs configuration satisfying $B_i=\pm D_i\Phi$. Its magnetic charge is the degree of the normalized Higgs field at spatial infinity.

= Hedgehog ansatz for a monopole
{parent=Bogomolny-Prasad-Sommerfield monopole}

A spherically symmetric SU(2) monopole can be written with the internal Higgs direction aligned with the spatial radial direction and the gauge field built from the invariant tensor $\epsilon_{aij}$.

= Abelian Higgs model
{c}
{parent=Gauge-theory soliton}
{wiki}

The Abelian Higgs model couples a complex scalar field to a $U(1)$ gauge field and permits magnetic vortex solitons in two spatial dimensions.

= Critically coupled Abelian Higgs field equations
{parent=Abelian Higgs model}
{title2=$D_\mu D^\mu\phi+\tfrac12(|\phi|^2-1)\phi=0$}

Use metric $(+--)$, $D_\mu=\partial_\mu-ia_\mu$ and Lagrangian $-f_{\mu\nu}f^{\mu\nu}/4+\overline{D_\mu\phi}D^\mu\phi/2-(1-|\phi|^2)^2/8$. The <Euler-Lagrange field equations> are $D_\mu D^\mu\phi+(|\phi|^2-1)\phi/2=0$ and $\partial_\mu f^{\mu\nu}=\operatorname{Im}(\bar\phi D^\nu\phi)$. Varying $\bar\phi$ gives a kinetic contribution $-D_\mu D^\mu\phi/2$ and potential contribution $(1-|\phi|^2)\phi/4$; variation of $a_\nu$ gives $\partial_\mu f^{\mu\nu}-\operatorname{Im}(\bar\phi D^\nu\phi)$. For static fields in <temporal gauge> $a_0=0$, the positive <Bogomolny vortex equations> imply the scalar equation because $(D_1-iD_2)(D_1+iD_2)=D_1^2+D_2^2+B$. They imply the spatial gauge equations because $\operatorname{Im}(\bar\phi D_1\phi)=-\partial_2|\phi|^2/2$ and $\operatorname{Im}(\bar\phi D_2\phi)=\partial_1|\phi|^2/2$, while $B=(1-|\phi|^2)/2$. The Gauss equation then vanishes identically.

= Abelian Higgs vortex
{c}
{parent=Abelian Higgs model}
{wiki=Nielsen–Olesen_vortex}

= Abelian Higgs vortices
{c}
{synonym}

An Abelian Higgs vortex has quantized magnetic flux and a Higgs field whose phase winds at spatial infinity. Its vortex number is $N=(2\pi)^{-1}\int F$ under the convention $D=d-iA$.

= Vortex number
{title2=$N$}
{parent=Abelian Higgs vortex}

The vortex number is the total multiplicity of the zeros of the Higgs field, equivalently its phase winding at infinity or its magnetic flux divided by $2\pi$ in the standard normalization.

= Abelian Higgs vortex moduli space
{title2=$\mathcal M_N$}
{c}
{parent=Abelian Higgs vortex}

At critical coupling, the Abelian Higgs vortex moduli space is the space of gauge-equivalence classes of static $N$-vortex solutions. Vortex positions provide complex coordinates, and the field-theory kinetic energy induces an $L^2$ Riemannian metric whose geodesics approximate slow vortex motion.

= Relative coordinate for two identical vortices
{parent=Abelian Higgs vortex moduli space}
{title2=$w=(z_1-z_2)^2$}

Interchanging two <Abelian Higgs vortices> replaces $q=z_1-z_2$ by $-q$. Thus $w=q^2$ is a single-valued relative coordinate on the unordered <Abelian Higgs vortex moduli space>. Coincidence is smooth in $w$; a geodesic crossing from positive real $w$ to negative real $w$ changes the line of separation by $\pi/2$, explaining <right-angle scattering of Abelian Higgs vortices>. The asymptotic cone in the $q$ coordinate has angular period $\pi$ and must not be treated as a singularity of the actual coincidence metric.

= Right-angle scattering of Abelian Higgs vortices
{parent=Abelian Higgs vortex moduli space}

A head-on geodesic through the smooth coincidence point of the two-vortex moduli space emerges along the orthogonal axis. Two identical critically coupled vortices therefore scatter through $90^\circ$ in the slow-motion approximation.

= Bogomolny vortex equation
{c}
{parent=Abelian Higgs vortex}

At critical coupling on a surface with conformal metric $g=\Omega dzd\bar z$, the vortex equations are
$$
D_{\bar z}\phi=0,
\qquad
B=\frac\Omega2(1-|\phi|^2).
$$

= Logarithmic form of a Bogomolny vortex
{parent=Bogomolny vortex equation}
{title2=$\mathbf a=\nabla\chi-J\nabla u$}

Away from zeros of the <Higgs field>, write $\phi=e^{u+i\chi}$ with $u=\log|\phi|$. The <Bogomolny vortex equation> $(D_1+iD_2)\phi=0$ gives $a_1=\chi_1+u_2$ and $a_2=\chi_2-u_1$. Consequently $\mathbf a=\nabla\chi-J\nabla u$, where $J(v_1,v_2)=(-v_2,v_1)$, and $B=-\Delta u$ on a regular phase patch. If the contours of $u$ and $\chi$ are orthogonal, then $\nabla u\cdot\nabla\chi=0$, so $\mathbf a\cdot\nabla u=0$: the <gauge potential> is tangent to constant-amplitude contours. This does not imply that it is curl-free. At a vortex zero, $u\to-\infty$ and the phase is only locally defined. Including phase winding distributionally gives $\Delta u+(1-e^{2u})/2=2\pi\sum_jn_j\delta^{(2)}(x-X_j)$. The usual <Taubes equation> uses $2u=\log|\phi|^2$, so its delta coefficients double.

= Bogomolny square completion for an Abelian Higgs vortex
{c}
{parent=Bogomolny vortex equation}
{title2=$E\geq\pi|N|$}

With $D_i=\partial_i-iA_i$ and $B=\partial_1A_2-\partial_2A_1$, use the critical-coupling energy $E=\int[B^2/2+|D_i\phi|^2/2+(1-|\phi|^2)^2/8]d^2x$. For positive <vortex number>, integration by parts gives
$$
E=\int\left[\frac12|(D_1+iD_2)\phi|^2+\frac12\left(B-\frac{1-|\phi|^2}{2}\right)^2\right]d^2x+\frac12\int B\,d^2x.
$$
The boundary divergence vanishes for the usual decaying vortex fields, and <magnetic flux> is $2\pi N$. The two squares vanish exactly at the <Bogomolny vortex equations>. Reversing both signs treats negative $N$. Energy coefficients and the numerical bound change together under other normalizations.

= Taubes equation
{c}
{parent=Bogomolny vortex equation}
{wiki}

For $u=\log|\phi|^2$ and zeros of multiplicities $N_r$ at $z_r$, the Taubes equation is
$$
\Delta u+\Omega(1-e^u)=4\pi\sum_rN_r\delta^{(2)}(z-z_r).
$$

= Vortex composition by conformal rescaling
{parent=Taubes equation}

If $u$ solves the Taubes equation for a metric $g$ and $\widetilde u$ solves it for $e^ug$, then $u+\widetilde u$ solves it for $g$. The vortex divisors add, so the total vortex number is $N+\widetilde N$.

= Bradlow bound
{c}
{parent=Taubes equation}
{wiki}

Integrating the Taubes equation on a compact surface of area $A$ gives $A\geq4\pi N$. A nontrivial vortex with nonzero Higgs field requires the strict inequality $A>4\pi N$.

= Hyperbolic vortex
{parent=Bogomolny vortex equation}

At the integrable curvature scale on the Poincare disc, the vortex Taubes equation reduces to the Liouville equation. Holomorphic self-maps of the disc then generate explicit vortex solutions.

= Witten hyperbolic vortex
{c}
{parent=Hyperbolic vortex}

For the disc metric $8|dz|^2/(1-|z|^2)^2$ and a holomorphic map $g:D\to D$, the Higgs magnitude
$$
|\Phi|=\frac{(1-|z|^2)|g'(z)|}{1-|g(z)|^2}
$$
solves the critically coupled vortex equations. Choosing $g(z)=z^{N+1}$ gives a radial vortex of winding $N$.

= Yang-Mills instanton
{c}
{parent=Gauge-theory soliton}
{wiki=Yang–Mills_equations#Instantons}

A Yang-Mills instanton is a finite-action Euclidean gauge field with self-dual or anti-self-dual curvature. On $\mathbb R^4$, its asymptotic pure gauge defines a map $S^3_\infty\to SU(2)$ whose degree is the instanton number.

= Boundary winding representation of Yang-Mills topological charge
{parent=Yang-Mills instanton}
{title2=$\frac1{8\pi^2}\int\operatorname{tr}(F\wedge F)=\deg g$}

For an anti-Hermitian <SU(2)> connection on $\mathbb R^4$ tending to $g^{-1}dg$ at infinity, the <Chern-Simons 3-form> and <Maurer-Cartan equation> give $(8\pi^2)^{-1}\int\operatorname{tr}(F\wedge F)=-(24\pi^2)^{-1}\int_{S^3}\operatorname{tr}(g^{-1}dg)^3=\deg g$. This is the second-Chern convention with the boundary orientation. Defining instanton charge with the opposite trace sign reverses this integer.

= Instanton moduli space
{parent=Yang-Mills instanton}
{title2=$\mathcal M_k=\{A:*F_A=\pm F_A,\ c_2=k\}/\mathcal G$}

= Yang-Mills instanton moduli space
{c}
{synonym}

For a fixed <principal bundle>, orientation and instanton sign, quotient the <Yang-Mills instanton> solutions by <gauge equivalence of principal connections>. Boundary or framing conditions determine the gauge group $\mathcal G$ on a noncompact base. At an anti-self-dual solution, infinitesimal deformations obey $P_+D_Aa=0$ modulo $a\mapsto a+D_A\varepsilon$. Combining this with $D_A^*a=0$ gives an elliptic deformation operator. This description does not assert that every quotient is a smooth manifold: reducible connections can have nontrivial stabilizers.

= Self-duality implies Yang-Mills equations
{parent=Yang-Mills instanton}
{title2=$D_AF=0,\quad *F=\pm F\ \Longrightarrow\ D_A*F=0$}

Both the <self-dual Yang-Mills equations> and the <anti-self-dual Yang-Mills equations> imply the second-order <Yang-Mills equations> because the <Bianchi identity> holds for every <principal connection>. Finite action is additionally required to call the solution a <Yang-Mills instanton>; the implication itself is a local differential identity.

= Self-dual Yang-Mills equations
{parent=Yang-Mills instanton}
{title2=$F=*F$}

= SDYM equations
{c}
{synonym}

The self-dual <Yang-Mills equations> require a <gauge curvature> two-form to have positive <Hodge star operator> eigenvalue on an oriented Riemannian four-manifold. By the <Bianchi identity>, they imply the full second-order <Yang-Mills equations>. Smooth finite-action solutions saturate the <Yang-Mills instanton Bogomolny bound>. Reversing orientation exchanges this equation with the <anti-self-dual Yang-Mills equations>.

= Self-dual Yang-Mills equations in temporal gauge
{parent=Self-dual Yang-Mills equations}
{title2=$\partial_tA_i=-\frac12\epsilon_{ijk}F_{jk}$}

With orientation $dx^1\wedge dx^2\wedge dx^3\wedge dt$, $\epsilon_{123}=1$, and <temporal gauge> $A_t=0$, the mixed <gauge curvature> is $F_{ti}=\partial_tA_i$. The <self-dual Yang-Mills equations> then have the displayed form. Placing $dt$ first in the orientation reverses the sign, so the metric alone does not specify it.

= Yang-Mills instanton Bogomolny bound
{c}
{parent=Yang-Mills instanton}

For $k=-(8\pi^2)^{-1}\int\operatorname{Tr}(F\wedge F)$ and positive Euclidean action, decomposition into self-dual and anti-self-dual curvature gives
$$
S_{\rm YM}\geq\frac{8\pi^2}{g_{\rm YM}^2}|k|.
$$
Equality holds for a self-dual or anti-self-dual connection.

= Anti-self-dual Yang-Mills equations
{title2=ASDYM equations}
{c}
{parent=Yang-Mills instanton}

The anti-self-dual Yang-Mills equations require the curvature of a connection on an oriented Euclidean four-manifold to satisfy $F=-*F$. In complex coordinates $(w,z)$ they can be written
$$
F_{wz}=F_{\bar w\bar z}=0,
\qquad
F_{w\bar w}+F_{z\bar z}=0.
$$

= Anti-self-dual Yang-Mills equations in temporal gauge
{title2=ASDYM flow}
{c}
{parent=Anti-self-dual Yang-Mills equations}

With orientation $dx^1\wedge dx^2\wedge dx^3\wedge d\tau$ and $A_\tau=0$, one anti-self-duality convention becomes
$$
\partial_\tau A_i=\frac12\epsilon_{ijk}F_{jk}.
$$

= Instanton number
{title2=$k$}
{parent=Yang-Mills instanton}

For an $SU(2)$ connection under a standard trace convention,
$$
k=\frac1{8\pi^2}\int\operatorname{Tr}(F\wedge F)\in\mathbb Z.
$$
Its sign depends on orientation and on whether the instanton is self-dual or anti-self-dual.

= Instanton number as a winding number at infinity
{parent=Instanton number}

For a finite-action $SU(2)$ connection on $\mathbb R^4$ that approaches $A=-dg\,g^{-1}$ at infinity,
$$
k=\frac1{24\pi^2}\int_{S^3_\infty}
\operatorname{Tr}\left[(dg\,g^{-1})^{\wedge3}\right]
$$
under the convention $k=(8\pi^2)^{-1}\int\operatorname{Tr}(F\wedge F)$. This integer is the <degree of a map between oriented manifolds> $g:S^3_\infty\to SU(2)\cong S^3$; orientation and trace conventions may reverse its sign.

= Dimensional reduction of a Yang-Mills instanton to a monopole
{c}
{parent=Yang-Mills instanton}

If a four-dimensional Euclidean connection is independent of one coordinate and that connection component is renamed $\Phi$, the self-dual Yang-Mills equation reduces to the three-dimensional <Bogomolny-Prasad-Sommerfield monopole> equation $B_i=\pm D_i\Phi$.

= Skyrme model
{c}
{parent=Classical field-theory soliton}
{wiki}

The Skyrme model is a nonlinear sigma model for an $SU(2)$-valued field supplemented by a four-derivative term that evades Derrick collapse. Its topological degree is baryon number, and finite-energy solitons are Skyrmions.

= Skyrme term
{c}
{parent=Skyrme model}
{title2=$\mathcal L_4=\frac1{32e^2}\operatorname{tr}([L_\mu,L_\nu][L^\mu,L^\nu])$}

The particular quartic-derivative interaction in the <Skyrme model>, with $L_\mu=U^\dagger\partial_\mu U$. Its static energy is positive and scales inversely with soliton size, opposing collapse under <Derrick scaling>. It is one choice of four-derivative mesonic interaction, not the whole general fourth-order <chiral perturbation theory> Lagrangian.

= Vacuum-preserving symmetry of the Skyrme model
{parent=Skyrme model}
{title2=$L=R$}

The massless derivative <Skyrme model> has <chiral symmetry> $U\mapsto LUR^\dagger$. For the fixed finite-energy boundary condition $U(\infty)=1$, a transformation preserves the same vacuum exactly when $LR^\dagger=1$, or $L=R$. This leaves conjugation $U\mapsto AUA^\dagger$, the <isospin> action. Since $A$ and $-A$ act identically, its effective group is $SO(3)$. Together with spatial translations and rotations it generates the localized zero-mode orbit of a <Skyrmion>. Independent axial rotations change the massless vacuum and are not additional normalizable rigid orientation modes in that fixed-vacuum sector. A usual positive pion-mass term preserves only conjugation even before the boundary condition is imposed.

= Skyrmion
{c}
{parent=Skyrme model}
{wiki}

A Skyrmion is a finite-energy topological soliton of the <Skyrme model>. Its baryon number is the degree of the compactified spatial map $S^3\to SU(2)\simeq S^3$.

= Skyrmion model of a nucleus
{c}
{parent=Skyrmion}
{title2=$B=A$}

A charge-$A$ multi-<Skyrmion> provides an intrinsic field configuration for an <atomic nucleus> with mass number $A$. Its spatial and <isospin> collective coordinates must be quantized, with <Finkelstein-Rubinstein constraints>, before assigning physical nuclear states. A classical intrinsic shape is distinct from rotationally invariant observables in a spin-zero state.

= Skyrmion stabilizer and collective-coordinate orbit
{parent=Skyrmion}
{c}
{title2=$\mathcal O=G/H$}

= Skyrmion collective-coordinate orbit
{synonym}
{c}

For a centered static <Skyrmion> $U_0$, the effective rotation-isorotation group is $G=SO(3)_{\rm space}\times SO(3)_{\rm iso}$. Its <stabilizer subgroup> $H$ consists of pairs obeying $A U_0(R^{-1}x)A^\dagger=U_0(x)$. Thus distinct rigid orientations form the <homogeneous space> $G/H$, by the <orbit-stabilizer theorem>. The unit hedgehog has diagonal $SO(3)$ stabilizer and three orientation coordinates; the toroidal two-Skyrmion has a one-dimensional continuous stabilizer and five; the tetrahedral and cubic cases have discrete stabilizers and six. Adding three translations gives zero-mode orbit dimensions 6, 8, 9, 9. These are symmetry-generated low-energy coordinates, not a claim that non-Bogomolny multi-Skyrmion solutions possess an exact flat moduli space of arbitrary separations.

= Cubic four-Skyrmion
{parent=Skyrmion}
{title2=$B=4$}

The familiar low-energy $B=4$ <Skyrmion> has a cubic baryon-density shell with six face-hole directions and full density symmetry $O_h$, the <symmetry group of a cube>. The proper subgroup is $O\cong S_4$, the <rotational symmetry group of a cube>; its field invariance includes compensating <isorotations>. A useful <rational map approximation for Skyrmions> has $R(z)=(z^4+2i\sqrt3z^2+1)/(z^4-2i\sqrt3z^2+1)$. The relation $R(iz)=1/R(z)$ pairs a spatial quarter-turn with a target half-turn. Its branch directions are $z=0,\infty,\pm1,\pm i$, corresponding to the six coordinate-axis directions. The map gives an approximate field, not an exact analytic energy-minimizing solution.

= Tetrahedral three-Skyrmion
{parent=Skyrmion}
{title2=$B=3$}

The familiar low-energy $B=3$ <Skyrmion> has tetrahedral baryon-density symmetry $T_d$. Proper rotations form the <tetrahedral symmetry> group $T\cong A_4$, acting on the field with compensating <isorotations>. An angular approximation is $R(z)=(i\sqrt3z^2-1)/(z^3-i\sqrt3z)$. Its <Wronskian of a rational map> is proportional to $z^4+2i\sqrt3z^2+1$. The four branch directions are the face-hole directions of a tetrahedral shell. A <rational map> and a variational radial profile approximate the actual field, and baryon-density symmetry must be distinguished from invariance of the <pion> field under an unaccompanied spatial rotation.

= Toroidal two-Skyrmion
{parent=Skyrmion}
{title2=$B=2$}

The standard low-energy $B=2$ <Skyrmion> in the ordinary <Skyrme model> has a toroidal baryon-density shape, with axial density symmetry $D_{\infty h}$. Its proper combined field symmetries have an $O(2)$-type stabilizer. The angular ansatz $R(z)=z^2$ in the <rational map approximation for Skyrmions> explains the axial relation $R(e^{i\theta}z)=e^{2i\theta}R(z)$: spatial rotation through $\theta$ is accompanied by <isorotation> through $2\theta$. The angular density vanishes at the two axial directions, yielding the toroidal hole. This describes the familiar minimal branch and a useful approximate field; it is not an assertion that every degree-two field is toroidal.

= Topological baryon number in the Skyrme model
{parent=Skyrmion}
{title2=$B=-\frac1{24\pi^2}\int\epsilon_{ijk}\operatorname{tr}(L_iL_jL_k)d^3x$}

For a smooth <Skyrme model> field $U:\mathbb R^3\to SU(2)$ with $U(\infty)=1$, compactification gives a map $S^3\to S^3$. With $L_i=U^\dagger\partial_iU$, its <degree of a map between oriented manifolds> is $B=-(24\pi^2)^{-1}\int\epsilon_{ijk}\operatorname{tr}(L_iL_jL_k)d^3x$, with sign chosen so the standard decreasing hedgehog profile has $B=1$. The integrand is the pullback of the normalized volume form on $SU(2)$. Hence it is integer-valued and unchanged by smooth finite-energy <homotopies>. In the <rational map approximation for Skyrmions>, a degree-$N$ angular map and profile $f(0)=\pi,f(\infty)=0$ give $B=-(2N/\pi)\int f'\sin^2f\,dr=N$. This is a topological conservation law, not the <Noether charge> of <isospin>.

= Skyrme baryon number as a mapping degree
{c}
{parent=Topological baryon number in the Skyrme model}
{title2=$B=-\frac1{24\pi^2}\int\operatorname{tr}(U^{-1}dU)^3=\deg U$}

Under <SU(2) as the three-sphere>, a <Skyrme model> field with $U\to I$ at infinity defines $S^3\to S^3$. Choose $T_i=-i\tau_i$ and positive left <volume form> $\theta^1\wedge\theta^2\wedge\theta^3$. The normalized form is $-(24\pi^2)^{-1}\operatorname{tr}(U^{-1}dU)^3$, so the <topological baryon number in the Skyrme model> equals $\deg U$. Generator and orientation conventions fix the sign.

= Skyrme baryon density
{parent=Topological baryon number in the Skyrme model}
{c}
{title2=$B=\int\mathcal B\,d^3x$}

The local <Skyrme baryon density> is $\mathcal B(x)=-\epsilon_{ijk}\operatorname{tr}(L_iL_jL_k)/(24\pi^2)$, with $L_i=U^\dagger\partial_iU$ and $B=\int\mathcal B\,d^3x$. It is invariant under global <isorotations> and transforms as a scalar under proper spatial rotations, so its contours reveal the geometric shape of a <Skyrmion>. For the <rational map approximation for Skyrmions>, $\mathcal B=-f'\sin^2f\,J_R/(2\pi^2r^2)$, where $J_R$ is the <angular Jacobian of a rational map>. Integrating $J_R$ over the sphere gives $4\pi\deg R$, recovering the integer total charge. A decreasing profile and holomorphic angular map give a nonnegative density, but a general field may have regions of negative density; positivity is not a general topological theorem. The density is different from the <energy density>.

= Skyrmion hedgehog ansatz
{c}
{parent=Skyrmion}
{title2=$U_0(\mathbf x)=\cos f(r)+i\sin f(r)\widehat{\mathbf x}\cdot\boldsymbol\sigma$}

The unit <Skyrmion> has a spherically symmetric ansatz with <Pauli matrices> $\boldsymbol\sigma$, $f(0)=\pi$ and $f(\infty)=0$. The <baryon number> is
$$
B=-\frac2\pi\int_0^\infty f'\sin^2f\,dr=1.
$$
The radial profile is obtained from the <Skyrme model> variational equation. Spatial rotations and <isospin> rotations act on the same hedgehog orientation, leaving only three independent rotational <collective coordinates>.

= Rotational quantization of a unit Skyrmion
{parent=Skyrmion hedgehog ansatz}
{title2=$E_j=M+j(j+1)\hbar^2/(2\Lambda)$}

Write $U=A U_0 A^{-1}$, $A\in SU(2)$, and $A^{-1}\dot A=i\boldsymbol\omega\cdot\boldsymbol\sigma/2$. The rotational kinetic energy is $\Lambda|\boldsymbol\omega|^2/2$. Since $A$ and $-A$ give the same classical field, the physical orientation space is $SO(3)$, covered by $SU(2)$. For fermionic unit baryons the <Finkelstein-Rubinstein constraints> require $\Psi(-A)=-\Psi(A)$. The <SU(2) representations> then give half-integer $j$, with <spin angular momentum> and <isospin> both of magnitude $j$. The rigid-rotor energies are $M+j(j+1)\hbar^2/(2\Lambda)$; the lowest multiplets model the <nucleon> and <Delta baryon>. Large rotational energies can excite deformation and radiation, beyond the rigid approximation.

= Rational map approximation for Skyrmions
{parent=Skyrmion}

The rational map approximation writes the angular dependence of a Skyrmion using a rational map $R:S^2\to S^2$ and determines a radial profile variationally. The degree of $R$ equals the baryon number, while zeros of its Wronskian mark directions of vanishing angular baryon density.

= Cubic rational-map ansatz for four Skyrmions
{parent=Rational map approximation for Skyrmions}
{title2=$R(z)=\frac{z^4+2\sqrt3\,iz^2+1}{z^4-2\sqrt3\,iz^2+1}$}

This degree-four <rational map> obeys $R(iz)=1/R(z)$ and $R((iz+1)/(1-iz))=e^{2\pi i/3}R(z)$. These pair the generators of the <rotational symmetry group of a cube> with target rotations, giving combined spatial-isospin symmetry in the <Skyrme model>. Its spatial half-turn kernel is a <Klein four-group>. The map gives a <cubic four-Skyrmion> approximation; preserving only its Wronskian zero set does not guarantee the same rotational symmetry.

= Angular integral in the rational map approximation
{parent=Rational map approximation for Skyrmions}
{title2=$\mathcal I[R]=(4\pi)^{-1}\int_{S^2}J_R^2d\Omega$}

For a degree-$N$ <rational map>, the <angular Jacobian of a rational map> has average $N$, so the <Cauchy-Schwarz inequality> gives $\mathcal I\geq N^2$. In dimensionless <Skyrme model> units the radial energy within the rational-map ansatz is
$$
E=4\pi\int_0^\infty\left[r^2f'^2+2N(f'^2+1)\sin^2f+\mathcal I\frac{\sin^4f}{r^2}\right]dr.
$$
First minimize $\mathcal I$ over admissible maps, then minimize over profiles with $f(0)=\pi$, $f(\infty)=0$. This gives a restricted variational approximation and an upper bound on the unrestricted minimum in that topological sector, not an exact multi-Skyrmion solution. Houghton, Manton and Sutcliffe developed this construction in https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9705151.

= Finkelstein-Rubinstein constraints
{c}
{parent=Skyrmion}
{wiki}

Finkelstein-Rubinstein constraints impose the allowed signs under combined spatial and isospin rotations of a quantized <Skyrmion>. They encode the fermionic or bosonic exchange behavior of topological solitons.

= Collective-rotation constraints for a Skyrmion
{parent=Finkelstein-Rubinstein constraints}
{title2=$\widehat D^J(R)\widehat D^I(A)\Psi=\chi_{\rm FR}\Psi$}

A rigid <Skyrmion> orientation is quantized on a cover of its <Skyrmion collective-coordinate orbit>. A lifted combined rotation in the static field's <stabilizer subgroup> identifies the same classical field and imposes $\widehat D^J(R)\widehat D^I(A)\Psi=\chi_{\rm FR}\Psi$, where the sign is the chosen <Finkelstein-Rubinstein constraints> character of that configuration-space loop. Choosing the nontrivial character models unit baryons as fermions. A $2\pi$ spatial rotation or <isorotation> of charge $B$ then has sign $(-1)^B$: deforming the field to separated unit lumps adds the unit-lump loop classes in the <fundamental group> $\mathbb Z_2$, while the labelled orbital paths can be contracted in three dimensions. The resulting <Finkelstein-Rubinstein constraints> sign is the product of the unit-lump signs. Hence spin and <isospin> are half-integer for odd $B$ and integer for even $B$. Further stabilizer constraints restrict which pairs $(J,I)$ and which body-fixed states occur. The <energy> operator comes from the <inertia tensors> on the orbit; symmetry selects allowed states but does not by itself determine their energies. A bosonic choice of the trivial topological character is mathematically possible but does not model a fermionic <nucleon>.

= Finite-temperature quantum field theory
{parent=Quantum field theory}
{wiki}

Finite-temperature quantum field theory represents thermal expectation values by evolution through an imaginary-time interval of length $\beta=1/T$.

= Retarded Green function
{title2=$G_R$}
{parent=Finite-temperature quantum field theory}
{wiki}

For a bosonic operator $A$, the retarded Green function is $G_R(t)=-i\theta(t)\langle[A(t),A(0)]\rangle$. Causality makes its Fourier transform analytic in the upper half frequency plane.

= Lehmann representation
{c}
{parent=Retarded Green function}
{wiki}

The Lehmann representation expands a thermal correlation function in energy eigenstates. Its poles occur at transition frequencies, and its residues are thermal occupation differences times operator matrix elements.

= Spectral function
{title2=$\rho(\omega)$}
{parent=Retarded Green function}
{wiki}

The spectral function records the weighted energy differences connected by an operator. With the convention $G_R=-i\theta\langle[A,A]\rangle$, it is proportional to $-\operatorname{Im}G_R$.

= Fluctuation-dissipation theorem
{c}
{parent=Spectral function}
{wiki}

The fluctuation-dissipation theorem relates an equilibrium correlation function to the dissipative part of the corresponding linear response. If $\rho=-2\operatorname{Im}G_R$, one common convention is $S(\omega)=\rho(\omega)/(1-e^{-\omega/T})$, which becomes $S(\omega)\simeq T\rho(\omega)/\omega$ in the classical limit $|\omega|\ll T$.

= Spectral representation of a thermal correlation function
{parent=Spectral function}

A Matsubara correlator is obtained from the retarded spectral density by a Cauchy transform evaluated at an imaginary frequency.

= Drude response
{c}
{parent=Retarded Green function}
{wiki}

A Drude response has a single relaxation pole, $G_R(\omega)=\sigma_0/(1-i\omega\tau_0)$. Causality places the pole in the lower half plane when $\tau_0>0$.

= Imaginary-time-ordered correlation function
{parent=Finite-temperature quantum field theory}

An imaginary-time-ordered correlation function orders operators along the Euclidean thermal circle. Bosonic correlators are periodic with period $\beta$.

= Kubo--Martin--Schwinger condition
{title2=KMS condition}
{c}
{parent=Imaginary-time-ordered correlation function}
{wiki}

The Kubo--Martin--Schwinger condition uses cyclicity of the thermal trace to relate correlation functions separated by imaginary time $\beta$.

= Matsubara frequency
{c}
{parent=Imaginary-time-ordered correlation function}
{wiki}

Bosonic Matsubara frequencies are $\omega_n=2\pi nT$. They are the Fourier modes on the periodic imaginary-time circle.

= Bosonic Matsubara sum
{c}
{parent=Matsubara frequency}

= Matsubara sum
{c}
{synonym}

For $E>0$, contour summation over bosonic Matsubara frequencies gives
$$
T\sum_{n\in\mathbb Z}\frac1{\omega_n^2+E^2}
=\frac1{2E}\coth\frac{E}{2T}.
$$
It separates into the zero-point term $1/(2E)$ and a thermal Bose--Einstein occupation term.

= Large-N expansion
{title2=large-$N$ expansion}
{c}
{parent=Quantum field theory}
{wiki}

The large-N expansion organizes an $O(N)$-symmetric theory in powers of $1/N$. At leading order, an auxiliary constraint field is determined by a self-consistent saddle-point or gap equation.

= Gap equation
{parent=Large-N expansion}
{wiki}

A gap equation determines a physical mass or excitation gap self-consistently from an interaction and a regulated fluctuation integral.

= Critical coupling
{title2=$g_c$}
{parent=Gap equation}

A critical coupling is a coupling value at which a gap equation first admits a massless solution. Tuning through it can separate symmetric gapped and symmetry-broken phases.

= Upper-critical-dimension logarithmic correction
{parent=Gap equation}

At an upper critical dimension, a nominal power-law critical scaling acquires a multiplicative logarithmic correction because the coupling is marginal.

= Path integral
{parent=Quantum field theory}
{wiki}

A path integral represents a quantum amplitude or correlation function as an integral over field histories weighted by the exponential of the action.

= Scalar field path integral
{parent=Path integral}
{title2=$Z[J]=\int\mathcal D\phi\,e^{i(S[\phi]+\int J\phi)}$}

= Scalar field functional integral
{synonym}

The <scalar field path integral> generalizes the <configuration-space path integral> by replacing finitely many coordinates with field values. Its regulated measure integrates one field variable at every spacetime lattice point. Fixed boundary fields give transition kernels; vacuum boundary conditions and sources give the <normalized vacuum generating functional>.

= Path-integral perturbation by source derivatives
{parent=Scalar field path integral}
{title2=$Z[J]\propto e^{iS_{\rm int}[(1/i)\delta/\delta J]}Z_0[J]$}

An interaction depending on the field can be represented by substituting $(1/i)\delta/\delta J$ into its action and acting on the free <generating functional>. Expanding that exponential produces <Feynman diagrams>, contractions and vertex weights. Normalize by the resulting value at $J=0$ to cancel vacuum diagrams.

= Vacuum projection by imaginary time
{parent=Scalar field path integral}
{title2=$e^{-TH}|n\rangle=e^{-TE_n}|n\rangle$}

Long imaginary-time evolution multiplies an <energy eigenstate> by $e^{-TE_n}$. After normalization it projects onto the lowest energy component with nonzero initial overlap, or onto the lowest-energy subspace if the vacuum is degenerate. This provides vacuum boundary conditions for a <path integral> and is related to the <Feynman i-epsilon prescription>.

= Configuration-space path integral
{parent=Path integral}
{title2=$K(q_f,q_i)=\int_{q_i}^{q_f}\mathcal Dq\,e^{iS[q]}$}

A <configuration-space path integral> integrates histories of the coordinates with weight $e^{iS}$. Fixed endpoint conditions specify a transition amplitude. For a quadratic kinetic energy it follows by Gaussian momentum integration from the <phase-space path integral>. Time-slicing fixes the normalization, and a regulator gives meaning to the otherwise formal <functional measure>.

= Phase-space path integral
{parent=Path integral}
{title2=$K=\int\mathcal Dq\,\mathcal Dp\,e^{i\int(p\dot q-H)dt}$}

A <phase-space path integral> represents a quantum transition kernel by integrating both coordinates and conjugate momenta with action $\int(p\dot q-H)dt$. It follows from inserting position and momentum completeness relations between short-time evolution operators. For a quadratic momentum dependence the momentum variables can be integrated by a <Gaussian integral>.

= Gaussian momentum integration in a phase-space path integral
{c}
{parent=Phase-space path integral}
{title2=$\int dp\,e^{-\Delta t p^2/(2m)+ip\Delta q}=\sqrt{2\pi m/\Delta t}\,e^{-m\Delta q^2/(2\Delta t)}$}

Integrating each momentum in a quadratic <Hamiltonian> yields the configuration-space kinetic action and its time-slice normalization. A raw $dp$ measure gives $\sqrt{2\pi m/\Delta t}$; the normalized Fourier measure $dp/(2\pi)$ gives $\sqrt{m/(2\pi\Delta t)}$. Dropping the time dependence changes the <quantum-mechanical propagator>.

= Time slicing of a phase-space path integral
{parent=Phase-space path integral}
{title2=$\dot q_i=(q_{i+1}-q_i)/\Delta t_i$}

The first-order term means $\sum_i p_i(q_{i+1}-q_i)$ in a finite-dimensional integral. Specify the endpoints and an ordering prescription, then take the maximum time step to zero. Typical Euclidean paths are not classically differentiable, so the discrete prescription is essential.

= Euclidean path integral
{c}
{parent=Path integral}
{title2=$Z=\int\mathcal D\phi\,e^{-S_E[\phi]}$}

A <path integral> with weight $e^{-S_E}$ uses the <Euclidean action> obtained by <Wick rotation>. Bosonic convergence requires appropriate positivity and boundary conditions.

= Operator-path-integral equivalence
{parent=Path integral}

With a time-slice regulator, inserting field-coordinate resolutions of identity in evolution kernels produces a bosonic phase-space <path integral>; integrating the <canonical momentum> gives the configuration-space <action>. For <fermions>, resolutions in <fermionic coherent states> instead produce a <Berezin integral> and the first-order kinetic term. Vacuum endpoint projection supplies the <Feynman i-epsilon prescription>. Both constructions reproduce the same free oscillator correlators and their <Wick theorem> expansions, and thus agree order by order in a regulated <perturbation series>.

= Euclidean worldline path integral
{c}
{parent=Path integral}

A thermal <path integral> represents the <canonical partition function> by paths periodic in Euclidean time of circumference $\beta$. For $H=p^2/2+V(x)$, its formal action is $S_E[x]=\int_0^\beta(\dot x^2/2+V(x))\,d\tau$. The <periodic boundary condition> results from taking the trace of the evolution kernel. The continuum measure requires a regularization and normalization; finite-dimensional measures make both choices explicit.

= Fourier-cutoff oscillator functional determinant
{c}
{parent=Euclidean worldline path integral}
{title2=$Z_N=A_N\omega^{-1}\prod_{r=1}^N(\omega^2+\nu_r^2)^{-1}$}

Use an orthonormal real <Fourier series> basis on a circle of length $\beta$, with $\nu_r=2\pi r/\beta$. Retain the constant coefficient and the sine/cosine pair for each $1\le r\le N$. The oscillator action becomes $\tfrac12\omega^2q_0^2+\tfrac12\sum_r(\omega^2+\nu_r^2)(q_r^2+s_r^2)$. A product of real <Gaussian integrals> gives the displayed <functional determinant>, with $A_N$ independent of $\omega$. Ratios for two frequencies converge regardless of $A_N$. The absolute limit requires its normalization: $A_N=\beta^{-1}\prod_r\nu_r^2$ gives the <thermal partition function of a quantum harmonic oscillator> in the limit. Multiplying this choice by an oscillating positive sequence preserves all frequency ratios but destroys the absolute limit.

= Functional measure
{parent=Path integral}

= Functional integral
{synonym}

The functional measure is the formal integration measure over field configurations in a <path integral>. Its transformation under a symmetry can preserve the corresponding Ward identity or produce a <quantum anomaly>.
